      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>St. James's Palace.—The Schloss in which Lhe King and Queen are to be lodged during their stay in Berlin, corresponds in status to the St. James's Palace in London. St. James's is the official palace and locale of the Court, although never used by the King and i Queen as a residence. It was built by Henry and from the time Whitehall Palace was destroyed by are, in the reign of William 111., until the occupation of Buckingham Palace by George IV., St. James's was the only residential London palace of our Sovereigns. It was comparatively easy in those times to obtain a -close view of royalty, and, according to a guide. to London published in 1767, by knocking at the side door of St. James's Chapel Royal, and paying the vergers one shilling, people were permitted to stand at the back during Divine Service, in presence of their Majesties. Royal Needlewomen.—The Queen, and Princess Victoria, are adepts in the art of embroidery, and beautiful pieces of work, completed by Her Majesty and the Princess, are from time to time bestowed upon those honoured with their friendship. The Princess of Wales leans more to the strictly useful varieties of -work, such as knitted jerseys, shawls, petticoats,scaryts ac., which Her Royal Highness sends to the poor, or to bazaars, or sales of work. The late Queen Victoria's daughters have all preferred fine, plain stitchery, Princess Henry of Battenberg being a wonderful needle- woman. The Queen of Spain follows closely in her mother's footsteps and a considerable number of the lexquisite little garments worn by her small sons, have been fashioned by Queen Victoria Eugenie. Difficult Times for the Amateur.—To the home dress- maker modes are trying just now. Especially is this so in the case of the pinafore-princess gown, beneath which is worn the finely-tucked semi-transparent chemisette and sleeves, It needs an expert hand to build such a gown successfully, and the amateur is doubtless longing for the return of the waistbelt, which most certainly covers a multitude of faults. But so far there seems little hope of such a revival, the princess, corselet, and pinafore-princess dresses, apparently having come to stay for some time yet. Ridiculous Fashions.—The story of the woman who bad to be lifted into a motor car, because her skirt was too tight to permit of lier taking the step, savours more of fiction than fact, though it must certainly be admitted that steps do appear trying, in a sheath gown. Then there is the difficulty in getting up and sitting down the constant tripping over the superfluous saaterial resting upon the ground; and the ungainly shale that of necessity takes the place of walking, all of which points to an unhappy life in the sheath gown. The more unhappy however the better, if such a state of affairs leads to the disappearance of this ridiculous and ungraceful mode. Welcome Simplicity.—The fashion of fixing upon the low-cut evening bodice, one huge flower, close to the left shoulder, grows apace, the cluster of several small blooms, tucked into the centre of the bodice, being for the time set aside. Flaming colours too, are preferred, eapecially when all-white or all-black toslettes are con• corned. Occasionally ono sees a bloom to correspond in the hair, but the tendency at the moment is to leave the hair—as regards ornamentation—severely alone. And after the wild and extraordinary effects we have long been forced to gaze upon, this simplicity is restful. Spring Millinery.—Some of the fur toques that soon now—and fortunately—will have to be laid aside, make decidedly for the ludicrous, crushed down as they are open the head, until scarcely any hair at all is visible.l The idea is certainly a convenient one for the woman ; aho has little hair, either acquired or her own,'but that is really all that can be said in its favour. The first of the coming season's headAear, upon which is heaped I quantities of gay spring flowers, form a delightful con- trast, and the artistic will rejoice at the very decided revival of the always becoming bell-shaped hat. Customs that Never Change.—Egyptologists tell us that the women of six or seven thousand years ago, worse false hair, false teeth, used scents and cosmetics, and kept little pet dogs just the same as the women of the present day. Egyptian ladies of quality also kept pet monkeys, a fashion which some society women of the twentiethcentury followed, thinking it was some- thing new. The most ancient of ancient histories is I that of the Egypti•tnii, going back to 5,006 years ' but there exist in the tombs and buried cities, relics, carvings and drawings, shewing that ali these habits and , customs were common before the dawn of history.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, January 30, 1909,</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, January 30, 1909,</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASIIION-EIOIIE.</p>
        <p>OPALS AND AMETHYSTS.</p>
        <p>The ill luck which is supposed to follow the wearing of opals does not seem to bother the up-to-date damsel if she has a liking for these poetic stones. And opals are just now high in favour with artistic jewel workers. If bigel lisht...:l Opals are used they are set. in diillf beaten silver. • The wonderful Mexican fie opals, shew to best advantage in red gold. 4 A peculiar lighting effect is obtained•by backing a fire opal with a sapphire, the costly stone hidden beneath the comparatively inexpen- siv'e one. The result is a queer mingling of flame colour and blue not obtained, in any other way. It is like the flame of burning driftwood. Since Queen Alexandra created an interest in the inexpensive amethyst, many stones of comparatively little value have been high in favour. A BECOMING EVENING HOOD.</p>
        <p>One' of the daintiest and meat attractive features of the present red6on'B evening attire is the big, soft, evening hood, or capuchon, as our French neighbours call it. These hoods are made in an infinite variety of style and meterial, and are wonderfully becoming, as well as most warm and comfortable coming out of a theatre or heated room on a cold wi:ster eve:ling. Our sketch pictures a par- ticul6rly attractive example of the evening</p>
        <p>_ . necklace and cheap jewellery, is calculated- to bring discredit on the whole class and urge upon young people of this kind, the necessity of a proper sense of press for office work. A tailor-made coat and skirt I —the lattor well off the ground—and plain washable hood. It is dainty, becoming, and graceful, blouse, with collar and tie, good boots and a very and yet is neither fragile nor very costly. The simply trimmed hat, is the style of dress the Associa- hood itself, which is shaped something like tion regard as appropriate, and it would be to the en enormous Red Riding Hcod bonnet, is interests of those girls who affect over-elaboration if they were to follow this advice. i carried out in soft woollen-backed satin of a I delightful tone of pale Naftier. blue. The IVould Have Nothirg to do with Man.—The Suffra- , gatherings near the front are drawn over an gists often manifest their contempt for man-made laws, immensely thick piping - cord, which adds but there • has just died at picknev an "elderly spinster" who had such a dislike 'for everything greatly to the effect of the hood. On to the associated with man that she would have nothing to do outer edge of this thick piping'; cord is with money which bad the King's head on it. She could gathered a frill of very soft embroidered only tolerate Victorian coinage, to which she bad been ivory not. This frill frames the face in 'nest accustomed all her life, and if given a King's head piece delightful fashion, and is finished with long she would fling it in the fire. She would only deal at net strings which are tied in a pretty bow shops where she could be served by a woman, and would beneath the chin. post ber letters without a stamp, or put it upside down, . and according to her landlady, she would sometimes SCIENCE AND LovE. refuse to take in letters because the stamps bore a man's Someone has affirmed that when science head. Some women go to prison for the feminisecause, mates with science domestic