      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>DUTCH TEAT MOULD.—Cover two shins of veal with cold water, bring to the boiling point, skim well, add a rounding teaspoonful of salt, a saltspoonful of paprika, two .cloves; one bay leaf, and bit of lemon peel, and simmer until the meat is tender. Remove it, strain the liquor, add the strained juice of a lemon and return to the stove to cook down to one pint. With two forks shred the meat finA Wet a mould in cold water. Chill the liquor until slightly thickened, add the meat, and stand aside , until firm. Unmould and serve as a luncheon or supper relish aCCOP3' panied by bread and butter points,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>YOUNG BRIDE'S MISERY. A Fortunate Monti-very Saved Her.</head>
        <p>A YOUNG BRIDE'S MISERY. A Fortunate Monti-very Saved Her.</p>
        <p>Just over a year ago, Mrs. Alice Potter, of 8, Paradise Row, Edenbridge' Kent, entered her present borne, a bride of twenty. From the first day of her new life, to use her own words "It seemed .as if I would never be able toile my housework, as every little thing I did exhausted me. 'Every effort brought on headache, and.while I was in the house by myself I jumped at every sound; and as I went about alone my heart fluttered until I was ready to faint." _ Yet Mrs Potter is to-day a bright, hearty, merry little wife, not a sign of nervousness about her. The secret of this remarkable change she divulged to a press representative. "A couple of months after we were married I was the most wretched woman one could imagine," said Mrs. Potter. "Frightful dreams woke me up with a terrible headache and palpitation, and I lay awalie in terror. I became weaker every day and my colour disappeared altegetlier, my face setting into chronic- pall°. "My food gave me terrible pains from indigestion, pains which went all over my body and seemed to rise into my throat and stifle me. I had been anietnic as a young girl. This as caused through my being constipated for some time. "At last I was lucky enough to get a thoroughly good night's rest. The next night my bad dreams ceased, then, a few days later, 1 was able to eat Plenty cf good solid food. The nervousness gave place to confidence, and these terrible heart flutter- ings left off altogether. The fact was I had made a discovery of the greatest value to all work or ailirig people, namely, that the one great medicine for curing indigestion and liver ailments is Chas. Forde's bile beans. The xesult of taking a course of that wonderful medicine was that I became healthy, rosy, and strong, and my housework became a pleasure to me. Indeed, for the last ten months I have enjoyed better health than ever in my life, and my husband and I are as happy a'young couple as anyone could wish."</p>
        <p>Every mother, young and old, should know that in Chas. Forde's bile beans, which cured Mrs. Potter, she will find not only a family medicine which renders her own life happier, but by keeping her children in g( od health, ensures safety against loss of money in doctor's bills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica. IMPROVING NEGATIVES.</p>
        <p>However careful we are in estimating our exposure and development, it frequently happens that the result- ing negative is not all that we have desired. The con- trasts may be too severe, and to give the best print, surne alteration has to be made on the negative. There are various methods by which this may be obtained, chemical and mechanical. I would here explain one of the latter methods. There is a paper sold by photo- graphic dealers, called papier rninerale, which is the best for the purpose that I am going to talk about, but you cannot obtain this, very useful effects can be obtained with ordinary tissue paper. Cut the paper to the same size as your negative, and then with the paper slightly damped fix the paper thereto. By this means, the paper on drying will be absolutely flat. For the parts of your negative which are dense, and that you do not wish to make denser, you make the paper translu- cent by the use of a little oil, which which may be rubbed on with the finger, so as to make the dense parts as transparent as possible. By so doing, ypu have in a small degree lessened the amount of con- Oast, but this may be carried farther still by covering he thin parts of the negative with a little stumping balk. If you have no stumping chalk available, ordinary lead pencil may be used, with which you lightly cover those parts that you do not wish to print too darkly. This method of treatment by paper may be N ery much enlaged when occasion necessitates by extra thicknesses, and instead of using oil over the dense parts, cot out these parts with a knife. This is especially useful in photographs of interiors of churches. so as to get detail in the windows. Another method for similar treatment is the use of ground glass or matt varnish. Matt varnish may bo obtained ulreody pre- pared, but if ypu wish to experiment yourself, the com- position of it is as follows :—Gum mastic, 20 grains, gum sandarac, 90 grains, and ether, 2ozs., benzole, loz. This will give you a nice line grain. If you require a coarser grain, increase the quantity of benzole. A word of caution with reference to the handling of ether. This must be done in daylight, as ether is most inflammable. The glass side of the negative is coated with this matt varnish, and is put on with the negative cold. It very quickly sets, and when hard, the parts which you do not wish obscured, cut away with a knife. ROUGH WEATHER SEASCAPES.</p>
        <p>For those who are anxious to secure rough weather sea pictures, this the time of the year when what are known as the spring tides prevail, and some very fine rough sea effects will be obtained at many of the sea- side towns, of the waves dashing over the sea-wall. Of course, you have to pick and choose those places where the sea washes right over the promenade. Because there is a gale blowing, it does not always means that you will find an opportunity for rough sea photographs at every place, as it varies according to the aspect of the town you select. Some towns are affected by •a south-westerly gale, whereas at others a northerly gale is the one which produces the rough sea. If you select a town where, say, the south west wind produces the roughest sea, you may depend upon it that if there is a gale blowing from that direction, and it is high water near twelve o'clock, and it is also within a day or two of the full moon, you will have an opportunity of securing some very rough seas. For this work, one very necessary precaution is the care of the camera, as only those who have done much of this work know the penetrating power of the salt spray, which unless well looked after will ruin the mechanism of many a shutter. A plain glass cap for covering the lens if you are using a stand camera and focussing is very useful, as other- wise TOU will have to be bontinually wiping the spray off the front of your lens, whereas if you have a glass cap, and you are waiting for a coming wave, you can remove it just before you are going to make your exposure, thereby keeping your lens free. A very good method for steadying the camera when there is a high wind blowing is to attach a cord to the tripod screw, reaching down to the ground, with a loop that you can put your foot into. By this means you are able to keep the camera much steadier than you otherwise could do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>DRESDEN INtERNATIONAL PHOTOGRAPHIC EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>THE DRESDEN INtERNATIONAL PHOTOGRAPHIC EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>From all accounts, the Dresden Exhibition promises to be a very important function, and so any of those who contemplate getting at all in that direction during the holidays this year, should, if possible, make a point of including Dresden in their programme. It apeears to have received a very large amount of international suppors. From England, I hear that there are 400 entries in the scientific, 180 in the pictorial, 200 in the instruction, and 5 in the trade section. I also hear that our friends in America are concentrating their efforts to show us their best. pictorial work. In fact, I understand that it will be an exhibit that is likely to excel anything that has been got together in their own country. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LYCEUM CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE LYCEUM CLUB.</p>
        <p>An interesting, if somewhat small exhibition, has just been held at the Lyceum Club, which is the recognised club of professional women, of work by their members. I noticed the work of several ladies who have already made a mark in the photographic world, such as Mrs. Caleb Keene and Miss 'Warburg, and also the work of Miss Tomlinson, who is well-known by the work that she has &amp;no in illustrating for publishers.</p>
        <p>Local Oddfellowshlp.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the "Haven of -Rest " Lodge was held at tho Pelican Hotel, Buttermarket-street, on Monday night. After the Secretary (P.G. Henry Fogg) had read the balance sheet, uhich showed that the lodge had had a fairly successful yebr, tbe follow- ing were elected the lodge officers : P.G. Thomas Critchley, as N.G., P. 0., James Ince as V. 0., P.G. W. H. Thompson Lodge G.M. and Bro. A. Evans as M.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] – THE MYSTERIOUS DRUMMER.</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] - THE MYSTERIOUS DRUMMER.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN K. LEYS, Author of "Mr. Miniver' s Marriage," "The Spy's Confession," "A Maiden's Victory," "In the Grey of the Dawning," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"Why do they call a commercial traveller a drummer?" I asked my friend Beaumont, the hotel manager.</p>
        <p>"Don't know, I'm sure. It's an American word. I suppose because a ' commercial 'is al- ways beating the drum to sound the praises of his wares," he added after a pause. "You have a goqd many of them about, haven't you?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, in the main we are a commercial house."</p>
        <p>"You are always pretty full, I notice." "Yes, but there's not much profit in it. You see, commercials insist upon having special terms since they live principally in hotels. And they insist on having good food and plenty of it. They know how to make themselves com- fortable." There was a pause, during which I continued to suck at my cigar in lazy content, while the manager stared contemplatively at the church tower which reared its hoary head above the roaring traffic of the street. "It seems to me," I said, "that most drum- mers are young men nowadays." "That is so, sir. The elderly men ,who would boast that they had worked the same district for the same firm for twenty or thirty years are comparatively seldom met with. Younger men, with energy enough to tackle strange shops with new articles, aro gradually pushing them out." "There is one over forty, anyhow," I said, as my eyes rested on a slim, upright figure dressed in quiet grey, that was just entering the door of the hotel. "But I'm wrong," I added. "I don't think he is a commercial traveller at_all."</p>
        <p>"He says he is, at all events," said *my friend the manager. as the new-corner steered a straight course for his den. "We call him the mysterious drummer."</p>
        <p>"Good evening, Mr. Beaumont," said the stranger, in a queer, high-pitched voice. "Can you let me have a comfortable room to-night?" "All my rooms are comfortable, Mr. Spen- low," said the manager, gravely, with a sly wink at me.</p>
        <p>" Ah, just so—just so. Yes; no doubt. But I am tired to-night, and I want a comfortable room with a nice easy chair, where I may sit • and read for a bit before I go to bed, if I like." No. 79 is vacant. The one you had the last time y,-)u were here." • "Don't like it," snapped Mr. Spenlow. "Couldn't sleep half the night for the noise of shuntinir trucks on the railway." , "Well, there's No. 45," said the manager, glancing at the pages of a big book over his clerk's shoulder. "That is—l didn't care for it, I remember." "Don't. think you've ever had No. 45, sir," put in the clerk.</p>
        <p>"Yes, I have; and, as I said, I didn't like it. Can't you give me another?" 'We are pretty full just now," said the manager, "but I think I might give you No. 65. It is on the second bedroom floor—a very com- fortable room."</p>
        <p>Here the clerk said something in a whisper to the manager, who said aloud "Oh, nonsense! I don't believe Mr. Scott means to come back. I believe he has forgotten all about it, and would grumble for six months if I charged him for the night. Come upstairs, Mr. Spenlow, and have a look at the room. I am going up now, and will shew it you myself."</p>
        <p>They crossed the hall and w -nt up by the lift. I accompanying ,them. But when they reached the second bedroom floor, and turned into a long corridor, the manager suddenly threw, open a door.</p>
        <p>1-Tore's a room, now; a better room than No. 65." said he. " which is vacant. Better have this one. What Carruthers said about No. 65 is quite true. It has not been formally surrendered. The R•entleman who had it last may return and riaim it, though I don't think it's likely."</p>
        <p>"I'll 'risk that." said Mr. Spenlow. "I don't like this room. It's- too dark.'</p>
        <p>We passed on to • No. 65, and the mana.g.er opened the door with his master-key. I "I like this room! I'll take it!" e:aeulated ; the visitor. _ -</p>
        <p>..._...____ " All right, sir. But you understand that, strictly speaking. I have no ri9-ht to let it to you, and if the gentleman who harl it last claims it, you will have to give it up to him." "Oh, all right—all right. Just send up my lu7,g-age when you go down, will you?" "I wish you would let me show you No. 63," said the manager after a pause. "You see, you may be disturbed here— - "Oh, all right!" cried the visitor, im- patiently. almost elbowing us out of the room. "That's a queer fish," said the manager to me. as we went back to the hall by the stairs. "He says he is a commercial traveller, . but. he seems- to. have no customets, and no legitimate business, lie turns up here constantly, and .is always very particular about his bedroom. It is almost imrossible to get him to occupy the same room twice—always some objection to it. You saw how pleased he was with No. 65. Yet I Would bet you a box of cigars to a toothpick that when he comes here next time he won't : look at it."</p>
        <p>"That is rather curious." "Isn't it? I can't believe he is a 'com- mercial.' lie has not the ways of one, and I never knew of his doing any business. I can't make out what he wants to come here for et all." Now these words seemed to set a light, as it were, to a smouldering heap of suspicions in my</p>
        <p>mind. • - I had a vague idea ihat I had met the gentle- man before, and that the tone, as it were, of the associations which he might have recalled to my mind was not a pleasant one. The circum- stances under which I had seen him were un- favoura.ble, or depressing, or in some way dis- creditable. Scarcely had the boots brought up the man's portmanteau, than I heard from the next room (which I had taken for myself) a tremendous altercation going on. , The row was something awful. Soon the manager turned up, and I tDok- the liberty of looking in to see what was wrong. When I went into the room Mr. Spenlow was seated in a big. comfortable-looking easy-chair, covered with striped tapestry; and Mr. Scott stood over him brandishing his fists. They were going on like this: New comer: "Come out of that!" Original: "I shell not." "Come out of that chair. or I'll throw you out of the window!" yelled Scott. "Do! I should like to see you try it! Thi3 is my room, I tell you!"</p>
        <p>- "It is not." The manager put me in possession." "He did not—legally. He had no power to do so, for the room is mine." Mr. Scott grew purple in the face, and was nil but dancing with rage. Snenlow was very white about the gills, but he folded his qprms across his breast in a very determined manner. Neither would give way. It was in vain that I interposed with an offer of my room, which was next door, and quite as good as the one in dispute. Finally the manager confirmed Mr. Scott in his pos- session, and threatened to send for the boots and two porters to eject Mr. Spenlow unless he withdrew. I met Beaumont five minutes later. He was wiping his forehead with his pocket-handker-</p>
        <p>. „ chief. "That man Spen!ow is mad," he said to me; "a regular monomaniac. Would you believe it, had to Promise him that he should have No. 65 as soon as the other beggar goes, before I could cuiot him?" " Why in the world should he be so particular to 1-a ve that room?" "Oh, I don't know—old crank!"</p>
        <p>It struck me as odd—very odd. I did not think it was pure cussedness on Mr. Spenlow's pnrt. The expression of rage and disappoint- ment on his face when he found that he would have to give up possession of the room seemed t.Do real for that. Why, then, should he make such a point of having that roOm, and no other? I conid find no answer to this (wry: and as I stood in the vestibule revolving this point, the object of my questioning came slowly past me and went out into the street.</p>
        <p>"(icing to call on his customers. I suppose— but has ho any customers?" I said to myself. On the spur of the moment I took the resolution of an idle man—l would follow him and see. . Of course, it was no business of mine, but my cr..-.-iosity had beon aroused. I wanted to see whether (in addition to his other peculiarities) this gentleman had given a false reason to ac- count for his frequent visits to the town. From one end of the town to the other I followed him. Not once did he enter a shop. But as he lounged along a wide thoroughfare a curious ineidont happer.ed. I was walking perhaps a score of yards behind Spenlow, when there met him a young man with a smartly-dressed girl on his arm. The girl started, blushed furiously, then blrned pale, and went by, her eyes on the ground. Mr. Spenlow grinned. and (quite ignoring her (,ompanion) took off hi; hat and made her a swooping bow.</p>
        <p>This act of mock politeness seemed to ion,' •-onne man to film " You dare to speak to the lady ! " he yelled, shaking his fist in the man's face—" You dare to look at her, or walk in the same street, azd</p>
        <p>I'll break every bone in your miserable body. D'ye hear me?'" This was accompanied by a look of such ferocity that the supposed "drummer" quailed, and glanced around, as though in search of a policeman. But the young girl came up, and passing her arm through that of her irate squire (without once looking at his antagonist), drew him from the spot. I left the mysterious Mr. Spenlow and followed the lovers.</p>
        <p>Presently they stopped at the spacious window of a shop crammed with furniture, new and secondhand. I strolled up beside them, and waited till I could catch the 'eye of the young man.</p>
        <p>" Excuse me," said I, in my most ingratiating manner, "but can you tell nie the name of the gentleman I saw you speaking to a moment or two ago?"</p>
        <p>"What gentleman?" he answered, evidently surprised. "The man you threatened," I began, but he interrupted me. . _</p>
        <p>"Hizia gentleman! Thon's an unhanged blackguard, an' ye can tell him I said sac!" "I see you know him," I broke in. "Now, I feel sure I have seen him before, under rather queer circumstances, I can't help fancying, and I would be greatly obliged if you could give me his name, and any details that would help me to identify him. You see, I am a reporter,' I went on, seeing that the young fellow's sunburnt face was beginning to spell out the word " refusal " "and it really is part of my business, I am afraid, to ask questions, sometimes, that may seem rather impertinent—but isn't that a tea- shop over there? May I ask if you have had tea yet? No? Perhaps you will allow me to ask you and the young lady to join me in a cup? Then, if you feel inclined to gratify my curiosity you may, but I shan't bother you about it." Scarcely waiting for the reply, which I feared might be a refusal, I led the way into the tea- shop, and presently we were seated in a quiet corner, where my fair guest presided with much grace over the teacups. Her name. I soon dis- covered, was Christine—Christine Welsby ; and as soon as I heard the name I remembered. I remembered where I had seen the man who called himself Spenlow. It was in a court of justice, which I had been attending for the pur- pose of reporting the trials. Spenlow had been in the dock, charged with having taken an ac- tive part in a great jewel robbery. It was pretty to watch the pair of lovers; they were so profoundly unconscious that there was anything remarkable in their demeanour, while all the time it was plain that they were living in an enchanted world of their own. They evidently belonged to the same class, but there was an air of greater refinement about the girl than her sweetheart could boast of. His name, he told me, was McDougal, and he was head gardener at Romford Chase, the sat- of Lord Pomford, seven miles away. As for Miss Welsby, she had lived at the Chase for several years in the capacity of lady's maid, which suf- ficiently accounted for her stylish appearance. When tea was over, I pressed a cigar upon my new acquaintance, and the girl good-naturedly said that if we wanted to smoke, we could go to the Smoking-room upstairs While she went back to the furniture shop. It seemed that their visit to the town was intended chiefly as a visit to the furniture shop. As soon as we were alone together, McDougal began: "1 couldna very well speak a fore her. but I've nae objection to tell you a' I ken aboot yon scoondrel. His real name's Hodgson, but I be- lieye he has as many fancy names as -would half- fill a directory. He's a first-class hand, they tell me, at two things—btrglary, and gettin' on tho soft side o' women.</p>
        <p>"ho frot on the soft side o' my Kirstie —hang urn! She was lady's-maid up at the House. then, and being in my lady's confidence, she had a good deal of liberty, especially when the frimily were at Roniford. And, in especial, she had charge of her leddyship's jewels.' Hero a gloomy look overspread the young man's intelligent face, and .a sigh that might almost have floated a fishing fleet into harbour broke from his lips. "Hodgson, or Montalambert, as he called himself then, met her, and made Idve to her. That was beer° she gave me her troth, you take me, an' before ever I had asked her, though we had been . keering company, off an' on, for some months.</p>
        <p>It appeared that she liked Montalambert best o' the tw), and you may guess .that I was mortal jealous. He had the advantage of see, in being able to come and go when he liked, and dr:lss like a gentleman, and speak like a gentleman too And for th,: time he stole her heart away from me. Perhaps I shouldn't say that, for it wasn't mine yet, except in my own mind and purpose: but, any way, she used to meet this Montalambert outside one of the little posterns that lead into the Chase on moon- light .nights; aid one night 'what did the foolish lassie do, but promise to bring him her mistress's jrwels for him to look at them. She had more sense, to be sure; than take them out 41' the house. but she told this scamp that if he came to a 5i,13 door just before dusk on a certain night, she would have the jewels there to she.,y him.</p>
        <p>" Perhaps I should tell von that .my lady's jewels were something by ene ordinae—l mean, something exceptional. But there was one that far surpass.A all the others. This was the famous :Mogul's Diamond. It was a yellow atone. as large as a big pigeon's egg, and of the purest water. as they call it. I've been told that my. lord has refused as much as twenty thousand Pounds for it. Ms grandfather. or his great-grandfather, brought it home ' from India in the clays when even noblemen and</p>
        <p>governors didn't 'think it beneath them to steal and rob :ike a common pickpocket when they had the. chance, and the booty was to their mind. _ _</p>
        <p>"There • were rings and bracelets, and other things, in my lady's jewel casket, but the bis diamond was worth the lot, ton times over. "W eu. Kirstie—that's Scotch for Christine or Christina, ye ken, and I call my little lass Kirstie. as it was rev mother's name—Kirstie came down to the still-room that night, carrying i the casket that held the jewels n her arms. She didn't think anyone was there, for she hadn't let in her liver. But no sooner had she stepped into the room than somebody—she could not say who it was--threw her down, clapping a thick handkerchief on her month to prevent' her screaming. Then they put a rag with chloroform on it under her nose, and very Soon they had the .casket at their mercy. She was found lying on a sofa in the little room. " Now, I'll no' conceal it from you, sir, but there were some that said that my Kirstie was as ba.d as the actual thief, or worse. They wouldn't believe, not if an angel came down, from heaven to tell them the iruth—they - would never believe that the poor girl had no hand in the robbery. And she lay ill for days with misery and anxiety. not to speak of the effects of the chloro- form! " "Were the thieves caught?" I 'asked. .</p>
        <p>„ ".Not exactly. Most of the trinkets were re- covered from pawnbrokers, chiefly in London; hut the gem of the collection, the great Mogul's Diamond, has never been seen from that day to this! Of course, all hope of finding it has long since vanished. But. Kirstio maintains that she is to blame for its loss, and she wanted to go and serve at the Chase for the rest of her life, without wages, as being the best she could do to make up for the breach of her -trust, as she, called it. Did 'ever mortal hear the like? 01 course, my lady wouldn't hear of such a thing, bit. Christie siv.v that she didn't like seeing her about her—which wasn't to be wondered at, see- ing that the sight of „her would always re- mind my lady of her loss. But you wouldn't bo'.ieVe the effect the loss of the big diamond had on poor Christie. • She just pined away, till we thought we were going, to lose her altogether. Now she's yielded so far as to say we are to be married in September I have hopes that in time I'll bring her to .forget the whole thing, but I doubt I'll have to give up my place and try to get another, for as long as she lives here she can never forget.” "And the man—was be not convicted? And if he was, how is it that be is at liberty ? " "Ho never 'was convicted of the • theft, for lb had been clever enough to let himself be over- heard bragging, in the . hearing of some strangers, that he was to have a. sight of the great' diamond that afternoon, • and how he was going to see it. So it was just possible that the robbery was committed by someone else, though there can't be a doubt that he was in it. None of the stolen property was traced to him or found on him, and, of course, his counsel made the most of that fact, and managed to get hiin off." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I -wonder he has the cheek to come to a neighbourhood where he is known," I said after a pause. "I don't think he is known in this town," answered McDougal. " He didn't stay here when he was after Kirstie. But he was arrested here, in the Alexandra Hotel, on the very night of the robbery, after he had gone to his bed- room. Since then, I have heard, he has been in prison for another robbery; but the hotel has changed hands since then, and I suppok they don't know he is a gaol bird." As he said this, McDougal was earnestly study- ing the dial of a timepiece, about the size . and shape of a curling-stone, which he hauled from the depths of his pocket by a massive silver chain, and announced that , Kirstio would be wearying" and it was time to go and find her. I accomoa.nied him to the furniture shoe. and on the way I was asking myself - once more why Sponlow should have returned so soon to tho scene of his crime. " Kirstie " met us at the door of the shop, and she asked me so prettily to go in and see a wardrobe they intended to buy, that I could not refuse. When the wardrobe</p>
        <p>had been duly admired and praised, -36 led the, way out of the shop, her sweetheart following close behind her, and as I sauntered after them my eye lighted on a large, comfortable-looking- easy-chair, covered in striped tapestry. Something about the chair struck me as fami- liar I could have sworn that I had seen it just lately—where? Then I remembered. I hack seen not this chair, but its fellow, in the bed- room which had been the cause of dispute be- tween Spenlow and the other man that very- afternoon. Spenlow had been sitting in it when he was summoned to give up possession of the- room, and that had drawn my attention to it. This chair was covered with tapestry of exactly the same pattern, and the size and shape of tho two pieces of furniture were identical. They- had once formed a pair. " Nice chair that, sir," said- the shopkeeper, ob- serving the direction of my gaze. "Wonderful: easy-chair that, sir, to rest in. And ridiculously cheap, sir. That chair has a history," he added, ir. a lower tone, with an air of mystery. Indeed! Whr.t may that be? I replied. "You've heard of the great Romford Chase jewel robbery, sir, no doubt? Well, a man named Hodgson, One of the criminals concerned in the robbery, was sitting in that very chair when be was arrested." " Indeed! " I cried, in an altered tone.</p>
        <p>"lie was indeed, 'sir. And when the police. smashed the lock and burt his door open, he out with a revolver, and sat ii that very chair you'ra. a lookin' at now, coverin' the constables with his gun. as he call,ml it." "I suppose his defence went no ..farther than, that?" "Meaning, sir?" "I 'suppose he didn't do any shooting?" "I was telling yoet, sir," said the shopkeeper, with a slightly aggrieved air. "Mr. Superintend-- ei t Smith, a marvellously smart officer, drew his baton as sv,iit as a. cat's jump, and went for. him."</p>
        <p>"Well, what happened?" I asked, for the nar- rator had made a dramatic pause. "Before the culprit had time to draw the. trigger," said the furniture dealer, in a deeply impressive manner, "the superintendent was on him. And it was only by the mercy of Provi- dence that the blow came down, not on tho head; which it must have smashed to- atoms, but -on the back of the chair he was sit- ting in—which you see before you. "Such was the force of the blow that it, chipped quite a large piece out of the mahogany. It has been cleverly mended; but you can see the mark easily still. See here, sip. The _chair was sold to Us not day, being much darra-ed; so we: can afford to let you have it at a very low price." "What do you call a low price?" I asked,. idly. for I had not the remotest idea Of purchas- ing the chair. _ _</p>
        <p>"Two pound ten, sir, and very cheap for what 7 was very nearly becoming . the scone of a. tragedy." I was quite unable to see why the chair should' have an enhanced value put upon it because a, constable did not hit a rogue sitting in it; but. as it wont] have hurt the man's feelings to say- so, I held my tongue.</p>
        <p>"Two guineas, sir!" cried the dealer, who evi— dently thought that my silence meant delibera- tion. .A pause. "Two pounds! There ! " Suddenly, as a flash of lightning from a. thunder-cloud, an idea shot across the field of my' brain, that, if true, would explain everything— the stran 7o whim of Spenlow to change his bed- room Y•ith every visit to the hotel, his anxiety to. kee-4 Mr. Scott's room that nizht—everything ! That chair must he mine! "Can you send the chair over to the Alex. andra Hotel to-night?" I asked. "Send it at once, sir. Have it there before- you are there yourself, sir 1 " exclaimed the- dealgr. I paid the money, saw the chair hoisted on the shoulders of a sturdy porter, and followed him out into the street. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Do you mind stepping into my hotel with me- -both of you—just for one minute?" I said to the young. couple, who were waiting outside the shop to bid me good-night.. "Well, if . it's only for a minute, sir," said McDougal, rather unwillimily, and .we walked' down the street after the porter. "Just ask Mr. Beaumont to step this way," I said to a passing waiter, when we entered the. hall of the hotel. The porter set down the chair, and I seated myself in it. "This voun.g lady, Beaumont," I said, in- dicating Christie, "had a very unpleasant ex- perience some little time ago. No doubt you have heard of it. She had a lover, who turned out to be a scoundrel, and be sneceeded .in rob- bing her of, a famous diamond belonging to her mistress. Lady Romford. The diamond was called the Mogul's Diamond. and was worth. I believe, something like £20.000 or £25,000. You no doubt remember that the police made every- effort to recover the jewel, but without suceess.i' (I had my. hand behind me as I was speaking. my fingers working down between the back of the chair and the deep, well-stuffed scat.) " suppose you would know the stone again if you saw it?" I went on, turning to the girl. "It could itot—Ah ! Is that it, do :von think?" At• that I whipped my hand from behind my hack,. and held up something that looked like a lump- of crystal. Everybody gave a cry or a silent gasp of as- tonishment and .delight. It was a jewel an em- peror might have envied. But Christie gave a. scream and hid her face in her hands. "Oh, take it—take it back to her- to my !a,./y, I mean ! " she cried, sobbing hyster.eally as she spoke. "Tell her that I had nothing to do with its being found. Tell her that this gentleman— oh, thank Heaven—thank Heaven that my good name is clear at last! " She trembled so violently that McDougal was- forced to support her by putting his manly arm around he waist. _ _</p>
        <p>"Perhaps you will lock this little gem up in your safe, Mr. Beaumont," said I, looking up. and handing it to him. As I did so the swing- door opened, and the pale face of Spenlow, showed itself in the opening. The first glance,. showing him the .chair with the peculiarly striped tapestry, the, agitated girl, the interested' faces of the spectators, and the passing of a glittering object from my hand to that of the- hotel manager, must have told him that his long search for the chair in which he had hastily hid-- dsix the great diamond when the police burst in upon him was at an end. Yes, the big diamond was found, and the. cloud that had. rested for a time on Christie's; fair fame rose and vanished for ever. As for the easy chair, I sent it to the lowly,. married couple as a wedding present, [THE END.I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CHEST TROUBLES.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CHEST TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>TI safe and certain Cure is 1/13110'S LIGHTNING COUGH eußs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry J. Rolls, The Grove Lodge, Malris, Newport, Mon., writes :—" Our little boy suffered for years with bronchitis and a chronic cougb,and I tried end- less cough syrups, embrocation, and doctors' medicines in vain. At last I tried your great romedy,and noticed an improvement after two doses only, and one large bottle effected a radical cure. It also cured him of a bad whooping cough, und I um sure that Veno's Lightning Cough Cure saved my boy's life." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is the surest remedy for all chest and lung troubles, price !lid., is. 11d., and 2s. 9d., of all chemists.</p>
        <p>Alone In Warrington. The police are making inquiries for the parents of a little bahy girl, two years old, who wa.s found toddling about in Orford Avenue at 0.45 on Saturday evening. A boy named Mullery found the child, who was very cold,but-appeared to be fairly well nourished. She was taken to the shop of Mr. George Cole, provi sion dealer, 1, Garner-street, and Mrs. Cole kindly looked after her. As no information has yet been forthPnming as to the parents or relatives, the child has been taken to the Workhuusr. She is bright- looking, with dark brown hair and blue eyes. At the time she was found she was wearing a dark blue cap with white string, white serge coat, dark blue serge frock, two pink flannelette underskirts, brown socks and brown sandals. The child appears to be very respectably connected.</p>
      </div>
