      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>tALL LIGHTS RESERVED.)</p>
        <p>"I have heard of your doings last night, Nancy." "My doings, father? I did nothing." Tie smiled, he was pleased that his girl could ihew a little spirit; she might have been a doll, ,tuffed with eaw-dust, for anything he knew of</p>
        <p>THE SUMMER-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>her mental composition "Forsooth, I've had. a talk with Vavasour; his wife seems to be a. fool. I shall not permit hcr to chaperone you again, child. I hear she mule . friends with a perfect stranger, invited him to ride in her coach, and after that was surprised to /ills: her diamond bracelet. Pray, were you also plundered?" The girl had recovered herself; she had been willing to tell her adventure; now something warned her to be secret. "You warned me, father; you said I was not to wear trinkets it the playhouse." "'Cad! so I did. 'Twas fortunate as you had to make acquaintance with a thief." But "—Nancy stammered, "it was not so, the gentleman who took charge of us was quite a .-pretty fellow. Depend on it, the thief was some ras:cal in the crowd." Mr. Newen frowned. were all fools. "Look you here, child. thcse cut-purses are mostly well-favoured and ci vii spoken," he added, with a sneer. ."I expected Mrs. Vava- Sour, at. her time of life, to be clearer-sighted. She has paid dear for her folly; 'twas the hand- somest bracelet I ever set eyes on. I'll wager my old crony was properly angered with her. If he'd cudgelled her, 'twould have been but her deserts." Nancy laughed. "Do you mean be should have beat her? Fie!. then, papa; 'twould be monstrous punishment for so sliTht an error." Mr. Newen could not believe his ears. He set his wig straight, and looked sternly at his daughter. "You have, I. see, miss, a good deal to learn before you are wed. Mrs. • Vava.sour has no pri- vate fortune. The ccach in which she rides. be- longs to her husband. How, then. dar.7d she presume to ask a stranger to ride in it. and when she was in cliari;e of my daughter? 'Twas most unseemly. Take my words to heze-t. child, and learn the duty a man expects from his wife. had an escort been needful, my friend would have provided it. I desire you to decline her future civilities. I cannot trust her with you." He proceeded to light his pipe at the flame of the candle placed beside him. Nancy tock this for dismissal, and gladly escaped. She walked up and down the parlour, too greatly agitated to sit still. Her dear mother had more than once said that the true secret of home happiness lay in perrect frankness hetweert its members; but she could not he frank to-day. She felt that she was more in sympathy with this new acquaintance than she could ever be with her father. Ile had accused her friend of being a thief! She would not listen to such a charge. Mrs. Vavasour must have dropped the bracelet. But her father would never believe it: she felt sure of that, and felt, also, that her future life must be full of deceit. She could not even cow- fde in Mrs. Vavasour. She sudd4nly stood still; light dawned on her She had one real friend. He bad said he would 'do anything to help her. At their next meeting she must ask for his advice. There came a faint • whisper of warning; she had been taught that good girls only made friendships with those of their own ex. The sole male friend allowed to a girl, in Nancy's narrow limits of -experience, was a brother, and, when she was married, a husband. This doubt was soon silenced; Nancy had neither brother nor sister. Her mother, the natu- ral confidante in such a case, had died years ago, and her father, instead of seeking consolation in his child's society, had thrown himself with creased ardour into business. Nancy's three lust vacations had been spent at school. She si?l,\ 1. "I see ni) other, way," shp thouOit: "I must consult my dear Harry." He had told her his name was Harry Raynon. and she thought how sweet this name Harry was.</p>
        <p>-1 KATIIA.RINE S. MACQUOID, author of "A Bad Beginning," "A Ward of the King," ' is Heart's Desire," "The Prince's Whim," "In An Orchard," Izc.</p>
        <p>A few years ago there stood on a wall in Tooting Graveney an unmistakable Dutch sum- mer-house. Bright green outside, an intense ver- million within, its three small diamond-shaped Ayindows were glazed bright yellow. The summer-house had a wide entrance-door; 7et it was stuffy, and smelt strongly of tobaren. Nancy Newen sat there every day. It rr--ick a 'change from the stately house which stood fbr !-back from the road; one could hardly distinguish objects thereon from the windows. Her rich father went daily to Threadneedle- street; her mother had died long ago. The brown-eyed girl, ever so sweet-looking in her -cambric gown and dainty mob-cap, spent most of her time alone in the summer-house. At that date, the beginning of last century, a :few vcry big houses stood on the high road, -with huge lawns in front, and a Massive pair of iron gates set In the red-brick walls. • Nancy was tired of her life. Home was duller :than school, for she had not any companions. When her father brought home a rare visitor, it was sure to be some old crony who could only talk business or politics. One school friend had visited her; but Nancy had not been allowed to return the visit. Her father deemed it safer to keep her under his own roof. She had grown to feel restive, and she fretted good many hours away in the summer-house n the wall. The plump pretty creature was not frettin; Ibis afternoon. She looked smiling anci excited. Her delicate rose-petal colour paled, and next moment deepened, as she caught sight of an ap- trcaching horseman. Alas! 'twas only a post- messenger on his way from town..._</p>
        <p>Ho told himself women</p>
        <p>Nancy bither re—ii under-lip. "Life has been 17 d ill, ' she sighed; "no chance of even get- ting into mischief." She laughed and blushed. Was last night true? or was it just a fairy tale Without an ending?" She had lain awake- all Slight, and now in broad daylight she still be- lieved in it. Her aunt, Mrs. Vavasour, had taken- her last night to the play-house. On arriving, the timid aunt was comused. She was about to ask the way to her box, when a gentleman sttpped for- ward and begged to help her. He at once offered his arm to Nancy, bade her aunt fellow, and soon placed them in their box. Mrs. Vavasour thanked him warmly-, but the stranger said his slight services had given him prodigious pleasure. He departed, and Mrs. Vavasour became ab- Sorbed in the play. Nancy's attention wandered; she sat thinking of the glances she had met when she looked at tile handsome stranger. Her fingers still tingled With the close pressure of his arm, as he bent over her, and whispered he was sure they had niet before. She could not see him in the theatre. To Nancy's delight when they quitted their box the stranger was waiting for them. He now offered his arm to Mrs. Vavasour, and the girl noted the flash of her aunt's brace- let. He led the ladies to their coach which, 'without his guidance, would have been hard to -find in a confusion of coaches and chairs, irunken men reeling about, link-boys screaming ;hoarsely for different vehicles. The stranger ex- pressed surprise that Mrs. Vavasour should have only the escort of her coachman and footman. " Heavens! Sir," cried the simple woman, "is ,there danger of footpads?" "There is such a risk; if you like, Madam, I ill gladly go with you a few miles or so; 'tWOUld be safer." She was terrified and, without waiting to so- Thank you heartily, sir," she cried, "if you spare time please go all the way; my niece nres at Tooting, and I but two &amp;les further; 'twill be a boon to two frightened females." He bowed, and placed himself in the coach cilyosite Nancy. -</p>
        <p>The days were rapidly shortening. This morn- ing, the fourth since Nancy's visit to the theatre, • it was hardly full daylight when Mr. Newen came out to smoke his pipe in the summer-house. Under his arm he carried his eheque-book—i.i thc:se days a cumbrous-looking article. • As he seated himself on the I.ench, a 13plught which had struck him over-night delayed his examination of the last entries in the book. Ile was preoccupied by the change he now saw in Nancy. What had come to the, quiet, well-behaved girl? She had grown suddenly very pretty, and her manner was never the same; she had been one day almost saucy in . answering him, and yesterday so dreamy, that he caught her putti7g a lump of sugar into the tea pet, insteaa of his tea-cup. Even after this • she had been .cry inattentive; when he told her the chimneys needed the sweep, she cheer- i fully answered: "Oh. yes, father, I u.. packed it and put it in the cellar." Evidently she mca...4; the Schnapps lately sent him from Holland. Then she had begged pardon, changed colour, , and looked confused. It was very strange. • A sound disturbed him. He instinctively thrust the book into a bulky pocket; he had forgotten the unused door in the fence, and was • startled to hear footsteps behind the cider-bush. ' In an instant two masked men sprang up tiv3 knoll, and stood on either side of him. Each man held a 'pistol, the muzzle pointed at the% merchant's head. "Your pardon, sir," a pleasant voice said. " We have no wish to incommode you, but we need cash." Newen had recovered himself. He smiled SA</p>
        <p>Mrs. avasour fell asleep; it was this journey in the delicious semi-darkness which filled the ,cirl's thoughts. The stranger had talked in a low, sweet voice, bending forward till his breath warmed her thc-ek ; he questioned her about her life, and leurncd that she lived alone• and spent her day- time in the summer-house on the wall. At this I.e sighed, " I must see you again, and soon, shall not be happy till I can see yon every day. Is there no Way to the summer-house but through the front entrance?" Her cheeks still flamed as she remembered low she told him about the unused gate in the oak fence behind the shrubbery. How could Ehe be So bold? mbShe whispered this when the coach stopped v ,ruPtlY at the big iron gates, and Mrs. ~Lvasour woke up It seemed to all the party tne drive had. been very short. Ihe footman opened the coach-door, but the stranger sprang Out, and helped her down the steps. "My father comes Leme each evening at 40.170 she whispered. f j-is warmly pressed her hand; then the tall c?otman opened the iron gate, and closed it Lc- idlid Nancy..</p>
        <p>I he answered: 11. I "I am sorry to disappoint you, gentlemen, The r round below the summer-house had been but I have no cash with me. my watch and 'arti6ciallY raised, and a few steps led down to seals you might have had, but 'Gad I left ?en! this knoll. On this grew an enormous elder tree, within." Shewing both his hands, l he ad tet! teovered just now with a profusion of rich fruit, "And you can see for ;ourselves I wear aa -tile gentle wind swayed the huge panicles of rings." turple berries which effectually screened Nancy's " Permit us, sir, to satisfy ourselves on those ~ceei::c•er from the house. The -little wooden door Points." As he spoke, the elder of the men put by was mesked by a plane tree which had his hand on Newen's coat.!leeves flung a branch over it This door was never The merchant swore a big oath. I ook off the detainina le(,)ekedt its existence seemed to be forgotten. ' "Have a care," he SA . r Early this morning Nancy " ar had crept behind the hand. "I am a justice of the peaee, with full ee, and with some difficulty had undrawn the Power to commit you , me. both ~to gaol for an vastY bolt, and set the door when she had freed aseault, with intent to rob , it from a growth of ivy slightly ajar. i "Call- for help at your ,reril, sir, we shoot you dead at the first outcry, was the answer. _., She was sitting on the bench that ran around Ins summer-house; a slight sound made her turn .In an instant, while one man firmly held 4nd look at the gate. Newen 's arrea, the other felt in his coat pocket. Her friend of last night was standing there; ' and. produced the cheque-book. The merchant .1410 had not hitherto felt shy of him; now she felt tempted to run away. - vanity struggled to free himself; then, seeinF, resistance was useless. he sank on the bonen behind him, cursing his assailants for a pair of . Her visitor saw the flutter of her gown; in an __um !nstant he sprung up the knoll, and stood beside '"'' •Ler, ; The younger man bent ove