      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>/ -</p>
        <p>The Exarain.er, Saturday, January 30, 1909</p>
        <p>toiled upwards with food for London. In the Strand the bus.tle was prodigiouc, and as he turned up a side street to avoid it he found himself involved in a labyrinth of empty carts and waggons issuing. from Covent Garden Mar- ket. With some trouble he extcated himself, and regained the Strand, riding slowly on till he reached the Hostel Lt the corncr of Chancery- lane. Here he left his horse, walked on to the 'ray old Inn, and learnt that I\lr. Young, the cashier, was at that moment tteakfaAing</p>
        <p>The grey-haired cashier took his seat behind the bank counter some minutes earlier than usual; Mr. Newen at at the back of the counter. Ile could SC e the faces of those who entered the bank without being observed. St. Dunstan's clock struck ten; on the last stroke a man came into the bank.</p>
        <p>Till now Neweiv had forgotten that. MI assailants were masked. lie noted that this man's face was. prepossessing. - The cashier looked keenly at the -zlwcomer, who presented a draft for payment. "A large -sum, sir," said the cashier. lie rang the hand-bell beside him. A door opened, and in came four Bow-street constables.</p>
        <p>Newen • started up, holding out the warrant he held ready. "Arrest that person for assault and rob- bery," he cried; " that draft was stolen." Taken by surprise, the thief was handcuffed before he could ue his pistols. lie cursed and swore, but resie.tance was useless. The charge was heard at Bow-street, and the prisoner was duly committed.</p>
        <p>Her lover visited Nancy the day after his first visit, and again next day. These visits filled her witli happy thought. lie so convinced her of his love that at their last 'meeting she promised to marry him secretly, on condition she might return to her father's house till her husband could openly claim her. Then he pas- sionately kissed her. "We will meet again without delay," he said. Moro than a • fortnight paeeed by, and her Harry did not enne back. Still she loved him, the girl had not dreamt of the rapture love had brought into her dull life. His continued ab- sence taught her she could not live without him and the warmth and bli,.)s he died into her poor starved soul; her strange new feelings tortured her. She could nether eat nor sleep, and she shrank from her father's ecrupany.</p>
        <p>He wai4 .greatly troub:ed by this new change in her. She looked ll and sad. She had *become 6:.% silent.</p>
        <p>To-night he looked keenly at her: "I hare a strange story to tell you, child." "have you indee(l, father?" Tier eyes shone, a tinge of colour rose in her pale face. "I have made a curious discovery, Nancy. Yon remember the night when Mrs. Vavaaiour lost her bracelet?" _</p>
        <p>"I shall never forget it, father," she said, passionately. lie frowned. "Less than a fortnight ago I narrowly escaped robbery. It is clear to me, from a talk I've had with Mrs. Yavasour, that the younger of the two scoundrels who attacked me in my own summer-house is the so-called gentleman who escorted you home on that even- ing." Nancy had risen; she stood with clasped hands and frightened eyes. "You were attacked in the summer-house; oh! tell me about it., father?" She went white, and trembled violently. Ire had not expected his danger would so scare her. "Gracious, child, be pacified, the villains did not harm me." . .</p>
        <p>—..-.... "Tell me the truth; I must know it!" sho cried, vehemently. lie stared; she seemed to him unreasonably agitated. "Not to-ni:;l4, my girl; it might give you a bad dream. Now I'll bid you good-night." The girl could not sleep; the horrible truth forced itself upon her. She began to understand the heavy presenthnek that had darkened her mind. She rose early; but her father had already gone to town, leaving word he should not return till the following day. When he came home, her haggard fare shocked him. Under her eyes were dark circles; a fever spot burnt on each cheek. lie also looked aged and haggard. It had come on him with a shock, though he might have expected to hear it, that the man whose arrest he had caused was sentenced to be hanged. A feeling of re- morse made him almost tender to Nancy. "Shall we go and see Mrs. Vavasour to-morrow, child? She's on the further side of London." She thanked him, and went to bed early; her weight of trouble lightened with the hope .of seeing Mrs. Vavasour. ller aunt would be more explicit about this attack, and she resolved to tell her everything. We must start very early," her father said. "It is a long drive."</p>
        <p>Latchford out cleverly at tirnes but having to yield to excellent combination at others. , The visitors' goals were scored by Banks (2) and Spensley and Newell one each.</p>
        <p>The Second team met with their second defeat of the season at Ogten. a stirring game resulting in favour of the home eleven by '2 —I. Tne pissing of the Oxton fonvaris was beautiful, backed up as they were so splendidly by their halves, while their full backs also played with good lodgement. Latchford after scoring through Allenier pressing very hard for an equilisin.g point but could not obtain it and undoubtedly the best team won. Tue Latchford backs played won.</p>
        <p>To-day the return matches with Altrinchana St. Gaol' are played. 'rho Virst Eleven at Latahford is as follows : —W. Unsworth, goal ; C. Piinston, T. Dixon, backs; H'. Groan is, T. B. Wilkins in, Alhinson, halves; If. B ,oth, D. Travis, IL Scatt, E'. Bulks, W. Spensley, forwards ; bully-otT 2.41</p>
        <p>The Second Eleven at Altrincha-n is :—A. N. Other, goal ; J. Wilkinson, W. H. Pickles, backs; Kenworthy, A. Knight, P. Tole, halves ; A. Starkey, H. Parker, A. Allend•r. W. Nfinards, C. HicknAy, forwArl4. Referee :—Mr.U. Warburton. frain froat Central,</p>
