      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>VII.</head>
        <p>VII.</p>
        <p>The bright morning cheered Nancy. Busied in her thoughts, she did not look out of the coach window, and her father was reading a letter. The coach suddenly stopped. Looking out, the girl saw they had reached a long common, and that there was a crowd of people. Newen put out his head. "What's the matter?" he cried. -</p>
        <p>The lackey got down from the coach-box. "They're hangin' a highwayman, sir, what 'as robbed a rich gentleman in his own grounds." His master did not speak. "See, sir "—the man pointed to the fast-increasing crowd—" they're that boisterous, they scares Donkin; he says shall us wait till the hangin's done with?" At last the road was freer. Newen now raised his head and bade the lackey tell Donkin to drive on. Then he turned to look at Nancy; she lay senseless in her corner, pale as though she were dead. Newen bade the coach drive home; the girl did not revive till it reached the iron gates; she then opened her eyes, and said the doctor must not be sent for. Her father bade the housekeeper put her to bed, and give her some wine. "It is only a faintness," he said. The girl did not resist; she even took the food and wine offered her; then she desired to be left alone; no one must disturb her. She soon fell asleep from exhaustion; it was dark when she awoke. She lay for some time listening, then a- sudden hope came, and with it a strange courage. She rose, softly opened her door, and listened. From her father's chamber, at the end of the long gallery, she heard stertorous breathing; evidently his sleep was troubled. Moved by a strong impulse she was quickly dressed, resolved to find her way to the summer-house. She hurriedly put on a warm cloak and hood, when a smart tap came at her window. Nancy started; instantly the tap was sharply repeated. She did not stay to think; gliding softly down- stairs, she groped her way to the kitchen and found the tinder-box.</p>
        <p>Light brought fresh courage. From the pantry steps led her up to the shrubbery. In the star. light she soon redched the house corner, and saw a dark figure coming towards her. It was her lover, but he did not take her hands in his; he stood with bent head, silent "Thank Heaven!" she murmured,</p>
        <p>"you arc</p>
        <p>sarc," " Safe?" he echoed: "I feel like a hound; I could not face the shame of discovery. Some- thing warned me your father would take prompt measures; he would cot else have yielded so quickly. But my comrade was of another opinion; I urged him to give up the enterprise. I said there was danger. Well, we quarrelled." He- sighed heavily. "Bet for you, Nancy, I should have been trapped as he was. You are good, sweet girl, to come to such as I am. I am here only to ask your forgiveness; I cannot live without it. I beseech you add this one more Messing to those I owe you." "I do not understand; you owe Inc nothing." Her voice was choked; she could hardly keep f torn weeping He took her cold hands and passionately kissed them. "Child! 1 mean what I say:- but for your sweetness I should not have shrunk from this last outrage; spite of my guilt, my cruel de- ceit, I love you truly. The hope of winning your love has made me a better man. I was not always a thief, .my Nancy. Your dear face. your voice, filled me with longing for all I had given up, and with loathing for my present life. If I become honest, dearest, it will be your Ivork." "You are honest now," she said, tears streaming over her cheeks. ." When I tell this to Ty. father he will help you to begin life</p>
        <p>- --- afresh." He shook his head. "No. I cannot hope it. My one chance of safety is to quit England at once; if I escape, you shall have tidings; if I ant silent, forget me. Now, Farewell." He strained her to his heart, while he pressed hiss after kiss on her lips. Nancy fondly clung to him. "I'll not say farewell. I'll die rather than give you up," she sobbed. "I owe you ever)-- thing. You have tauzht me what happiness is. I shall wait for you, Harry. If you never come, I'll live and die your widow, but. you will come hack. I know you will. I cou!d not feel such faith in you it you were not true. You will coma bark, and I'll be your wife." A dog barked loudly. then another. "Hasten in, darling." he whispered. Another passionate emb:ace, and he disappeared in tile darkness.</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>It was a distinctly creditable performance by War- rinaton on Satiarday to beat the greatly imnroved St• Helens team on their own ground. The Saints made no secret of the fact that they intende I and expected to avenge the Lancashire Cup defeat sustained at the hands of the Wirepullers e illy in the season. They had a strong side out too, but Warrington, with Dickenson and Jenkins back again, were t o god for them. It was an exciting and often brilliant game, for while Warrington held the lead throughout, till Saints once got within a point of their visitors, aryl might easily have snatched the game out of the fire. Warrlngton eventually won by 15 points to 9, thouuh as each side scored three tries the winners had nothing to spare except as regards goal-kicking. The homesters missed some, easy shots, while Warrington were fortunate enough to secure a bull's eye every time. It is not often they win by superior goal ki3k- ing, and I have seen din boot very much on the whet leg against the same opponents.</p>
        <p>Warrington started well, and in this CASA the good start was rather more than half the battle. They were all over the opposition from the first twenty minutes, and put on ten points, which is a big start for even the home team to wipe out. Fish and Tteharne got fine tries after brilliant combination, and Fish and Thomas were responsible for the goals. Then the homesters bad a look in, and bef,ire the interval they had scored tw tries, bat failed to land the equalising golls. In the second half, Warrington narrowly missed adding to their cc ire early on, but the Saints were more successful and scored a third try, which would have .been a winning point if converted. Warrington still held a lead of one point, and matters were decidedly interesting. At this stage it looked slight odds on St. Helens palling the match out of the fire, when an incident occurred which settled the issue. One of tha home halves kickeducross for Flannagan, folretf l of the fact that where Flannagan was Fish was not far off. The cross kick was " pin" to the Warrington crack, who snapped up the ball under Flanrngan's nose and sailed right in under the posts, losing the full back en route. It was a great run from nearly half way and showed Fish at his best. lie easily added a goal and gave Warrington a six points lead which they maintained to the end.</p>
        <p>As already indicated. Warrington playea a power- ftil and clever game. They were just a bit too good for the Saints, who had no men quite equal t hold- ing the Warrington third line in check. Taykr was the best of the four on the day's play, his running, swerving, and kicking being magnificant. The halves did well, though Jenkins got roughly handled at times through Lloyd passing too mechanically. Brooks was much missed, of course. A word of praise is due to the forwards, who had rather the better of the argument with the St. Helens six. Tilley was safe, as usual, but was beaten in kicking by Barton, who played a great game for the home team.</p>
        <p>There has becn no change this week in the leading positions of Clubs in the League. Oldham got a severe fright an 1 nearly saerifieed their long: unbroken home record to Rochdale Hornets. The latter are humble side in the Rugby woild, but they always give their near rivals a good game, and twice this season they have run them to a goal-kick. Oldham are a lucky side, but Fortune will not always smile She forgot to look upon the Spinners when they were at Wileerspool. Perhaps she is preparing another rebuff for February 20th. Warrington would dearly like to smash up the home record which has with- stood assault for two years. And they are just the side to do it. Bear the date in mind Warrington, and prepare !</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows: For .Agst. Per</p>
        <p>P. W. L. 11. (4. T. P. C. T. P. ettitace Oldham 2l 19 2 0 56 80 352 19 20 08 90.47 Wigan 2O 18 2 0 80 111 493 26 25 127 9000 Halifax —.21 17 3 1 52 81 347 18 21 los 85'71 Batley 20 13 5 2 28 50 206 23 29 133 70110 ffunslet 2O 13 6 1 36 57 243 28 39 173 6750 Wddersfild24 15 7 2 46 74 314 41 38 196 66.66 MerthyrT 16 10 5 1 26 39 169 31 25 143 65.62 Salford —.20 13 7 0 49 52 254 29 41 181 65.00 Runcorn l9 12 43 1 21 50 198 23 25 121 6519 Waletl'ldT2l 13 7 1 49 72 314 32 39 181 64.28 Warring'n 21 12 8 1 43 47 227 24 38 162 59.52 Broughton 21 12 8 1 51 56 270 37 49 221 59.52 Ebbw Valel9 10 8 1 23 51 199 31 40 182 55•26 [lull 94 12 11 1 54 78 342 39 55 243 5208. Keighley 21 19 10 1 46 55 257 34 56 236 50 00 Dewsbury 21 10 11 0 46 52 243 37 50 221 47 61 Leeds 29 11 11 0 39 63 297 42 47 225 45.45 Leigh 2l 912 0 30 36 168 43 50 236 4285 Hull K.R 20 811 1 no 58 274 43 65 281 42.50 St. Helens 20 711 2 31 47 203 46 56 260 4000 Mid-Rleda 14 5 8 1 13 22 92 25 35 155 39.27 Bradford 21 813 0 38 52 232 41 57 253 38.09 Swinton —2l 815 1 31 39 179 44 71 301 35-40 York 91 Gl4 1 40 50 230 51 77 333 3095 Rochdalell2o 513 2 13 30 116 35 46 208 30110 Widnes —.lB 411 3 18 27 117 26 40 172 3055 Barrow ... 20 614 0 23 80 136 47 75 319 301)0 TreherbertlG 411 1 15 17 81 18 47 177 2812 Barry l6 2 14 0 8 16 61 52 93 383 12.50 Aberdare 14 113 0 17 24 106 50 77 331 714 Bramley 18 117 0 23 22 112 60 109 447 5.55</p>
        <p>Warrington and Wakefield Trinity renew their acquaintance to day at Belle Vue, Wakefield, and as they are neck and neck in the League there should be a big struggle. The Trinity will want to wipe out that defeat at Wilderspool on the 10th, but if form is anything to go by the task should prove beyond them. Warrington are playing an exceptionally strong game at present, and with Brooks back, they should be cqual to the task set them to-day. I have not been to the cathedral t(avn since that memorable cup-tie %Thiel' Warrington lost by a goal-kick after having all the best of the game, but I hope to see them have better luck to-day.</p>
        <p>All being well, Warrington should place their NI Plavitm strength in the field, viz. : Tilley, full back; Fish. Dickenson, Taylor, and Treharne three-quarters *Brooks and Jenkins, half hacks ; 011, TIIORMS;' Dowell, Chesters, A. 13.ardman and Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>The " A" tram has ascended to the head of the Lancashire Combination table as a result of Saturday's win over St. Helens "A "and the defeat sustained by Wigan "A " at Pemberton. They have excellent prospects of the championship, but it is too soon to begin jobilating. There are three away engagements which must all he marked "dangerous," viz., Wigan flighfield, Pemberton and Leigh Shamrocks. A three points lead might easily be dropped in these engagements.</p>
      </div>
