      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NEV SCHOOL MANAGERS.</head>
        <p>NEV SCHOOL MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the managers, the Rey. Father Conroy was appointed foundation manager of the Latchford Roman Catholic School. Councillor Singleton was elected a representative manager of the Ilarnilton-street Church of England School. With reference to the resignation of Alderman Pierpoint as representative manager of Christ Church School, it was decided to ask Councillor W. B. Roberts to fill the posi- tion, and, failing him, Mr. George Venn. TRE INCREASE OP TEACIIER3' SAL IRIE3. The School Management submitted Cm annual list of increases of teachers' safeties in accordance with the Mile. The total amount of the increases is £1,083. Mr. UPSON moved and Councillor PEMBERTON seconded the adoption of the minutes. Alderman Bseeerr remarked that the in-reaee went on sear after year and the amount was boning serious. Had the Committee any idea when they would reach rock bottom? Mr. ROBERTS Said it must not be assumed that the advances represented an increase on the year's expendi- ture of £1,083, because a number of the appointments were appointments not exactly of junior teachers but teachers who commenced ate commencing salary. Some of these were replacements of teachers who had retired perhaps near thy praximatn of the scale, and new teachers had heenNppointed at the maximum commenc- ing salary. The actual increase for the year, therefore, was not £1,083. Wits it not futile to discuss these questions of automatic increases when they were all based upon a contract entered into, the terms of which were thoroughly discussed a long time ago? If they took the case of It man with a commencing salary of £lOO, with an undertaking that it be performed his duties satisfactorily, the committee would gradually increase his salary to £l5O, it was too late to raise the question of that increased expenditure when the contract had been mutually agreed upon. They could only consider the whole question of the automatic increases of sala-ies. Ile maintained that anxiety in ' regard to education expenditure was not solely the property of members of the Corporation. The com- mittee itself was just us anxious. Mr. JOLLEY pointed nut that in consequence of the changes of staff mentioned by Mr. Roberts the net increase was relatively small. For 1907 the increase was about £l4, but that was before the staffing of the two Council schools had come upon the rates. Mr. tlesost said Mr. Roberts had put the matter very plainly with reference to the salaries question. All the new teachers came under a different scale to what the majority of the teachers whose names appeared on the agenda were enjoying. The first scale the com- mittee adopted compered favourably with most towns, but he was sorry that the new scale under which teachers were now being engaged was not anything like so good in relation to the old one, as the first one was to the scale of salaries in neighbouring towns, lie anticipated that in a year or two the total paid in salaries would he less than was actually paid now. The ranks of the older teachers were thinned by promotions and marriage, and new teachers came along and were appointed at a lower scale than many teachers were now enjoying. In a very short time the expenditure in salaries would not show an advance even of £l4, asssuming that the num- ber of school departments remained the same. The DIRECTOR slid the total increase last year was 21,078, and this year £l,oB3—a difference of £5. The number of teachers appointed during the year included the whole staff and headmaster of the Christ Church School, assistant mistress of the Bolton School,assistent mistress at the Ikamont School, and here and there about the town extra .teachers on account of the increase in the number of children. TIIE SECONDARY SCIIOOL.</p>
        <p>Mr. BURGESS clli attention t.) thqfotlotciog minute :—" A letter from the Board of Education: confirming their provisional approval of the committees propasals for improving the accommodition at the Secondary Scholl was road and note I." Had the Board finally approved of the pl ins, and, if so,how soon would the work he commenced? Mr. 110BERTS replial that the work was actually in hand. The approval of the Board WAS such as to justify the work being proceeded with and it would Co completed as early as possible. E •IPIRE DAY.</p>
        <p>of the School Nfanage nest Committee.—" Suggestilos were enhinittel from the Warrington an-I District Teachers' Association in connection with the celebra- tion of Empire Day this year by the school children and it was resolved, thst the celebrations take place on Emp're Day (Monday, the 24 h May), and that arrange- ments be made for the children to be assembled in groups and for addresses suitable to the occasion to be given ; and that the seinols he closed in the afternoon so that the annual school children's sports may be held." It was doubtful if, by any system of grouping, they could make the celebration so widespread as it was by the old method. Mr. Reenters replied that it ?.i,l not always fall to pilrl'o:NeNeerfrinn,,iqi their lot to have such a youthful Nfayor and Mayoress as they had bad daring the past two years—(hear, hear and laughter)-.-wh I had gone through a vast amount of work. The effectiveness of the dernanst ration last year was to a great extent modified by the fact that a large number of scholars in different p las of the town ti of to wait so long for the arrival of the Mayor and Mayoress that they had got tired. The Mayor himself must have almost been worn out in having to visit the whole of the schools. Th 4 primping of schools would . I shAolrttleenrmthaen au.ielAhet°,lidtlinqotfothili'lnwkitnhgevmwinonutled The test match at Newcastle proved a great success • " ' • ' • I 1144 less effective. The teachers themselves suggested in all tespects, the Union team winning by 15 point.; . , the change. to 5, an w d the Kangaroos netting their recd gate of 1 Mr. PAIRCT,OUGII asked where the groups were to the tour. 22,000 neople. paid for admission and the he formed. If the weather was bad they would have to proceeds were g,561. With such takings as 3:23 at be formed inside the schools, and would there be a Widnes, 125 at Batlev,end .V 0 this week at Keighley, i building large eneugh for the purpose ? Alderman BeaNte-rr : That was my point—Thether the Australians need a " bumper " or two to make i the schools would hold the whole of the children. the tour anything like a success. The K•mgaroos are . 1 Mr. UrsoN said that last year the schools first Yisired not likely to act rnr.re than half the £10,031 raised l'Y by the Mayor hal their celehratien and then the chit- the All Blacks. The third test will take place at dren went on with their work in the ordin try way, In Glasgow on Wednesday next. Dickensan is selected the majority of the sctoela, however, there was an in place of Jenkins ;it centre and Brooks will have atmosphere of excitement which prevented any work Lilly of Widnes as planer. The halves will thus being done. The teichers were somewhat affected in both be Wartington lads. the same way ; the children became worn out with the prolcnged excitement. In the event of the day being wet it. was suggested that the day should be celebrate • Hockey. i in the same way as hitherto, because there was no one building large enough to hold 2.000 or 2,500 scholers. 1 Latchford First experienced no difficulty in defeating The new arraneemeut would da something to break Liverpool University at Stanley last week, the score down parochialism, which often kept schools separate being 4-1 in their favour. Some very good hockey and the committee asked for a trial of the new method t was shown on both sides, the University backs keeping at least for one year. (lies-, hear:.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Combination League table up-to- date is :</p>
        <p>. - P. W. L. D. Pt. Warrington l7 ... 12 ... 1 4 ... 28 Wigan Oldham l5 ... 9 3 ... 3 ... 21 Pemberton Pia Shamroelss Wigan Hightleld Runcorn Broughton Rangers 17 ... ... 9 ... 2 ... 14 Saltord l7 ... 5 ... 9 ... 3 ... 13 St. Helens Ince Rochdale Hornets l5 ... 5 ... 10 ... 0 ... 10</p>
        <p>A friendly match with Salford St. Dart's is the home fare to-day, and Warrington " A" will give a trial to seceral local juniors. Team : J. Bramhall, full back ; Bradshaw. Landor, Ifockenhull and Green, threegnart.rs ; Lees and Lloyd, half-backs ; Sling irs, Na3lur,Gibbs,Bradshaw, Hewitt and Leslie, forwards.</p>
