      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>GM' OF ENGRAVINGS'.</p>
        <p>It was repertel that a gift of a folio of engraving t had been received by the School of Art froon the rel L. tives of the late Mr.J. C. Tholipion, sal it was resolve! that the same be accepted with thank. The minutes of the Schoel Itaganant Ce:nraitter ware then agreed to, o.njihe 01 itien of Nfr. Llesal seconded by Cauncillor Pint rialivrog. FISEDIN3SJ 10J",•CIIILD The Scheel Atten.lince S th-co.n nittee's initiates included pirticulars of the art rigs wets in tie ter the feeding of scho.il childrio. It wis reselvei that the teachers be th Inked for their rail y offer Le co-neerete in this matter. It was ale) res alved th it Mr. Walter Thomason be spa iintei in de c aek at a weekly wage of 305., and that the ten ler of Nleisrs. Migitie Bros., for the conveyance of the me ila at a cost of £3 15s. per week, he accepted. Authority 'via given to the Director to employ the necessary wasistance -for the cook at 03 trven Cot tape. The following tenders were accepted ; Frederick Harding for the supply of Dread, ; Nfr. rnomis , Ilthiwin for the supply of matt, ; Mr. liezh McDermott for the sopoly of groceries and p-ovisious. Reports upon the cases sent in by the he ol teachers as being insu!ficiently fel were considered, and th ase to whom meals were to be at once provided were determined upon. In certain cases it was decided to request the School Medical 0 ficer to ex tame the children, with a view to furnishing his opinion as to whether they were being su'ficiently fed or were being neglected. Alderman ELLISON, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, said be understood that the scheme for feeding the school children was workinz very satisfactorily. There were about 220 children partaking of meals daily. Mr. PLINSTJN, in secen ding, said he felt certain that every member of that authority would agree th it their 4hanks were die to the Direetor and his staff, and the teachers for the admirable way in which they had carried forward this work. There had not been a single hitch. If there was one individatt in ire thaa weather who had rendered use,til work it had been Mr. Elliot., of the Education (Mice. Councillor LonD asked how the Committee managed to advertise for tenders for the conveyances of meals am' accept a tender on the stale d ty. Bath these proceedings were recorded as having happened on December 29th,. The DIRECTOR pointed out that the minute re adver- tising was the recommendation of the sub-° inimittee which was submitted on December 29th. In the mean- time tenders were received and ono was accepted by the full Committee. The minutes were approved. THE PROPOSED NEW S 7103 L. At a meeting of the Sites and Baildings Sub-com- mittee, a letter was read from the Bear" of Education pointing out that the Board had not yet received particulars of any site for a new public elementary school in the neighourhood of Wellfleld-street, and inviting the observations of the sub-committee before definitely deciding as to the making of a substantial deduction from the grant n conditionally due iii respect of the infants' depirtment of the Moil y School in vie.; of the protracted delay which had occurred in this in itter. After some discussion, it was resolved that the special committee appointed on the Void May last be instructed to seek it further interview with Lord Lilford's agent, with a view to securing from , him a firm offer of a suitable site for a school in the Wellfleld-street district. Mr. FAIRCLOCGII proposed the adoption of the minutes. He said they had not managed to secure n site for the new school up to the present time. Mr. Selby, Lord Lilford's agent, had been aw ty, but they were determined the question should be settled one way or the other. If they could not settle at the neat inter- view they would immediately look out for other sites. Alderman SMETHurtsT seconded. Mr. PLINSTON : Seeing it has taken from May to January and one letter from the Beard of education to bring about this resolution, how many letters will be required before action is really taken? Mr. FAIRCLOUGLI said he was afraid he could not answer that, but he hoped t have something to report at the neat meeting. The minutes were passed. MEDICAL INSPECTION. The Medical Inspection Sib-Cemmittee reported that they met, in conjunction with the Health Committee, a deputation from the Warrington Division of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Branch of the British Medical Association, and beard their views as to the treatment of school children found by the School Medical Oilier to be defective. After discussinz the matter with them, the deputation were requested to put their views into writing and to submit theta for their farther con- sideration. The minutes were passed on the proposition of the CIitIRMAN, seconded by /therm in Satrritcasr.</p>
