      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>STOCKTON REATEI.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON REATEI.</p>
        <p>!TILL CLIEFE AND STOCKTON LIE VTII UNITED BAND OF 110PE.—A cinematog,raph entertainment, tinier the auspices of this useful organization, was given in the Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday evening. The exhibition was supplied by the " Onward " Man- chester, and the net proceels were in aid of the Forward movement" of the Lancashire and Cheshire band of Hope Union. Mr. Arthur Mounfield i-ett, of time society) presided, and the proceedings were opened by the hymn Dare to he a Daniel " being thrown upon the shoot., the audience joining hetrtily in the singing. Time maim mted pictures were of an interesting character, and included, "The rival brothers," " Tramp and washer wom tn," " Photographic terms," " Rebellions schoolgirls," " Tommy's box of tools," "The sticky bicycle," " Storm and sunshine," " Tile stolen bridle," " The bargain hunters," " Father's baby," and" From the field to the oven." The pictures were very steady and remark mhly clear, and were skil- fully manipulated by Mr. Eddy, who, in addition to the animated pictures showed a milliner of still pictures, of a very interasting character. Variety was introduced by way of illustrated songs and recitations. Miss May Isherwood sang very sweetly " Angie 1 inrie" and Miss. H. Nfounfleld contributed " Tit for tat," Nliss Minnie Whitmore recited "Two little shoes: the pictures thrown on th*sheet illustrating these songs being very effective. Mr. G. A. Whitfield acted as accompanist to the various items.</p>
        <p>OVER.-FATNEiS SRO R rEN'i LIFE. \V HY PLT rUP WITH I AN LONGER?</p>
        <p>No doubt of it; fatty lietrt, tiCh is almost inseparable from extreme stoutness, fatty kidneys, and fatty liver have been the cause of countless catastrophes. The whole system is in a condition of fatty congestion; it is a positive shame to neglect that diseased state. And, after all, why put up with it ? Here is a recipe of a simple ani hannless cure, the ingredients of which any chemist will supply, viz. : One hilf-ounce of Martn )It, one ounce of fluid extract of Glycyrrhiza BP., one ounce of pure Glycerine 8.P., and Peppermint Water to mak., six ounces in all. Mix an-I shake in a Large bottle, and take two teaspoonfuls aft,.!r meals and at bedtime. Nothing simpler, surely ; yet the change wrought by this pleasant home mine iy is well-nigh magical. Special dieting and torturing exercises are out of it. This mixture is everything; it will give you a good appetite and strengthen your digestive powers ; the surplus fat will melt away at a rapid rete (some- times as Inur..ll as 18oz. in a day), and in a feA weeks your weight will ba normal and your health robust.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>St. Clem3nt's Mission Bible Class</head>
        <p>St. Clem3nt's Mission Bible Class</p>
        <p>The first annual dinner organized by the members of the St. Clement's Young Men's Bible Class was held at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street,and the gathering proved very successful. The Rev. 'l'. W. Barber, who presided at the meeting held after dinner, expressed regret that their teacher (qr. G. H. W. Steel) was absent through ill-health. He eulogized the work of Mr. Steel, and said he felt very prowl of him. lie (Mr. limber) was very proud of the Bible clSss. A musical programme was gone through. Mr. J. Proverbs rendered two violin solos, entitled" La Verginella "and Bercense," which were much appreciated. Slugs were contributed .by Mrs. A. Proverbs, the Rev. T. W. Birlmr, an I Messrs. O. U.Ahaw, C. .1. Sheath, and N. Millington. Two recitstious were given by Mr. R. Slevin and J. Shaw, entitled " Christmas Day in the Workhouse " and " Football." A song by Messrs. J. and Jack Gray," A song of songs," was the pick of the evening programme. Mr. F. D ivies was the accompanist. General toasts were proposed and responded to, that of " The health of the teacher, Mr. G. H. Steel," being received with musical honours.</p>
      </div>
