      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>f4_,X A MINE K.</head>
        <p>THE f4_,X A MINE K.</p>
        <p>JA.;I/47UARY 3Jtb, 1909.</p>
        <p>Sir FREDERICK My E's RADIU)I. recent lecture on the</p>
        <p>curative properties of racUurn was speedily fol:lowed by an announcement which will give a new and permanent distinction to the present reign. Both the KING and the QUEEN seem to desire to be remembered, first of all, in connection with what they have done for the relief of human suffering. It was the QI:EEN who, ES Princess of Wales, installed the Finsen light at the London Hospital at her own expense. She had become acquainted with tile wonderful properties of this new means of healing during one of her visits to her former home in Copenhagen. She found that it could cure many of the otherwise hopeless kinds of disease,such as elephantiasis, for instance, and she at once placed it within the reach of the poor in the most crowded districts of London. And this is only one of the numerou3 ways in which the QUEEN has proved her constant solici- tude for, and her determination actively to help, ' those who endure and suffer. The KING, for his part, has been unremittingly eager to assist every variety of curative work ; and especially the fund which he established as Prince of Wales, and which has saved some of the hospitals from ruin and others from chronic financial troubles, will be a monument outlasting the art of statuary, probably equalling in duration the work of the I the historian. It is characteristic of his Majesty that he should so readily have given the stamp of Royal approval to the new Radium Institute which I is to be founded by means of funds supplied by Sir ERNUT CAsskr. and a gentleman who desires to remain unknown.</p>
        <p>What radium may be capable of in the way of</p>
        <p>curative treatment cannot as yet even be surmised. But we already know of some wonderful things which it has done. It will, for example, remove exterior malformations and discolourations which have defied the efforts of surgery. This we learn on the authority of so brilliant a surgeon as Sir FREDERICK TREVES. The "strawberry mark" need no longer be a permanent disfigurement. Cures that were simply marvellous have been effected in the case of " growths " that have been operated upon in vain. But there is a great realm of discovery that is still explored. If ever a universal cure should come to light, it seems not improbable that the use of radium will be the general panacea. There are, however, many reasons why rapid progress cannot be made with , the work of experiment. The chief of these is the [enormous coat of radium itself. Sir FREDERICK TREVES exhibited the other day a small tube of this precious substance. Its value was £BOO. I Should radium ever come to be worth only its weight in gold, he said, it will be entitled to be considered cheap. As things are at present there are the very limited opportunities of making investigation into the uses of this most wonderful metal iu connection with medicine and surgery. We doubt whether, at the present moment, the scientific men of all the world have much more than a pound of radium at their disposal. Thel establishment of a Radium Institute may bestow incalculable benefits on mankind. It will, at any rate, open out, in a quite unexpected way, the ifield of experiment and discovery. There seems, on a review of the facts, to be k something almost miraculous in the advances which have been made in medicine and surgery during the last half-century or so. There was really no great amount of exaggeration in the pictures of medical men drawn by GIL BLAS—of the doctor who bled his patients to death and the doctor who starved them. It was not quackery so much as ignorance. Surgery was simply brutal because it had not discovered any but brutal methods. The light broke in all at once, or, at any rate, within the space of a not very long lifetime. First of all, there came the discovery of effective anaeithetics,with the consequent abolition, during the period of operation, of all pain. Even then, however, there remained the imminent danger of blood-poisoning, and, in fact, nearly two out of three of those who were operated upon in serious cases died of the subsequent effects. Then I came Lord LISTER'S great discovery of the antiseptic treatment, which has saved unnumbered thousands of lives, and relieved more agony, it is probable, than has the use of the aumithetics. We , are living now in the time of the X rays, of the Finsen light, of radium AS a curative agent. A man now seems unduly incredulous if he doubts the possibility of any discovery whatever. Certainly we are far from being near the end.</p>
        <p>It is not vety comforting to CRIME IN reffect that crime is on the WARRINGXON. incretse in Warrington, but</p>
        <p>‘„ such is the deduc'ion to be drawn from the annual report of the Chief Con- stable (Mr. M. Nicholls) just issued. The number of crimes reported during the year 1908 was 264, an increase of 116 as compared with the figures of the previous year. Fur these offences. 157 persons were proceeded against, an advance of 43 Simple and other larcenies easily top the list of. crimes, 211 cases being reported and 136 persons brought. before the magistrates. There is an exceptionally large number of shopbreaking cases —2B—but the Chief Constable points out that DO fewer than 16 of these were traced to one prisoner who was committed for trial and convicted. The value of the property reported stolen or fraudulently detained, amounted to £576 10s. 9d. of which £257 2s. 101. was recovered. One of the most regret cable fear urea of the report is the increase in juvenile crime. Thirty-nine offenders (sixteen years of ago and under) charged with theft were apprehended during the year, an increase of 17 as coi-npared with 1907. The number of persons proceeded against for note- dictable often, es tried summarily by the magistrates was 1,222, of which number 530 were apprehended and 681 summoned. As compared with the figures of the previous year there was an increase of 23 in the number of persons arrested, tied a decrease of 163 in the number of persons, sum- moned. The increase in the apprehensions was ceased through a greeter number of assaults upon the police and of charges under the Vagrancy Act. It is a rather surprising fact that the number of persons proceeded against simply for drunkenness (308) showed a decrease of 22. This figure, however, does not cover all the cases dealt with, as in several instances of men charged with drunkenness they ware also accused at the same time of a more serious cfience,and consequently they are classified under different headings. The authorised strength of the Police Force is 80 of all ranks and the gross total cost is £9,151 3 91 as against £10,104 12s. Bd. in 1907. Salaries and pay show a decrease of £193, clothing and accoutrements have cost £l5B more while the expenditure on rent, rates, taxes, purchase of buildings, 84.. e, has dropped to the extent of £BBO.</p>
        <p>-- The tendency in trade of THE Sitsu, recent years has beer TRADER'S FIGHT, almost directly toward, consolidation. Wheneve at has i'Per possible the big man or the syndicate actiog as grower, producer, manufacturer, and dealer has been able to undercut the small man in any of the departments of his tra le, and to give him less and less possibility of livelihood. The small man has struggled hard to retain his independence, but it was feared he would have to capitulate. It looks, however, as if he has at the _ last moment 'come upon a plan lay</p>
        <p>which it has dsveloped to its huge size by the expository accretions of the Northern School. The - work claims to be Buddha's "Three Baskets," but the thirt y-oce books of the Southern canon have grown to over a hundred, and each book in the Thibetan version consiste of over a thousand pages. Each volume weighs 10Ib. After the Kah-gyur there come other 225 volumes,called the Tangyur, which are desirable commentaries. Outside the Tan-gynr, it is a relief to know the Thibetan will tell you, there is net much left that, is worth knowing. Translation and collation may prove a great work for some Oxford scholar. Seine portions of the book will be worth rendering into English—with yet more notes. It includes the apocryphal gospel on Transcendental Wisdona, and much else that is curious and instructive. It must necessarily be that a book which takes twelve oxen to carry about has a great deal in it, but the value of this authorised copy of the Kah-gyur is that it is the Thibetan canon. I should be highly instructive to us on that account. The Thibetans generally regard it with too: much reverence to think of reading it, and it is said to be hignly ufficacious, drawn round the field in a bullock waggon, to ensure that the crops grow properly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Horsemarket-street between Market Gate and Lyme-street is to be paved with wood blocks For this relief much thanks! If the improvement were only extended as far as Bewsey-street it would be substantial and greatly appreciated in such a, busy and uneven thoroughfare.</p>
        <p>* * * Next week will see the advent in our midst of that composer of colour, Mr. S. Coleridge-Taylor. We do not mean that be composes anything but music, his colour being the of Ethic:l;i= sort. Coleridge-Taylor's renown is of a two-fold kind. He is one of the most original geniuses, and being a black man he is a standing refutation of that theory of intellectual inferiority upon which much of the race prejudice held by "whites " is based. The musician who could write " Onaway beloved ! " needs no further credentials for his right to be included amongst the great men of our time.</p>
        <p>The French are a peculiar people. They abolished capital punishment some time ago for humanitarian reasons, but they have been compelled to revive " Madame Guilliotine " owing to the outburst of murder and other horrible crimes. Most people will agree that there was reason for this btep, but where the French have gone wrong has been in alloWing recent executions to be public. The people of Bethune and Carpentras, where executions have just been held, seized upon the occasion as one for hold- ing a carnival of blasphemous rejoicings which accords ill with the boasted civilization of France. If they have got to cut off criminals' heads,they should do it decently and in private.</p>
        <p>- * * * Lord Avebury contributes an article entitled "What is Socialism ? " to the first number of the Anti-Socicaiet. "It is difficult," he writes, "to say what Socialism really is, for Socialists differ so much amongst themselves, but, as I understand, they have two great objects:— Firstly to divide all property from time to time amongst all members of the community; and Secondly —to support all forms of municipal and national enterprise in substitution for private _ enterprise.</p>
        <p>I do not kno-w whether the division under the first head is to be confined to our own countrymen, or whether it is to extend to the whole world ; nor, as far as I know, are Socialists agreed how often the process is to take place." * * * _ _ _</p>
        <p>It is surprising what a variety of services,in addition to their ordinary duties, the police are called upon to perform. During the year 1908, the Warrington police restored to their parents 160 children who were found wandering in the streets, and conveyed to the pin fold 20 horses, cattle, sheep, ctc., found straying on the highways. Ninety stray dogs were seized, of which number 48 were destroyed and 42 restored to their owners. Fifty-five collisions and accidents caused by runaway horses tic., during the year were reported to the police and assistance ren- dered on each occasion. Our local custodians of the peace have also delivered no fewer than 6,737 circulars and notices intimating time and date of Corporation committee meetings &amp;c., and over 3,500 notices have been sent out to magistrates intimating busineas for disposal at the Court. • * _ _ _</p>
        <p>We notice a minute of the health Committee which reads as follows : "That the Sanitary Works Com- mittee be requested to meet this Committee to discuss the whole question of the collection of nightsoil." We hope the deliberations of the two Committees will have some effect in reducing the various nuisances which the Sanitary Department regularly commits in the course of its collection of nightsoil. There is no more abominable public nuisance in the town than that created by the passing along the streets of the nauseating nightsoil vans, and even the fitting of rubber tyres to the wheels which one Councillor suggested, would not lessen the trouble to any appreciable extent. One's ears might Molerate the noise if one's olfactory nerves were not so grossly offended. At its best it is an archaic system, but we suppose it cannot be abolished until the town sewers are re-constructed. Their already congested state prevents the general adoption of the water carriage system, which offers the only real cure for the existing evil. * * _ _</p>
        <p>It is a scriptural injunction that we shall " bless and curse not," but oft times when the carol singer comes a-carolling at Christmastide we feel more inclined to curse than to bless. Of course, much depends on the quality of the carolling, and more (in the way of contributions) would depend on the destination of the funds if we always knew for certainty. Some of the carolling is on right and proper lines. For instance the Choir and Wesley Guild members of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church have made a practice in recent years of carol-singing at Christmas time for charitable objects. During the season just passed they collected £l7, and the money was expended last Saturday evening in giving a tea and entertainment to 1,200 poor children in the Silver- street school. Three years ago these self-sacrificing carollers gave a treat to 500 children, the following year to 800, and now in addition to the increasingly large number catered for in the way of food and entertainment, a portion of the money has been avail- able for the provision of clothing for the poorest of the poor mites to whom the joys of Christmastide are, as a rule, merely fiction.* * * Our neutral contemporary.</p>
        <p>as usual, gave a huge</p>
        <p>- which he will soon be able to attack as well as report last Saturday olthe srnallTsriff Reform League defend. He is going to try what consolidation meeting held in the National Schools early in the week. can do for him. It is remarkable that the ides ! Councillor Broadhurst preached a long sermon about has not come to him before, it seenss SO simple and the distress and unemployment which existed in the so obvious as it is explained in an article con town, and for which he offered Tariff Reform as the tributed to the " Magazine of Commerce." The unfailing cure. We wonder his audience does not scheme first takes shape in Berlin, but no doubt get sick of such sophistry, when it well knows the lead will very soon be followed in other towns. that the same evils are just as rife in coun- An immense arcade market-hall, the largest in the tries which have the boon and blessing of world, has been built, and is now open to contain scientific tariffs. Conditions are even worse in these a number of independent shops. In the centre countries, for living is poorer and the necessaries of of the Ksufhaus is a central cffice, controlled by fife dearer. Free Trade has not yet brought us the .black bread. and horse beef which form staple foods the individual shopkeepers, which will deal with n protected Germany. Councillor Broadhurst says heating, lighting, delivery of goods, banking, safe 1 he has been fighting this fight alone since he was deposits, and those many other common needs fifteen. Ho cannot have absorbed many facts of real which can be served so much more economical.lY-- value in the interval or he would not make such on a large scale. No doubt there are substantial' absurd statements as : "Warrington once produced objections to the scheme, and some huge diffi- hundreds of thousands of pieces of fustian per week." culties will be experieuced as it settles into He might well ask " Where is the trade now ? " We extensive working order; but its advantages should like him to prove that Warrington ever pro. declare themselves conspicuously. At worst, the duced hundreds of thousands of pieces of fustian per independence everyone prizes will be retained ; at; week. People in the trade marvel at his colossal best, it may be the beginning of the trim indivi- ' assumption of knowledge and reckless use of figures duality which is prominent in every dreamer's ; which he does not understand. ideals.</p>
        <p>-- A good deal of talk has ' runner Warrington Curate's Promotion. THIBETAN been created by the arrival 1 The Rev. A. S. Cameron, vicar of Weston, near SCRIPTURES in England of a complete Spalding, has been appointed by Lord Lilford to the IN ENGLAND. set, in 103 volumes, of the living of Atherton Parish Church, vacant by the Kah-gyur, or Thibetan resignation of the Rey. W. Nuttall. Mr. Cameron,who version of the Buddhist scriptures. The "Times" will commence his new duties next month, held now states that this monumental book is the miracles at Grassendale and Warrington before going property of Lord Ffrench. The Dalai Lama,whgn to Weston. he went to Pekin, took with him two sets of this i • precions work, giving one of them to the Diwager- I OUGHT ITO USN GrAssgs.—ln order to answer this question it is accessary to test the sight. This Is Empress. The other Lord Ffrench now has. It is done and all information given willingly. JOHNSTON printed from carved wooden Thibetan blocks, and WATSON, Eyesight Testing Rooms, 84, Bridge-s treat, is the authorised edition of 1731 printtd at Warrington. Hours of Business: 9.30 to 7.30. Johnston Narthan in Western Thibet. Ode of'. the Watson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to curious interests of the work is the way in 4.30, Wednesday 11 to 6.80. Thurday 11 to 1, and . Friday and Satur4ay 11 to s.3o.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Wycliffe Juvenile Rechabites.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Juvenile Rechabites.</p>
        <p>A successful concert in connection with the Wycliffe Juvenile Tent of Recha')ites was held on Monday evening, the Rev. John Yonge presiding over a; large attendance. An interestimy, programme was submitted, including songs by Ella Morley,Ethel Lowe and Doris Green, trios by E. Morley, J. Warbrick, and E. Lowe, recitations by Gladys Molyneaux, Charles Molyneaux, Jane Warbrick, Charles Bradley and Eva Nunnerley. All the children acquitted themselves very creditably. Daring an interval the Rev. John Yonge distributed the prizes and commented upon the progress of the tent, which has now a membership of seventy. A framed emblem was presented to Thomas Higham for his services in the various offices, and:a sash to the treasurer (Bro. Wakefield).</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENI:FIT.-lINDBB. TIM CLOCK FOR BIZST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRIL—Advt. Sankey Bridges _ Wesley _Guild. Festival. _</p>
        <p>Special services in connection with the above guild were held on Sunday last. The preacher morning and evening was the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees (formerly of this circuit)and both services were very well attended by in. Any of Mr. Rees's old friends in the circuit. During the afternoon a musical service was held, presided over by Mr. G. Weaver. A short address was given by the Rev. E. B. Keeling. Special anthems were rendered by the choir and solos by Mrs. Taylor and Messrs. E. Walker, W Ireland and J.E.H innan ;cornet solo : by Mr. W. Rutter. A recitation entitled " The Way to ,Heaven " was given by Mrs. Highley. —Special collections were taken daring the day on behalf of the Guild Funds.— On Monday evening a lecture on " The Bunyan of Wales" was given by Mr. Rees, in which he portrayed many of the struggles and triumphs of one of the greatest preachers of the principality, namely, Christmas Evans.—A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Lecturer and Dr. Bennett, who presided. LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coqrrhs and "Colds. Of proven efficacy. 9ad. Stls.lid. Of Chemists only.—Advt. ANNUAL SALE of Tea Services. Dinner Services, etc., great bargains.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge street, Warrington.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. Meetings.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the Wycliffe Church P.S.A. Society was held on Sunday and was very well attended. The Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) presided and spoke upon " The objects and aims of the brotherhood." The first object was to bring men to Christ and the second to make good men, happy fathers and faithful souls. They had always kept that to the front. When a song was introduced it was to drive home both music and words. A short time ago a song was rendered in that church that in iyed him to the very depths of his soul. At a conference of P.S.A. Societies of Lancashire and neighbour- ing counties yesterday, continued the speaker, a stirring and interesting address was given by Mr. William Ward author of "How can I help England." At the close of his address he (the secretary) asked him to give the conference what his own experience was in visiting the P.S.A.'s all over the country and abroad, with respect to the semi-concert. He said he was delighted the question had been asked. The P.S.A. men and men did not want the concerts. They could have them any day. What they want was a good stirring gospel address, and where the concert element had been tried it soon came to grief. When solos were given they were intended to draw out the best in the brotherhood and the address was to lead heavenward. Mr. Hitnsworth gave an address upon " Playing the fool." Mr. Joseph Owen took part in the meeting. Mr. R. Wood sang effectively " The soft*Southern breeze" and "The sailor's grave."</p>
        <p>Ai ARTICLE no housewife should be without.— See special offer for six weeks in Miscellaneous Column.--Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATIFSISAD PLIGHT.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATIFSISAD PLIGHT.</p>
        <p>The Swing Bridge 0n Strike.</p>
        <p>For nearly three hours last evening the owing bridge at Stockton Heath was not available for road traffic. Shortly after 5.30 the bridge was opened to allow a vessel to pass but, owing to some hitch in the mechanism, it could not be s‘vung back again and in consequence great inconvenience was caused to pedestrians and to passengers by the tram can from Warrington which could not proceed beyond Stafford-road. The 5.30 car from Stockton Heath had just got over the bridge when the accident occurred. Not until 8.10 was the bridge righted and traffic resumed as usual. Meanwhile the execrations of the Heathens filled the air. Luckily the swing bridge offends but rarely in this way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LIBERAL NIEETIM AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL NIEETIM AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>A public meeting promoted by the Knntsford Divi, sion Liberal Association was held in the Hall Cafe Stockton Heath, last evening. There was a large and enthusiastic gathering.. Mr. Josiah Longland presided and was supported by Mr. A. J. King, M.P., Mr. Hay Morgan, M.P. for the Truro Division of Cornwall, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, W. H. Sherburn, E. Lloyd Jones, H. Congreve, Will Ford (agent), and others. A letter of apology for non-attendance was received from the Rev. J. Yonge. Mr. King moved a vote of confidence in the Govern- ment. He said he had no apology to make for them, for they had not spared themselves in their work for the people. He instanced the Postmaster General's revision of the wages of postmen and the recognition of their trade union. Alluding to Old-sge Pensions, Mr. King described the attitude of Tories on the question, some saying that people were dead before they reached the age of Seventy; yet 600,000 of them were in receipt of pensions. With regard to the Miners' Eight Hours Bill, he ridiculed statements of the Coal Consumers' League who oppoLed the Bill and said it would raise prices ss. per ton. How could it when the highest price paid was 45. 6d. and in other cases lower than 2s. 6d. ? The Liberals intended getting revenue from those who could afford to pay it. (Applause). __ _</p>
        <p>Flay Morgan complimented the Knutsford Division on having such a hard-workinz Member and urged them to retain his services. Deal- ng with the fiscal question he slid that if Tariff Reform would do what Was claimed for it—find work for all the unemployed—he would be a tariff reformer to-morrow, but it was because he could clearly see that Tariff Reform so far from increasing employment would decrease it, that he was a Free Trader. A tax upon an article decreased the consumption and, in consequence, the production of that article and reduced the amount of labour required to satisfy the demands. Tariff Ref'am was no cure at all. The Liberals would very likely introduce some scheme of afforestation but he relied more particularly upon the taxation of land values. He believed that if they could get that, they would increase employment by leaps and bounds. He hoped Mr. Lloyd-George would have the opportunity of showing the country that Liberals were really in earnest ou these foundation problems. (Applause.) Tariff Reform., he contended, would be the shortest out to the disrup- tion and disintegration of the Empire. The ideal of the Liberal party, so far from imposing extra burdens on the toilers of the country, was to make the conditions of their life happier and their burdens lighter. (Applause.) The vote of confidence was heartily agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE</p>
        <p>" Peter's Mother."</p>
        <p>There should be crowded houses at the Theatre next week, when the famous comedy, " Peter's Mother" will he played by Miss Emma and Mr. Percy Hutchison's London Company, including Miss Violet Ley as " Peter's Mother." The comedy is charmingly written with a good subject, interesting characters, amusing lines, and a pretty sentiment. Other members of this capable company are Mr. Drew Mackintosh, and Mr. Vivian Gilbert, while the same scenery will be used as was shown in the perform- ance of the comedy before the King and Queen. "THE LADY OF OSTEND." -- Dramatic Society's Forthcoming Performances.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Dramatic Society will present Sir Francis 13arnand's celebrated farcical comedy in three acts, "The Lady of Ostend" at the Royal Court Theatre during the week commencing February Bth. The proceeds are to be devoted to three excellent institutions—the Warrington Infirmary, District Nursing Association, and Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade—and we hope the enter- prise will receive the generous support of the towns- people. The rehearsals give promise of an admirable presentation of the play, which is one of the most laughable imaginable.. The cast includes Mr. H G. Mosley, Mr. Sydney Wallhead, Mr. H. M. Ashton Ur. W. H. Brown, Mr. R. P. Johnson, Mr. J. Craik, Mut; May Morrison, Mrs. Jennings, Miis Dorothy Browne, Miss Bessie Forshaw, Mies Helen G. Wright. The plan of dress and reserved seats will be opened at the theatre box office to-day (Saturday).</p>
        <p>UNSHED COMPOUND with warm water is an oxeellent gargle for sore throat.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNE</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNE</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB. The annual meeting of members was held on Monday evening, Mr. John Ford, chairman of the com- mittee, presiding. The annual report showed a defici- ency on the year's working of £1 12s. 6d., due to the members seeking more out-door recreation during the summer months than usual. The debt on the club was now £lB5 19s. 3d. The report was adopted. The officers were appointed as follow :—President, the Right Hon. Sir John T. Brunner, M.P.; vice-presidents. Alderman Norman, Messrs. S. Taylor, F. Boston, F. J. Nosman, J. M. Frith, G. Bellamy, and R. H. Posnett treasurer, Mr. E. R. Pearson ; secretary, Mr. Alfred Riley : auditors, Messrs. Harry Wright and Garnett Hough ; committee, Messrs. J. Ford, A. Ravenscroft, W.B.Brimelove, C. Woodward, B. Jolley, V.H. Ceuth, S. Watton, Walter Riley, S. Hopkins, W. Cubley, W. Leathwood, Joseph Hughes, and John Clare, EMBEZZLENIENT AT RUNCORN'. At Runcorn, on Thursday, before Mr. E. Imison and Dr. M'Dougall, John Dickson, a married man, of Halton-road, traveller in the employ of Edward Monks, Hazeldene, Weaverham, Northwich, trading as a grocer and provision dealer at Bridge-street, Runcorn, was charaed with embezzling three sums of £6 7s. 9d., £3 3s. and £4 4s.—The prisoner had been under remand at Knutsford Gaol for a week.—Mr. Monks said the prisoner had been in his employ for five years • pre- viously he was at Price's stores at Acton iridge. Prisoner had to go into the country for a radius of about ten miles in Cheshire to solicit orders from farmers and others. It was his duty to collect accounts and pay in the money each day. He had received the following accounts and not accounted for them : September 3rd,£9 Is. 2d. from Fred Crowther,baker and confectioner,Halton-road ; £3 3s. from Annie Elizabeth Jones, shopkeeper, Halton; and £4 4s. from Mrs. Elizabeth Timperley, Egerton-street. In consequence of what came to his knowledge on January 21st he had an interview with the prisoner, who confessed he had done wrong, and furnished him with a list of accounts he had collected and not paid.—ln reply to the magis- trates, Mr. Monks said some customers took from a month to six months' credit. If persons had been behind he had asked the prisoner about them, and he 'nad always been able to furnish some excuse, Cash received from one customer the prisoner had credited to another customer. It was ascertained that the prisoner had embezzled considerably over £2oo.—The prisoner pleaded guilty.—By Dr. 111'Dougall : He paid him £2 ss. a week and expenses. He had not the slightest idea what company the prisoner kept. He had no proof that his habits were irregular, but he had heard so.—Superintendent Bunion said the prisoner was in the habit of going down to Liverpool.—Mr. Imison (to Mr. Monks) : Can you tell us how long he has been embezzling this money ?a-From his own state- ment, it will be from three to four years.—He was sentenced to four months with hard labour. LIBERAL MEE rING AT RUNCORN. SPEECHES BT MR. J. F. L. BRUNNER AND MR. H. VIVIAN. At Runcorn, on Wednesday night, a public meeting was held under the auspices of the Northwich Division Liberal Association, when the principal speakers were Mr. J. F. L. Brunner, M.P. and Mr. H. Vivian, M.P. Mr. Williarn Carroll presided. Replying to a resolution of confidence in the Govern- ment, Mr. Brunner dealt at length with its legislative achievements, and referring to the House of Lords said both Conservative and Liberal speakers and writers were agreed that something must be done to reform that august chamber. Even the Lords themselves thought that something must be done, and appointed a Commission, with Lord Rosebery as chairman. That commission published a report; and how any man wishing to preserve his reputation for commonsense could have produced - such a report, he did not understand. Personally he did not believe in a second Chamber. He thought it was a nuisance and prevented progress. It was argued, however, that if the Socialists were in a majority in the House of Commons, a second Chamber would be necessary ; but all he could say was that if a Socialist Government was elected we should deserve it. If they were to have a second Chamker at all, let it play fair. Let it be a second Chamber for the Tory Government, as well as a second Chamber for the Liberals. Speak- ing in regard to Tariff Reform, Mr. Brunner said Pro- tection meant death to the Lancashire cotton trade. As to the work of the Government in the future, he said the present poor-law system would have to be dealt with, or the administration as at present carried out was hated by the poor. In conclusion, Mr. Brunner said he felt sure that Mr. Lloyd George would bring in a democratic Budget. Mr. Vivian dealt with the question of Free Trade and Protection, and said the Tariff Reformers made speeches to suit the localities in which they were. In Bermondsey they would tax imparted leather, but in Leicester and Northampton they would not do so, because it was the raw material for the trade of the district. In fact, Tariff Reform was one long compaign of lying and fraud. (Applause.) We could not taa ourselves into prosperity, because all trade was in the nature of an exchange. If they adopted Tariff Reform they would not add to wealth at all. It was simply juggling with taxes, and taking money out of one man's pocket and putting it into another man's. Tariff Reform meant reform for the rich, but drudgery and higher prices for the poor. If they kept to Free Trade they would protect the poor and protect their political life. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>FOUND HANGED. A tragic discovery was made in Havelock-street, St. Helens, on Monday evening, when it was found that Joseph Hopper, aged eighteen, had hanged himself behind one of the doors of his house. When discovered he was immediately cut down, but was quite dead. Hopper, it appears, had been out of work since August, but for some little time past had been wcasioaally employed at a local hairdresser's establishment.</p>
        <p>_ _ FATRL FALL FRO 4 A TRANICXR. INQUEST ON A PRESCOT lAN. At St. Helen.'' on Saturday, Ar. Brighouse held the inquest on Williana James Lawronson, aged thirty- seven, of 23, Derby-street, Prescot, who died in tho Providence Hospital on Wednesday afternoon, apparently from injuries inflicted by falling off a tram- car.</p>
        <p>William Doming, glass-flattener, of Gladstone-street, said that at 2.25 on Wednesday afternoon he went out of the end of the short street by the Eculeston Arms on to Prescot-road. He saw a tramcar coma down the road, and deceased was trying to get off it. He dropped off the footboard, but kept his hand on the rail too long, and was dragged off his feet. Witness picked him up, when he said." I didn't think it was going sn fast." With the help of another man he put him against the rails at the side of the road. Several other people calne up. Deceased looked shaken, but witness thought he would come round all right, and as his wife was waiting for him he joined her and went on. Joseph Mousdell, the tram conductor, said he did not see the man getting off the car at all. The place where he attempted to get off was a" stop-'oy-reliest" station, but he was never asked to stop.</p>
        <p>Dr. A. S. Wilson, of Prescot-road, 'said that there was a fracture of Lawrenson's skull, beginning at a wound on the ileft side above the ear, and death had been caused by the formation of a large blood clot near the fracture. A verdict of" Accidental death" was return3d. PARK SUPERINTnIDENr'S DEATH. FATAL MISTAKE IN DRUGS.</p>
        <p>An inquest on Edward Pye, superintendent of the St Helens Corporation Park, who died on Monday morning was held on Wednesday. Deceased was sixty-fire years of age, and lived at the Victoria Park. Mrs. Pye said that her husband had in his possession a large quantity of corrosive sublimate, which was used for killing worms, Szc., on the bowling greens in the park. He had been suffering from nervous debility for nearly a year, and had been attended by Dr. Unsworth. Mrs. Pye found her husband lying on the hearth-rug in the parlour crying out. She asked him what was the matter, and he said, " I have take corrosive sublimate in mistake • for Gregory'st powder." She went into the kitchen and found the cupboard open where deceased kept his grub killer and other substances, as well as Gregory's p)rvdar. There was a bottle of Gregory's powder and another on the ledge of the cupboard, and she in her agitation took up the bottle and cup and threw them into the ashpit. She sent for Dr. Unsworth, and the doctor said Pye did :alt tell him how he came to take sublimate. Dssth was due to exhaustion' following the taking of pois)a. The Coroner: If we had seen the two bottles and seen that. they were both alike, and that the contents looked similar, it would have helped us very consider- ably to what we want to know. Were both bottles exactly alike. Mrs. Pye.—Not quite, but very nearly so. Tha Gregory powder showed so plainly through the glass that I knew the difference between the two. Dr. Unsworth said that the corrosive sublimate was evidently mixed with something, and it was a dirty reddish-brown colour. Pye said that after the 6th inst. deceased became a little better, but late last week he got into a serious state, and died on Nionday morning. . Dr. Unsworth said that deceased was mentally sane up to the time when he began to sink and die. The Foreman said he understood that deceased was in no trouble of any sort with regard to his employment, and he lived happily. A verdict of " Death from misadventure" was returned. ST. HELENS DE kLER SUNINIONED. At St. Melons, on Monday, James G slway, of Waterloo-street, was summoned on four inform stiona by the Corporation for dealing in second-hand goads without a licence; two charges of purchasing a less quantity of brass than authorised ; aud purchasing a less quantity of lead than authorised. Mr. Lindon giley, instructed by kissers. Barrow and Cook, defended. Mr. Turton, deputy town-clerk, prosecuted. He said that between August lath last year and the beginning of this year Galway bought and sold quantities of old metals of all sorts and kinds, and also rabbit skins. Defendant had beets licensed , butfor otrtain reasons</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>,_ , * . &lt; :7_ libiligi. `--,:,-7,-,..,_ -• .</head>
