      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>licence was not renewed. Since that time Galway bad deliberately set the police at defiance. Uuder these circumstances he asked for the maximum penalty of R2O. Detective Anders gave evidence that on four days he Watched defendant go to a number of shops and buy rabbit skins. He agreed that Galway probably pur- chased 300 dozen rabbit skins per week, and that he sold six tons of salt a week. On December 12th Galway spoke to him and asked what the game was following him about all round the town. He said he Would compel him to toll him, and complained that he Was being persecuted, not prosecuted. Inspector Dunn said that Galway was refused a licsence in 1904. He then got a man named Rigby to &amp;et a licence for Galway's premises. Rigby then lost his licence. He then went on to the premises of a man named Eden, and Eden lost his business. Then the Whole of the business was transferred to Clarke's yard in Homer-street, and Clarke left his license in March, 1908. Witness: He has been collecting rabbit skins this Morning. Mr. Riley: He intends to go on collecting them Ur. Turton : That is what I want to show. Ur. Riley aeked for a definition of secondhand, which Witness quoted from a dictionary. Mr. Riley argued that rabbit skins could not be called second-hand goods, aid asked, " Are e to understand that the moment the skin goes to anyone except the lo,an who has flayed the rabbit it becomes second-hand?" • (laughter.) Further, Mr. Riley asked how it was that, although by the informations the police knew that 'knee July 29tn, Galway had been continuously breaking the Act of Parliament, they had not issued summonses ~11 the matter until Galway was on the point of going "' Wigan, and had taken premises, and wit* applying for licence. Galway was fined £2O and £3 costa for acting as a second-hand dealer, and the other charges were then proceeded with. The other charges were dismissed on the payment of costs.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON. -- ACCIDENT TO A BURTONWOOD MAN. Albert Naylor (4)), of Tanhouse-lane, Burtonwood, met with a serious accident at the Bold Collieries on Thursday. It appears that the man was in a motion brow, and when he was ascending the incline some boxes of coal began to come down, ai,d Naylor scrambled on top of the boxes, but when they arrived at a low part of the roof he was severely injured, and was conveyed to the St. Helens Hospital sullering from broken ribs and severe internal injuries. EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT CANINE SOCIETY. The above society held their fourth members' show on Thursday evening in the Town Hall. Confined to a radius of twenty miles, this young society are making capital headway, and it is not surprising either, when for an entrance fee of ls. 6d. a first prize to the value of 4s. and a second 9s. 6d. is offered for eompetition. All dogs, except those exhibited by the children, were benched, and a fair muster of spectators turned up and Showed keen interest in the judge's work. A nice round number in entries was received for Mr. Tom Lawson to assort, viz., 190, and some good specimens were on view. Two classes were arranged for children, one for boys and the other for girls. The Challenge Cup and gold ~„raccial for the best slog in the show went to 'a Newfoundland (St. Helens), whilst Laindon Lovat (Rock Ferry), a Scottish terrier, was reserve, and carried oft the silver medal. n MR. SEDDON M.P.'S STEWARDSHIP. Thursday night last week Mr. Seddon addressed tiarge and enthusiastic meeting in the National school, ainhill. Councillor Bartell Fletcher (St. Helens) presided , and Mr. W. Forshaw spoke at some length, ontrasting the work of the present Parliament with toTl,37.ears Tory blundering. Mr. Seddon gave an account .enrceoing ciL?,la stewardship, as member for the division. He rwl'ELrr in detail with the Old Age Pensions Act, and ha to Lord Balfour of Burleigh receiving an Was me of over 20,000 a year, he said that though he neither old nor poor, yet as an ex-Cabinet minister took • f £2,000 a year, without • a pension o tion „h; Ping through the degrading examina- Next --'e" he claimants for is. a week had to face. able cited the Miners' Bill, and 6aid it was notice- __ that Tory members representing mining constitn- retes steadily voted in favour of it, while the Tory arty as a body steadily opposed it, and Lord Newton lle "early kid it. He spoke on the Children's Bill, and f_nade out an excellent case for the Bill as being better fo_r:licence-holders and the public generally than the Bill of the Tory Government, which was unjust and dis- tributed comp.nsation inequitably. The coming .aSssion they were told was to be 0110 of confiscation. lirrade had shrunk and unemployment was rife, yet Mr. 'Lloyd-George had got to find money for old age Pensions, increases in Naval expenditure, and to make !IP the loss in national revenue. There were many forms of taxation which might be utilised, but the Tory party suggested Tariff Reform, which would increase the cost of living by indirect taxation. This he dis- approved of, and declared that Tariff Reform would not r good, but harm, in Lancashire.—The vote of confi- dence was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Pound" Service at St. Paul's Church. s the services at this church on Sunday were on behalf o wrhichhe Chahsurachhooml Secretary, e j E ungland Waifs and Strays Society, Bri ku-Iset rite eet;. WEI! g F.st.o Collier.n T he preacher was the . who, in the course of his sermon in the evening, called attention to the excellent manner in which St. George's Horne is conducted. What is known as a "pound service" was held in the afternoon when the children of the congregation and others brought pounds of tea, Auger, rice, coffee, , and these were placed inside the communion rails 'and afterwards sent to the home. The collections realized about £ll. 80ld-street Wesleyan Church. The evangelistic services held immediately after the i ord --aarY Sunday services have proved so successful that they are now being held in the Church instead of in the schoolroom. There was again a large attendance O n Sunday evening, the Rev. T. Miller conducting the proceedings. A solo was well rendered by Mr. H. 'eluropton entitled " Behold Me standing at the door," the chorus of which was heartily joined in by the con- gregation. A story of his early life and conversion Was given by Mr. Smiley, who for the past 27 years has endeavoured to do all in his power to spread the glad tidings of salvation. Mr. Wallace and others also took Part in the service, which was brief but encouraging. Baptist Church, Golborne-street. The church anniversary was celebrated at this place Of worship on Sunday. In the morning the Rev. E. E. Letheren announced as his subject "What's the good of ft church le" and his text was from Malachi iii. 14 „...What profit is it that we have kept his His ordinance? " at was a question still asked concerning the service of God. It savoured somewhat of selfishness, but when sincere it demanded a reply. It was not the highest *zeirt of appeal, but it was a bar before which one ought ?lot to be unwilling to plead. A church was a necessity ln the very nature of things according to the laws of thought and action. Common faith created a 00nam0n fellowship. We are so made that we am members one of another. We are called as Christians to the fellowship of Christ. Phristian character could not be developed in isolation. A church justified its existence as an associ- ation for the development of Christian character. It Was for the church to keep Christ's social ideals before Men and to send into the various spheres of the world's 'work men inspired by Christ's spirit and animated with Lis aims. A church owed to its neighbourhood to be the conscience of the community, the dread of evil doers, the ally of all who did well. A gronp of Christians offering itself to Christ was an organ of Christ through Which lie could express tfim i self n His own activities. In the evening Mr. Letheron referred to a recent dis- covery of an anvil used by John Bunyan, and made it the basis of his discourse grouping some very apposite Illustrations suggested by it to drive home the truths he taught. The choir, under the direction of Mr. C. Dawson, rendered an anthem, and Sir. W. Tynan sang, With good effect two solos "If with all your hearts ' 'and Behold Me standing at the door."</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild. Lecture on Dr. Johnson. The Itev. John Gunnell always commands a good attendance when he visits the Bewsey-road Wesley Guild in the Lecture Hall which adjoins the church. On Monday evening there he took as his subject" Dr. Johnson," and had for his Chairman, Mr. John Upson. The Lecturer said that Samuel Johnson was born at Lichfield on September 18th, 1709. In his youth he antlered very severely from several diseases. He con- tinued his education at a danes school, then he went to the Lichfield Gra,umar School. Later he entered New College Oxford, but was unable to stay there very long °wing to his impecnniority. However, whilst only there a short time his mental ability was noticed. It Was said that even when a boy of only seven years of ago somebody read a poem of 16 verses to him, and he leeited it, only making one mistake in a line, which !rOProved the poem itself. Between the ages of 16 and lhe helped his father at his bookshop in Birmingham. e Was a veiacious reader, devouring theology p:iilosophy, poetry and biography, which he laid up in !Ql5 mind for future use. He was startlingly eccentric 111 his manner, and his bearing towards his fellow atadents was very independent. He was as proud as he Za,a Poor, and when somebody thinking they would be °tog him a kindness, placed a pair of boots in his room replace his own worn out ones,he flung them through t!lle.window. It was only poverty that prevented him oarkr.thg his degree. However, he was an honorary L.L.D. to- University. The production of his dictionary srindYears for which he received £1,1575, which ed all expenses. This dictionary contained tO°oo words and was the first dictionary at could be read with any degree of pleasure. pa",°e,n years later the Government recognised his i;;;:tees to the state and granted him a pension of £BOO aro:nun, which he had enjoyed for 27 years. Niae virrs before his death his old University granted him a eo whereby he was able to change his name from thal„ni." Mr. Johnson" to "Dr. Johnson." Votes of were heartily accorded to the Lecturer and the Vkzairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>„:„</p>
        <p>THE ELECTION PETITION. -- Hearing to Commence on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>The petition of Mr. J. A. Rushworth against the return of Councillor Peter Peacock as representative of Orford Ward will be tried on Tuesday at the War- rington Police Court before a Commissioner of the High Court of Justice. The proceedings will commence at 10.30 a.m., and are expected to last about three days. We understand that the petitioner will be repre- sented by Mr. Dickens,K.C. (a son of Charles Dickens, the novelist), Mr. Rigby Swift and Mr. Morris; barristers. The respondent will be represented by Mr. William Madden and Mr. Reece, barristers, Liverpool, instructed by Mr. F. R. Roberts, Warrington. The particulars of the charges against respondent, of bribery and treating, were only received on Wednes- day, so that not much time is available for the preparation of the defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>A TRA.Gic OCcdRRENCE.—The village was much shocked on Thursday afternoon by the news that Mr. George Fowne, of Doe Green, had committed suicide. From the facts at present available it seems that the deceased, who was about 69 years old and was employed at the Tannery, had been ailing for sometime. On Thursday afternoon, between 2 and 3 o'clock, his wife went out to the doctor's, and on her return she learned to her great distress, that deceased had been found hanging in the fowl run belonging to Mr. Joseph Carman, which is near by. Mrs. Carman made the terrible discovery, and when the body was cut down life was found to be extinct. Deceased was a man much respected in the village, and up to the time of his death was a member of the Bible Class conducted by Mr. C. E. Parker at the Wesleyan Church. An inquest will be held at the "Crown and Cushion" Inn at ten o'clock this morning.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT PEOKETH LODGE.—There was great excite- ment at the residence of Mr. C. E. Parker, J.P., on Friday night when it was found that a fire was raging underneath the hearth of the schoolroom. Mr. Parker was at once informed and he took down the mantel- piece. Then it could be seen that the fire was under the hearth and with the help of the coachman the hearth was removed. A beam and several boards under the hearth were found to be on fire, which was soon extinguished with buckets of water. It is fortunate that the fire was discovered before the family had retired to bed,or the affair might have been very serious. TUE BIBLE CLASs.—The Penketh Weileyan Men's Bible Class held their open service on Sunday last in the chapel when a good number attended to bear the Rev. J. F. Matthews, who catue to pay the class his farewell visit before he leaves Warrington. As usual the rev, gentleman gave a most helpful address to those present. Mr. James Dodds occupied the chair and in opening the service spoke in high terms of Mr. Matthews' work in Warrington and district. Miss Hipkiss gave two solos in a manner which did her credit. Mr. Pearce was the accompanist for Miss Hipkiss and Mrs. C. E. Parker played for the singing of the hymns. Mr. C. B. Parker moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Matthews and wished him success in his new sphere at Sheffield. This was seconded by Mr. J. Walters and supported by Mr. Midwood. Mr. Dodds thanked the artiste for her beautiful singing on behalf of himself and those preseut. This brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TARIFF REFORM.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>Challenge to a Debate,</p>
        <p>We have received the following correspondence, which has passed between the Progressive Labour League and the Tariff Reform League : [TO THE EDITOR OF THE WARRINGTON "EXAMINER."] Sir,—We send you herewith copy of a letter which has been forwarded to the Hon. Secretaries of the Warrington Tariff Reform League.—Yours respectfully, J. MORTON 1. Hon. J. HovvEs 5 Secs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>COPY.</head>
        <p>COPY.</p>
        <p>Dear Sirs,—At a meeting ol the Progressive Labour League held last night it was unanimously resolved to invite the Warringtua Tariff Reform League to put forward a representative to meet a nominee of our organisation in public debate in the town, to discuss the relative merits of Free Trade, versus the Tariff Reform programme. If your society is willing to accept this challenge,kindly let us know at your earliest convenience, when the necessary arrangements can at once be made.—Yours respectfully, J. MORTON 1 Hon. J. HOW BS, J CTN. f S ecs Hon. Secs. Tariff R3form League, ‘Varrington.</p>
        <p>P.S.—A copy of this letter will be sot to the Local Press.</p>
        <p>TILE REPLY.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Secretaries, Progressive Labour League, Bewsey-street.</p>
        <p>Dear Sirs,—We are in receipt of your challenge to debate the question of Free Trade versus Tariff Reform with the Progressive Labour League. We are prepared to accept the challenge on the following terms : The Progressive League to nominate three of its own local members to meet three local members of the Warrington Branch of the Tariff Reform League. You as challengers to open the discussion, and the speakers to address the meeting alternately. Each speaker to be limited to fifteen minutes. A Chairman to be nominated who is mutually accept- able.</p>
        <p>No charge to be made for admission. Tickets to be issued and divided equally between the two parties. The cost of tk3 meeting to be borne in equal propor- tions. If you will accept these terms we will endeavour, in conjunction with youtselves, to arrange for a convenient date and a suitable room. _</p>
        <p>VO-lii:;' faithfully,</p>
        <p>GEORGE E. NIACKEY 1. Joint G. H. WARBURTON STEEL. J Secretaries. P.S.—Seeing that you have sent copies of your challenge to the local Press we have also sent copies of this letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODR3LIE.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODR3LIE.</p>
        <p>This popular house of entertainment is now under new management, Mr. H. C. Hardcastle having I sucaeeded Mr. G. D. Bradbury on behalf of the Macnaghten Circuit. Madge Grey, the famous ' comedienne and child mimic, is making her return ! visit, and she is undoubtedly a great draw. She scored her greatest success as a Lancashire lass, in clogs and shawl, relating her adventures in London on a visit to the great exhibition. Madge Grey's command of the Lancashire dialect is remarkable and her inimitable recital kept the audience in roars of laughter. She told some excellent child stories in baby voice, and gave a clever mimicry of recitations of "The Village Blacksmith " by a cockney, a , Lancashire lad,a Scotchman and a Jew. Madge Grey is fortunate in possessing both good looks and talent, and she makes the best of both. Leslie's Comedy Cadets, who have earned fame and popularity by the seaside, give a bright entertainment consisting of songs, part songs and a ventriloquial sketch full of real fun. Their 's is a : popular turn, and the same may be said for Coggin , and Davies, who present an acrobatic speciality which ,is distinguished by clever dancing and daring ; tumbling on the bare boards. A unique feature is provided by Paul Powell with his electrical marion- ettes. Not only are the figures wonderfully life-like and well managed, but the electrical appurtenances I make the show one to be remembered. It concludes with a sort of tableaux in which coloured electric lights play upon fountains of water, the effect being I gorgeously beautiful. Jamie Piper earns a cordial reception as a young instrumental soloist, and Archie Pitt appears as comedian, with a couple of songs in character. His firtt song " Arn't you going to show us any more " would be better omitted. It didn't even tickle the " gods ; the strain of vulgarity was too evident. " Twice one , are-two, " was a legitimate success. A lively comedietta "Mam'selle Poin-porn" which has a strong flavour of the Spanish bull-ring, was presented by 11. C. Lovell and Co. The lady took most of the honours, with the bull-fighter next, and the "dude "a long way behind. The biograph presents a series of , remarkable pictures entitled " The Mysterious Thief," and the orchestral selections are well-played and popular. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. Next week we are to have a return visit from Lew Lake and his colossal comedy company in the petite comedy drama, entitled " My Pal Jerry," the sequel to "The Bloomsbury Burglars" which has also been presented here before with great suce6ss. Bob Morris will appear as Jerry Jackson and lAw Lake as Nobbler Nolan. There is pathos as well as sensation ; and humour in the piece and it should prove a power- ful attraction. Mysticus, described as the English , human chameleon, will present his mystifying yet ; funny entertainment " Spirits ? Spiritualism? or what?" Other artistes will be :—Adah Payne, vocalist; Captain Ellis and Son in a comedy musical act ; Sergeant Brennan, champion diabolist ; Wilton Heriot and Co. in the Protean comedy sketch" An Anglo-American Alliance"; Kirk and Saraski, the , only aquatic acrobats.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp;Colds. Relieves i Asthma and difficult breathing. Sid., is. lid.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, January 30, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, January 30, 1909.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL FESTIVAL. INTERESTINU CONTESTS ON FRIDAY AND SATURDAY NEXT. The second Warrington Musical Festival will be held at the Parr Hall on Friday and Saturday next and there is every indication that the event will be a complete success in every respect. The programme is of a sufficiently interesting character to appeal to all lovers of music. Friday will In set apart for the purely local competitions. The public proceedings will com- mence at 6.30 p.m. under the presidency of the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), with Mr. E. Lloyd Jones (chairman of the committee) as conductor. The chief events will be the children's choral contest, children's action song (confined to Band of Hope members), the church or chapel choir contest (for the silver cup presented by Mr. Jas. Dodds) and the second church or chapel choir contest for smaller choirs. There should be a large attendance at this concert which is a praiseworthy effort to encourage local talent. Saturday will witness the open competitions, and a more attractive series could ' hardly be desired. The afternoon concert will begin at two o'clock, with Alderman J. Smethurst in the chair. The great event will be the second choral competition for male voices which will undoubtedly be one of the finest contests ever held in Lancashire. The test piece is" Martyrs of the Arena " (De Mlle) and the following choirs have entered : —Warrington Male Voice, Warrington Apollo, Birkenhead Bruns- wick, Liverpool Cecilia, Goodshaw Glee Union, Sale Apollo, Garton Co-operative, Wigan Harmonic, Ashton-under-Lyne, Southport Vocal Union, and Denton Male Voice. Other events will be the chil- dren's choir, instrumental, and mixed voice quartette competitions. At the evening concert, which will commence at 6.30, the Mayor will preside. The crowning event of the festival, to be decided at this meeting, will be the chief choral competition (mixed voices), for the challenge shield presented by Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, for which the following entries have been received :—Stockton Heath Choral Society, Ashton Select Prize Choir, •Droylsden Co-operative Choral Society, Blackley Co-operative Choral Society, Sale and District Musical Society, Stretford Glee and Madrigal Society. The test pieces are : " Thanks be to God " (Mendelasohn) and" 0 happy Eyes" (Elgar) Another very pleasing feature will be the children's action song c mapetition for which there are three local entries. Mr. John Upson has consented to act as conductor at each of Saturday's gatherings. The adjudicators will be Mr. S. Coleridge-Taylor and Mr. Harry Evans. Tickets can be obtained from Messrs. Wadsworth's, Sankey-street, Mr. E. Wilkes, Legla- street, and all members of the committee. The Adjudicators. MR. S. COLERIDGE-TAYLOR. The Festival Committee has been fortunate in securing the services of that distinguished composer, Mr. S. Coleridge-Taylor' as an adjudicator. We have extracted the following biographical details from Mr. Booker T. Washington's preface to the Musician's Library (Messrs. Oliver Dickson Co., Boston U.S.A.) edition of" Twenty-four Negro Melodies" transcribed for the pianoforte by Mr. Coleridge-Taylor and pub- lished in 1904 : It is given to but few men in so short a time to create for themselves a position of such prominence on two continents as has fallen to the lot of Sahmel Coleridge-Taylor. Born in London, August 15th, 1375, Mr. Coleridge-Taylor is not yet thirty. His father, an African and a native of Sierra Leone, was educated at King's College, London, and his medical practice was divided between London and Sierra Leone. As a child of four and five Coleridge-Taylor could road music before he could read a book. His first musical instruction was on the violin. The piano he would not touch, and did not for some years. As one of the singing boys in St. George's Church, Croydon, he received an early training in choral work. At fifteen he entered the Royal College of Music as a student of the violin. Afterwards winning a scholar- ship in composition he entered, in 1393, the classes of Sir Charles Villiers Stamford, with whom he studied four years or more. Mr. Coleridge-Taylor early gave evidence of creative powers of a high order, and to-day, at the age of twenty- nine, he ranks as one of the most interesting and reruarkablo of British composers and conductors. Aside from his creative work, he is actively engaged as a teacher in Trinity College, London, and as conductor of the Handel Society, London and the Rochester Choral Society. At the Gloucester Festival of 1893 Mr. Coleridge-Taylor attracted general notice by the performance of his "Ballade in A Minor" for orchestra, , Op. 33, which he had been invited to conduct. His remarkably sympathetic setting in cantata form of portions of Longfellow's " Hiawatha "op. 30, has done much to make him known in England and America. This triple choral work, with its haunting melodic phrases, bold harmonic scheme, and vivid orchestration, was produced one part or scene at a time. The work was not planned as a whole, and the composer's original intention was to set " liiawatha's Wedding Feast " only. This section was first performed at a concert of the Royal College of Music under the conductorship of Stanford, November 11,1898. In response to an invita- tion from the committee of the North Staffordshire Musical Festival "The Death of Minnehaha " Op. 30, No 2, was written, and given under the com- poser's direction at Hanley, October 20, 1899. The overture to " The Song of Hiawatha," for . full orchestra, Op. 30, No. 3, a distinct work, was composed for and performed at the Norwich Musical Festival of 1899. The entire work, with the added third part— Hiawatha's Departure," Op. 40, No. 4—was the first given by the Royal Choral Society in Royal Albert Hall, •London, March 22, 1900, the composer conducting. In 1902 Mr. Coleridge-Taylor was invited to conduct at the Sheffield Musical Festival his orchestral and choral rhapsody " Meg Wane" Op. 48. The fact that this work was given on the same programme with a Bach cantata, Dvorak's " Stabat Mater" and Tchatovsky's " Symphonie PathiStigne " indicated the high esteem in which the composer is held. A sacred cantata of the dimensions and style of a modern oratorio "The Atonement," Op. 53, was first given at Hereford Festival, September 9tlr, 1903, under the composer's baton, and its success was even greater at the first London performance in the Royal Albert Hall on Ash ‘Wrinesday, 1904, the composer conducting. The first performance of " The Atonement" in this country was by the Church Choral Society under Richard Henry Warren at St. Thomas's Church, New York, February 24th and 25th, 1004. Worthy of special mention are the " Quintet for Clarinet and Strings " Op. 6 (1897) which Joachim has given, and the " Sorrow Songs "Op. 57 (1904)—a setting of six of Christina Rossitti's exquisite poems. Besides the works already mentioned are a " Nonet for piano, strings and wind," Op. 3 (1391), " Symphony in A Minor " Op. 7 (1395), Solemn Prelude for Orchestra" Op. 40 (1899), between thirty and forty songs, various piano solos, anthems and part songs, and works in both largo and small form for the violin with orchestra or piano. Mr. Coleridge-Taylor has written much, has achieved much. His work, moreover, possesses not only charm and power but distinction, the individual note. The genuineness, depth and intensity of his feeling, coupled with his mastery technique, spontaneity, and ability to think in his own way, explain the force of the appeal his compositions make. Another element in the persuasiveness of his music lies in its naturalness, the directness of its appeal, the US3 of simple and expressive melodic themes, a happy freedom from the artificial. These traits, employed in the freedom of modern musical speech, coupled with emotional power and supported by ample technical resources beget an utterance quick to evoke response. The paternity of Mr. Coleridge-Taylor and his love for what is elemental and racial found rich expression in the choral work for which he is best knowneind more obviously in his "African Romances," Op. 17, a set of seven songs ; the" African Suite " for the piano, Op. ; and " Five Choral Ballads " for baritone solo, quartette, chorus and orchestra, Op. 54, being a setting of five of Longfellow's ' Poems of Slavery." The transcription of Negro melodies continued in this volume is, however, the most complete expression of Mr. Coleridge-Taylor's native bent and power. Using some of the native songs of Africa and the West Indies with songs that came into being in America during the slavery regime, he has in handling these melodies pre- served their distinctive traits and individuality, at the same time giving them an art form fully imbued with their essential spirit. It is especially gratifying at this time, when interest in the plantation songs seems to be dying out with the generation that gave them birth, when the Negro song is in too many minds associated with "rag " music and the more reprehensible " coon " song, that the most cultivated musician of his race, a man of the highest aesthetic ideals, should seek to give permanence to the songs of his people by giving them a now inter- pretation and an added dignity." Since the above was published, the works written and produced by Mr. Coleridge-Taylor include "Variations on an African Theme' (produced by the London Philarmonic Society), the music to " Nero " (produced by Mr. Beerbohm Tree at His Majesty's Theatre), arid the music to "Faust" which has just been so successful at the same theatre. His most recent composition is a "Ben-Hon Suite" for baritone solo, chorus and orchestra, produced at Brighton Musical Festival on January 14th last, and which according to the Press will probably rival " Hiawatha's Wedding Feast" in popularity. Mr. Coleridge-Taylor is novvl engaged in orchestrating his first grand opera in three acts, entitled " Thelma" which will probably be produced in May next at Drnry-lane Theatre by the Moody-Manners Opera Company. Mr. Coleridge-Taylor has had a wide experience as an adjudicator at musioal festivals. He was chief adjudicator at the Royal National Eisteddfod held at Llangollen in 1908 and the other festivals at which he has officiated include Cardiff International and Southport.</p>
        <p>MR. HARRY EVANS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coleridge-Taylor will have as his co-adjudicator Mr. Harry Evans, F.R.C.0., of Liverpool, who has also achieved considerable distinction in the musical world. A native of Merthyr Tydvil, ha commenced his musical career at an early age and by the time ho had attained his majority he was conductor of the famouq Dowlais Male Voice Choir which in 1900 won the chief prize at the Royal National Eisteddfod. He alga conducted the Merthyr and Dowlais Choral Society for several years and is at present conductor of the Liverpool Welsh Choral Union—an organization which ha.4.-done much to foster a love of the best music in that city. "In Mr. Harry Evans" say the Musical News " these Welsh singers have a conductor exactly suited to their tempera- ment and needs. Alert, sensitive and unsparing of gestures, he contrives to communicate his own fire and intelligence to highly receptive material. The result is certainly the best Welsh chorus yet heard in Liver- pool." Mr. Evans has also won his spurs as a oomposer. His first work" The Victory of St. Garrnon " was com- posed for the Cardiff Musical Festival, 1903, and his second work " David ab Gwilym " for the Royal National Eisteddfod, 1908. He has composed many choruses for male voices and , children's choirs. Mr. Evans' services as an adjudicator are greatly in demand. , He adjudicated at Swansea National Festival in 1897, Llangollen, Blackpool, Morecambe, Middlesborough, Buxton. Southport, and Lytham (all in 1903) and he is engaged for the London, Buxton, Blackpool and More- cambe festivals during the present year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</p>
        <p>ARTICLE IL To Prevent Invasion.</p>
        <p>Lact week we published detttils of the proposals of the National Service League, the only organisation in Great Britain which is working to persuade the British race that compulsory military training is just, necessary and advantage-ins. THE NECESSITY.</p>
        <p>1 It would be a forlorn horn •to propose compulsory 1 training unless it was necessary. A fierce debate arises over this question of necessity. The" Blue- ! Water 7 School says: —" Trust in the Navy. If the Navy can't keep the enemy out of England, we had better surrender at once." Then speaks the ' Cold- , Water ' School: "Bother armaments, dread- nothings and Militarism ! Pull down some of these slum dwellings. Relieve the unemployed. Then attend to home defence' So cold water is thrown on a question which touches every home in England. For like Italy of 1860, our first business ilto exist, and only afterwards to consider tbe means of existence. However a "Safe Home" School is arising in Great Britain, which asks plaintively "I don't understand military and "naval affairs, but I do want to know for certain that my workshops will not be blown up by a foreign shell. I don't want my wife and children thrown out of my home, so that foreign soldiers may sleep in my beds. I don't want my wages reduced to a pound a week so that England • may pay a Continental power a-huge war indemnity" Yet 57 Admirals, Lord Roberts and many men of 1 wide experience and commonsense say that such horrors may b 3 possible if a foreign army slips past our Navy. "By Jove," says the man in the street "1 ; belong to the 'Safe Home school. I don't like all my eggs to be in one basket. I want a strong navy land an army which could really defend me in case of ! need."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>HAIN' OBJECT.</head>
        <p>THE HAIN' OBJECT.</p>
        <p>The starting point of every discussion on home I defence should be the axiom that the object of British I policy is Peace. None of the forces we maintain or Ihope to provide ought to be placed in motion save for tightful motives of legitimqte self-defence and the more interest the nation takes in affairs of war, the !less liklihood there will be of an aggressive policy. Those who know how to box do not thoughtlessly ! rush into a street fight. Bat it. is not enough to desire peace. We must enforce a conviction upon ! other powers that they have nothing to gain from war. An old gentleman who walks into a slum district notorious for hooligans and pickpockets ' invites assault if the time he chooses for his walk is midnight and his sole defence an umbrella. But let him carry a thick stick and his possible assailants will think again before risking their heads. THE OPEN DOOR,.</p>
        <p>The whole volume of evidence on the Continent from Napoleon to Moltke points without a doubt to ' the invasion of England as the ultimate aim of every I plan of operations against our country. A. blow at the heart is the quickest and most efficaci ms way of settling Great Britain. The stupendous results of success warrant almost any conceivable risk to attain it. Now the front door of England on the East Coast is guarded by a very fine watch-dog, the Navy. But the dog is not always there. He may be tempted away after a decoy enemy. At the time of the Dogger Bank incident, when we were on the point of war with , Russia, our fleet was in Vigo Bay. When Austria • aroused the Balkans last autumn, .eight ships of the Channel Fleet were in dock, just as the watch-dog sometimes goes to feed iii the backyard. When a Continental naval force was mobilised last year in tho , North, Sea our fleet was in Lamlash Bay on the West 'of Scotland. But most front doors fasten tight with a good strong lock. The front. door of England has a I very weak lock, for the Regulars cannot in all emergencies be kept at home, and when they go we ; must trust to the Territorials. If the enemy once I dodge by our watch-dog of the Fleet, they will have littl3 difficulty in forcing open the front door. We must have a strong navy, and behind this an army with which to hold back invaders until the navy has time and opportunity to do its work. What is the make of the present lock? THE PRESENT LOCK. The regular army, of Great Britain has the honour- able reputation of being the most efficient as it is the smallest army in Europe. The recent reductions face us with a bloodless butcher's bill heavier than was exacted during the South African war. Many con- tingencies may occur in the next few years, for which we shall need far more trained men than we have at present available. Take one example. According to experts in case of war on the Indian frontier, 350,000 men would be required on the Helmand in Afghanistan. For this work would be needed reinforcements of at least 103,000 men or eight divisions from home, but there are only six available. Our regular army cannot possibly be kept at home in such contingencies. As ; Mr. Haldane has insisted again and again, we must take the Territorials seriously. They are the begin- nings of a citizen army. Upon them alone may some day fall a serious task —the task of defending English homes. Are they equal to take the task? Mr. Haldane with wise foresight has provided the framework of an efficient citizen militia and thoroughly deserves the nation's gratitude for his energy and eloquence in trying to persuade men to fill the frame- work. But even the most public spirited citizen may well ask hi easel f why he should risk his life and damage I his business prospects, while able-bodied neighbours sit at their ease and contribute nothing to the national safety. However at present the nineteen neighbours , sitting at ease try to coax the one public-spirited man to do their duty. How can such an unfair system ; ever provide the country with a home defence army of , sufficient numbers and adequate training? Is there , not a necessity to put a new lock on the door? THE NEW LOCK. If we are not safe from invasion, and clearly we are not, or we should require no Territorial Army, our home defence force should be adequately trainedlo meet regular troops. Every crisis has shown us that we had too few men. Inability to provide a sufficient , number of men has always been the eternal:difficulty of our military problem. It always will be till we : follow the example of every great Continental power, ' and also of that tight little island Japan, and adopt compulsion. Mr. Haldane's scheme is good to the extent that it supplies the machinery, only it requires compulsion to give the steam pressure to make it I work. The sooner we realise that our Territorial ! Army without suftleiently trained men is but a skeleton, ; the better it will be for the security of our Empire and for the preservation of the peace of the world. THE QJESTION. I have now tried to place before readers an honest and truthful account of our home defence, as it appears from one point of view. It is for the British public, in Whose hands lie the destinies of four hundred million people, to. decide whether they consider compulsory training to be necessary. The question is not :4 Who 'a going to attack us? but "Are we ready to defend ourselves, if we are attacked?" A dying Scotchinan asked if he would be saved from the wrath to come by leaving I£lo,ooo to the kirk. "1 canna promise that, said the shrewd theologian, "but its worth trying." Neither do I promise that compulsory training will abolish all war and physical' and moral degeneracy from Great Britain, but at any rate it is worth con- sideration whether it is not necessary to put a stronger lock. on the door of our country —a lock of the character recommended by the National Service League. Warrington.</p>
        <p>B. S. TOWNEW E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>"THE STITCH IN TIME."</head>
        <p>"THE STITCH IN TIME."</p>
        <p>National Service League Play at Warrington.</p>
        <p>It was anticipated that " The Stitch in Time," a play in two acts by Miss T. H. Crosfield and Mr. B. S. Townroe, which was performed for the first time in the dining hall at Messrs. Crosfield's works on Saturday evening, would have a strong N.S.L. flavour. Nor were expectations belied: From beginning to end the play is simply a medium for the propagation of the League's proposals. England, caught quite unprepared, is made to suffer the ignominy of invasion by a foreign power and, of course, the moral drawn is that compulsory training shenld be adopted without delay. The ardent Leaguer no doubt found this deeply impressive but to take the play seriously it was necessary to be thoroughly steeped in N.S.L. principles. Otherwise, the performance was hardly calculated to do more than amuse. To outline the story: The uprising curtain discloses the drawing- room of K.nareton Hall, near Manchester, where all is peace and tranquillity. Here, at any rate, invasion would be the farthest thought from one's mind.Uarion Allison, the daughter of Colonel Allison, reclines on the sofa and by and by begins to talk to Susan Nfiller, the family nurse, about the "stupid" National Service League meeting to which several members of the house ' party have betaken themselves. Mari m has no great opinion of the League and unkindly places it in the same category as the Leagues for the extermination of black beetles and the provision of milk for stray cats. Next enters her brother Charles. He is the picture of indolence and quite fails to understand why people should trouble their heads about national defence. To attend to his own comfort appears to be about the limit of his exertions. The arrival of the party from the meeting brightens things up a little. Edward Harrington, a territorial offiaer, has ben advocating the cause of the League in glowing phrases, and has obviously 'made a deep inr,oression upon at least one listener —Hilda Watson, niece and ward of Colonel Allison. The couple wax eloquent on the subject when they return and treat the camp any to a few of the arguments familiar to all who have , ever attended a League meeting. Reginald Metcham, UP. for Kuareton, looks bared and —like other M.P.'s—cannot be induced to commit himself on the question. All he does is to say something vague about his anxiety for social reform, which calls forth the wrath of Hilda Watson who considers national defence of far greater importance than oh-age I pensions and education controversies. Masoa, the chauffeur, an ex-naval man, is charmed with the League programme and also with Jenny Larkins, the parlourmaid, bat he cannot induce this coy damsel to share his anxiety as to England's safety. " %rare Hinglieh and that's suft:ieat tithe strain in whiCh she pooh-poohs the idea of invasion. Conversation —all on the same theme —proceeds more or less interestingly bat it is finally clinched by Itarrington saying very s olemely " Depend upon it. Invasion will come like a thief in the night." Marion, however, has been so impressed that when the M.P. tries to steal a kiss he is repulsed with the remark, in withering tones, that the man who cannot defend his sweetheart cannot expect any kisses. The happy- go-lucky Charles y:elds to his sister's entreaties and reluctantly decides to join the Territorials. The scene no..Y is the e:nboiiinent of c dna and serenity. Marion, looking out into the garden, observes " What a peaceful night," when In ! there is a reverberating roar, as of heavy artillery, which causes Susan to faint on the spot and the other ladies to exclaim with ! apprehension. The M.P. is still unperturbed. "Its only a peal of thunder" is his comment. But pre- sently the chaufteur,who has been investigating on his ' own account, arrives with the startling news that the Tradelanders (probably some distant relatives of the ruthless Tariff Reformers) have indeed invaded , England and that the explosion was caused by the blowing up of a neighbouring railway bridge. ' Moreover, he has seen oas of the enemy busily engaged in cutting the telegraph wires. Fortunately this destructive gentleman deferred his operations just lons eaough for a telegram to arrive, informing Harriugton that the enemy have reached Edinburgh, Barwick, Hull and Suffolk —a pretty large reach! The house is plunged in dark- ness by the failure of the electric light, the reason being, to use the expressive words of Mason, that the electric station at Manchester has gone up to he-woo! Consternation reigns supreme. All the male-kiad, with the exception of the M.P., betake themselves to the scene of hostilities, and as the curtain descends the women are wringing their hands and bidding tearful farewells. Act 2 shows the desolated household a fortnight later. The women are looking pile and wan as the result of worry and privation, and Susan g Nos about weeping and wailing for her "Dear Mr. Charles." The ALP., nor garbed as a sea of toil, returns from a forragiug expedition, having commandeered a solitary dog biscuit which he pro-- duces with great pride Then the heart-rending news is broken that poor Plebs, the darling pet dog, has been sacrificed on the altar of Hunger ! The latest news from the front shows that poor Charles has not covered himself with glory. One of his most striking achievements, it appears, was to mistake the signal to retreat for the command t i charge, with disastrous consequences to himself. Eventually the luckless Charles comes home, bearing grotesque evidence of having fought the Tradelanders single-handed —by mistake of course! His head is swathed in gory bandages and his arm hangs limply in a sling. "It's all the guvnor's fault for not having me trained " is tho burden of his co ma- plaint. Another pleasant diversion is the arrival of Mason, now in full naval outfit, who imparts the melancholy intelligence that he has lost t fingers in a brush with the enemy, Judging from his version of the state of affairs, Eneand is in a worse state than even his fingers, which are already lost. The Territorials have- proved almost helpless against the trained troups of the Tradelandecs and are, of course, being killed off like harmles3 flies. The last arrival is Efarrington and he is effusively welcomed by Hilda Watson, who has taken upon herself the duties of an ambulance worker, and figures in nurse's costume, with the Red Cross prominently displayed. The plir enjoy a hearty meal off an apearently savoury pie made from the remains or the departed Pluflis, and the fare seems quite agreeable. Harrington is also pessimistic about England's chances and while giving th. Territorials every credit for having fought like Irish terriers asks, " But what can you expect a terrier to ' do against a mastiff?" A stray shell busting in the conservatory creates a tremendous sensation. By this time the whole household i§sthoroughly converted —or scared--;-to the adoption of National Service League proposals. The M.P. rehearses the high-flown oration he has pledged himself to deliver in the House of Commons —if ever that assembly is fated to meet again, which is doubtful—advocating compulsory training in unmistakable terms. The advance of the enemy's artillery causes the abandonment of the house, and the bulk of the party flees conveniently per motor-car to the Welsh moun- tains, doubtless to endure privations worse than those alleviated by the sacrifice of the lamented Pluflles, Hilda and Harrington nobly resolve to stay, and as the curtains falls on a decidedly colourless denoue- ment, the lady laments: "If only England had made the stitch in time the flag would not have fallen." England's ultimate fate is, like that of the rest of the house-party, left a matter for dismal conjecture. The presentation of the play was, on the whole meritorious, although a little less stiffness and more enthusiasm on the part of most of the performers would have improved it. The cast was as follows :- 1 Susan Miller (the Family Nurse) .. .. Miss C. Broadbent Marion Allison (Daughter of Colonel Allison) Miss B. Broadbent Charles Allison (Son of Colonel Allison) .. Mr. J. Harvey Mason (a Chaffeur, late of the Royal Navy) Mr. B. S. Townroe Hilda Watson (Niece and Ward of Colonel Allison) _</p>
        <p>Miss S. Broadbent Jenny Larkins (a Parlour-maid) Miss 1.1. Heath Rdward Harrington (a Territorial 091:er) .. Capt. G. R. Mott Reginald Metchara (M.P. for Knareton) .. Capt. G. W. G. May</p>
        <p>Mr. B. S. Townrol, as the indomitable Mason, pleased the audience immensely by the naturalness and "go" of his portrayal. The Misses Broadbent were yell placed in their respective parts, and Miss B. Heath was admirable as Jenny Larkins. Captain G. R. Mott was a gallant and soldierly Edward Harrington, Ciptain G. W. G.. May assumed the role of Reginald Metcham, 11. P., with considerable succesa, and Mr. J. Harvey did justice to the part of Charles Allison.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mix Woois acted as prompter, and lfr. G. B. Dean and Mr. H. Flood mlnage,d the sttge effects. The curtain raiser, "Amateur Burglary," was acceptably presented by Captain G. R. Mutt and Mr. B. S. Townroe.</p>
        <p>During the interval if r 3. W. B. Roberts' ladies' orchestra played choice selections in cre litable style. The performance was repeated in the Christ Church Parish Room, Latchford, on Monday evening, and it is the intention to produce the play in various parts of Lancashire.</p>
        <p>Tdke Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; it never fails. Is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETTS IMPERIAL FEDERATION BILL.</p>
        <p>Tariff Reform Dis3assei.</p>
        <p>Great interest was taken in the meeting of the Warrington Parliamentary D3baing S33iety on Tues- day evening, br it W:l3 the mecasion of the eagerly anticipated debate on Alderann Bennett's Bill "to federate the Empire ami impose an imperial tiriff for defence." There was a large attendance of inenV)ers and of the general pu!)lic and the discussion was entered into with much keenness.</p>
        <p>A. batch of questions having been anßwered, kfr. J. F. Awbsry , Dover), announcei tb.it fr. E. Wynne Jones (Cheiter), bad boan ad ptel leader and Ir. W. Furness (Bristol N.) deputy lenier of the Opposition. Alderman Bennett (Colonial Sscretary), who had a cordial greeting from all parts of the house, then introdn3od the Bill which was pnblished in last week's " EKatniner," The provision relating to tariff reform is as follows : _ _</p>
        <p>That a spacial al valorea tax of five per cent. over and ahove existing duties be levied in the ports of the United Eingdom upon all foreign goods with the exception of such food supplies or raw material as cannot advantageously be produce(l within the limits of the Empire, and upon the goods of any Colony or Possession which is not prepared to levy a similar tax. That tie proceeds of this spacial tax, wherever levied, be administered by the Imperial Cabinet for purposes of Imperial Defence. Tufa any dissentient state shall be entitlei to representation in the Imperial Cabinet, but sh tit not participate in the preference ai'fordA by the special tax. That the tax may be increased provided that each iacrease is accompanied by a proportionate decrease in the duties at present levied by the assenting states as between each other. That similar fiscal privileges may be accordal to any foreign state which is willing to reciprocate. . . . _ .</p>
        <p>The-ALD A.N slid the subject was about the mist important it was possible for any body of English citizens to discuss. Ensphaeising the immense size aced possibilities of the British Empire, he stated that the Empire was 52 times as large as Germany, 53 times the size of France, four times as large as the United States and bigger than three Earopea. Its area was 11,40J,400 suare miles, or 21 per cent, of the earth's surface. Its population was 410,003,053, or 22 per cent. of the population of the habitable globe. The total revenue was 2431,030,000 per annum and the debts were £1,503,003,000. Its trade amoented to £1,600,033,003 per annum. It extended to every quarter of the globe, and provided every variety of food and material known to civilization. Ia spite of its huge size and enormous resources there was no other Empire on the face of the earth more chaotically governed or with more room for improvement in reference to its constitution. The Imperial Cabinet was so only in name. The Imperial Parliament was an absolute farce, as it simply repre- sented the United Kingdom. There was no opportunity of any consultation between the did'arent portions of the Empire, except the haphazard method of occasional Colonial Conferences. The defence of the Empire was in a chaotic state, and unfair to this country. We had to shoulder almost the whole of the burden, bat the navy was just as essential to other parts of the Empire as it was to this country. It was an absolute fact that of the cost of the navy the United Kingdom paid not less then 19s. GI in the 2, while the rest of the Empire paid 61. That was eminently illogical. Teen was also no union for commerce. lie did net think there was any other netion which bad ne kind of Z ollyereia or commercial arrangement among the various States of which it was constituted. In the British Empire eve-y part of the Empire merle whatever fiscal arrangements it pleased ; England entirely differed from all other parts of the Empire. The closest approach to a Zellverein was that certain colonies of their benevolence and goodwill hed con- us a preference in reeeri to some of the gods this country sent into their borders. Taere mist, he contended, be anion for consultation, commerce mid defence. fie hoped his Bill would meet with the support of many on the opposite silo of the fleuse. Some of then, he believed, were proa 1 of the 111113 of "little Engla.n ler " bat he world hide his diininiehei head f anyone applied it to him, because it inaelied a narrow outlook on the world. appealed with con- fidence to Liberal Imperialists to loek beyend the policy of the parish pu no, and to look beyond the borders of their o ;en country. He contended the best way to begin with the s 'eject wont I be to convene a colonial conference. Td3 1(131)3e:11 a3VOrtraCiat should go before it with definite propoeils. A Bill should be submitted to the conference and if the conferense, by a substantial majority, sly two-thirds, approved of it, then the British Parliament shoald translate it into legislation an -I get the self-governing colonies to pans similiar measures. The first step on the formation of an Imperial P irli onent would be the i.npesi- tine of some Imperial tax for Imperial parpases. fie hoped they would I )o!I at the que-tien as Eaglish- men first and psrty politicians afterwards. Then they would cease to embitter it with acrid party controversy which was uncalled for. He wanted to interfere as little as possible with existing tariffs and his proposal was to levy a soeciai 5 per cent. tax over and above the existing tariff; on foreign goods only, coming not only into the United Kiugdon but into all parts of the Empire. The only objection that could conceivably be raised was that the E 'Tire was not at present able to produce all the things we needed in saoiently quantities. For the moment it might net be wise to Mlarge impose a duty on cotton and similar relaxations might be suggested with regard to other things which at present could be better produced in other countries. A 5 per cent duty on the total foreign imports into the United Kingdom would amount to over 221,030,030 per m annum and a 5 per cent duty on the foreign imports into the rest of the Empire to nearly £7,030,000 per annum, together about £31,003,000, but as it might be necessary to admit certain raw materials,such as cotton, duty free and as the effect of the tax weal) probably be to reduce the amount of foreign goads imp orted, ho would hake that figure. This would leave the sum of between £15,030,030 and £16,030,000 as a contribution towards the present cost of imperial defence, to which the colonies would c ontribute between £3,03),0” an 1 £4,090.030. The amount by which the Imperial Exchequer would bearoat would be more than suilicient to abelish the existing taxes on food. A common objection to tariff reform was that it would lead to a system of " lobbying" and that cliffereat interests would button- hole members of Parliament and try by all sorts of unfair and fraudulent me-ms to get a preference for their particular tradee. Thera was no risk of that under his scheme.es every trade would b 3 on the wile footing, the only difference being as between foreign and colonial goods. Another provision was that as the tariff was raised to the outside 'world it would drop to the variceis States of the Empire and the ultimate goal would he 're 3 Trade vrithia the Empire. Alluding to his scheme of Parliamentary reform, Alderman Bennett said the Conservatives were often taunted with being retrograde and foolish in their devotion to the House of Lords. -lie ha:l no such blind devotion to that institution, although he believed a Second Chamber was an absolute necessity—and in that he was backed up by the opinion of the civilized world. He knew gentlemen opposite toek no notice of the opinion of the civilized world when it happened to run counter to their own theories.(Laughter.)fie wantea the Second Chamher to be thoroughly dignified, representa- tive cod effective for its great an I lofty purpose. Alderman Bennett expleined the details of his scheme for the constitution of the Imperial Parliament and the House of Commons for the United Kingdom, and claimed that in giving the House of Commons power to delegate to committees powers relating to local matters, he was doing something to solve the Irish wiestion.ln conclusion he said that of all the work to which English statesmen could dedicate their lives there was none more noble than the federation of the Empire. It would lead, he believed, eventually to Free Trade, peace and Christianity throughout the world, and the things for which good men had been striving all their lives. It had been his dream all his life, and he hoped, the dream of every true Englishman. lie vys not without hope that during his life he would see!' scheme on the present or similar lines brought into the domain of practical politics and embodied in legisla- tion. (Applause). Hr. E. GORDDV BiertaEss (chief secretary for Ireland), formally seconded the adoption of the Bill. Mr. W. Furls:ass (L., Bristol N.), moved that the Bill he read that day six months. tie remarked that the Bill had some very curious characteristics. It was not a Conservative measure; it was too broad and generous and altogether too revolutionary, lie did not know how the hon. members opposite reconciled some of the proposals in the Bill with their admiration for the gilded chamber. The Liberals could not support the Bill under any conditions whatever, so. long as the plank of tariff reform was so securely nailed down in it. He could not see how the measure could be agreeable to ) the Nationalists, because it made no provision to meet their aspirations. With regard to the proposed imposition of a tariff of five per cent. on goods coming into the Empire, he warned the Houses of the loss of the American Colonies during the reign of George M., consequent upon the imposition of the tea tax. The imports from foreign lands into this country during the year 1002, amounted to £314,000,030 and a 5 per cent. duty on that would realize about £15,500,030. The expenditure on imperial defence, however, amounted to £59,050,003 per annum. Did the right hon, gentleman expect the colonies to find the other £45,000,000? If so, it would mean a tax on the colonies of a very unjust character. He was glad Alderman Bennett had seen fit to withdraw the tax on cotton.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : It would be left to the Imperial Cabinet.</p>
        <p>Mr. FETEMESS : I take it you are in dottbt whether it would he to the interests of the trade of the country to put a tax on cotton. Alderman BEN&gt;lwrT: Yes, at present. FURSIRSS contended thit that admission damned the whole Bill. If it was not good for cotton, was it</p>
        <p>good for anything else? If the imposition of a 5 per cent. duty on raw cotton would hamper or interfere with the development of trade in cotton, would it note do the same thing for other manufactures? We could' noteax ourselves into prosperity. We were already over-burdened with taxation. The most simple course would be to ask the Colonies to treat us as we treated them by throwing their ports open to our goods. The unity of tbe Empire would then be arrived at in a less complex and more equitable way. Mr. E. ‘reeee-Joess (Leader of the Opposition) formally seconded the rejection ef the Bill. Mr. Beaele. (N., Donegal) condemned the Bill as an insidious attempt to coerce the House into passing Tariff Reform, The Nationalists were in favour of Imperial federation, but not on the lines embodied ia the Bill. They could not be in favour of anything that was un-Conetitutional—(laughtor)—as they maintained that the Bill W. 13. He did not agree that the only way to raise funds for Imperial defence WdS by taxing the necessaries of life. England had paid the piper long enough. 'The Bill Was an absolutely impossible,one and he advised the aldertnta to bury it and try again. (Laughter.) After all, sentiment Was the indestructible basis of the Empires. (Applause). Mr. NV. Clutter es (Home Secretary) said the illustra- tion of the reign of George 111. was beside the mark, as this Bill did not propose to do anything without the consent of the Colonies. It was in response to the wishes of the Colonies that the scheme was brought forward. Mr. Chrimes proceeded to urge that unrestricted competition meant the survival of the fittest and the weakest must go the wall. It was a great fallacy that cheapness was in all respects a bless- ing. It was nothing of the kind. It was indirectly encoaraging sweating. Cheap goods must mean cheap labour, and the producer should be considered as well as the consumer. If for the sake of argument, they assumed that Tariff Reform would mean a slight increase in the cost of living, it would be more than counter- balanced by the increase in wages. Mr. C. E. PA-RKER, (L., Dundee) congratulated the Colonial Secretary on the earnestness and ability with which he had introduced the Bill. The measure was, however, altogether bad. It was possibly better than some of the plans of the Tariff Reform Association but when they he'd two bad things they were not obliged to choose the lesser evil. What surprised him was how the Colonial Secretary had managed to win over his Government to his proposals, because one of the principles of Tariff fleforea as advocated by Mr. Chamberlain was that we should not tax raw material. The Colonial Secretary abandoned that principle and suggested taxing everything—(Conservative cries of " No. ") —except those things which could not be advantageously grown or mule in the Empire. 'Pe second feature of the Tariff Reform as we generally knew it was that it should be of a sciontida character, and the science consisted of graduttine the taxation according to the character of the import. Thus, if they took the trade with which he was familiar, sapposing 21 per cent. daty were put on hides, probably 5 par ceat. would be impose-1 on leather and 10 or I cent, on boots. It was surprising how the Colonial Secretary had got the party which was so attached to Tariff Reform as generally advocated to swallow that Bill which went on entirely different principles. He supposed that, as in the case of the other right hon. • member for West Birmingharu, they looked to him and said amongst themselves: "When father turns we all turn." (Laughter.) The Bill was bad because of the unfairness of its incidence. llt would be unfair first of all to the mother country, because the Colonial Secretary hinaselfilhad shown that the great burden would fall upon the United Kingdom, and an amount out of all proportion to population or ability to bear the tax. Tee Colonial Secretary had not explained how the additional amount required for defence, after he had raised £12,030,0J0 or £15,009,00G by the tax, was to be raised. He (the speaker) supposed that, as at present, the mother country would have to bear it, as well as the larger proportion of the new tax. 't'he scheme would be unfair in its incidenee as between the colonies themeolves, because they were differently situate! in regard to drawing_ their require- ments from outside sources. Some of the colonies rag iired a great deal from other countries for their in mar:v.:tures ani food, whilst others needed _very little. The colonies which regaired the most would have to boar the heaviest harden, and that might net be in- any sense proportionate to their ability to ply it. The tax would also press unfairly upon particular trades and Sonae trades in this country would be vary hard hit b3CAQ3O they had to draw their raw materi ii aim ost entirely from foreign •so areas, whilst other traiee, such as the Bradford trade which obtained its wool almost entirely from Australia, would not have t stand their seare of tha taxation. Therefore the burden woald be very ueeenally distributed in all respects. Tile Bill WAS altogether bad because wine to its ineceitlities, it woull undoubtedly cra,ste within the Empire suaea an ameant of dissatifsation that instead of cementing the Empire, as the Colonial secretary fondly hoped it would, it would breed dis- card and discontent. It WAS a bad thing for us to try to de ourselves what we cook! get other countries to do for us at a less cost. As a trader, if he could get I somebody else to de something for him at a smaller cost than ha could do it himself he would certainly take advantage of the fact, especially if that iudividuelsere: willing to exchenge..sorne of his products 'for those which he (qr. Parker) was better able to produce. .A country was involved in economic loss wheta artifici if means to de things which soother could d better for it. He would like to ask the ' Colonial Secretary w bother• in using the word "ad van ta.geously," he inearlit on the present level terms or when the 5 percent. tariff wall had been erected. I Alderman Be things like said he meent there were erowaa nt by like wheat which the E npira obviously coeld produce adventageelnly, and he \wall give it the opportunity of doing so.</p>
        <p>otaits t behoisneg Mr. PaRKM contended it was a misuse of the term. E very thing we could produee advantageously at present w enterprisespr oi uo e d. wThr.itedhe rws owuel rde ycioentrta hn et [my sae a rporhoifint.g Pt Voice : "Abroad.") Here mostly. (Opposition applause.) They could leave vary well the question of advantage to individual traders, but when they erected a 5 per cent. tariff wall and nude it advantageous because of that wall, they had an economic loss. He • agreal with Alde.r.n in Bennett that the question should be approached from the economic rather than the party point of view. It was a grelt—pity the alderman's own party had not done that, inste id of indulging in se. much waving of the flag and trying to fire out from their ranks those who ventured to think differently from themselves. (Cheers.) The strength of the Free Trade pasition was the economic aspect. Elaborating his argument, Air. Parker said that supposing, under the present conditions, we could buy LON tomatoes fore sovereign, and that behind the shelter of a tariff wall the same quantity could be grown for 21s.it would bee loss to the country of ls.,andit would be so in all trades made artifioially Profitable. Mr. Parker also emphasised the fact that in this question money was only the - measure of value and pliyed very little part in the payment one way or the other. Mr. E. J. HALL (C. North Gloucester) said the last speaker had described the Bill as altogether bad. There were four principles in the Bill. First it said the country should co-operate with the Colonies. He said that was altogether hid. Secondly. it said, Do some- thing to increase the revenue of this country. Mr. Parker said that was altogether bad. Thirdly, the Bill said this country should do something to help their industries. He said that was altogether bad. Fourthly, it said that they should ensure a reform of the House of Lords. That, ho said, was absolutely bad. He (Mr. Hall) asked why so long as revenue was required by this country—and until the millenitnn arrived, it would be necessary—should it not be raised in a manner that would assist the industries of this country? What was the use of talking to a man about bread being cheap when he bad not a farthing to buy it with? With regard to the House of Lords, they had nothing to hope from the Liberal party who had been threatening so long but had done nothing. The Conservatives would deal with the question, and the House of Lords itself was anxious that it should te dealt with. Mr. W. Vl..asos (1., Ne Ncastle-under Lyme) moved the adjournment of the debate, which will be resumed nett Tuesday evening.</p>
        <p>A BOOK FOR EVERY SEASON. Most of us, when we F.r,o away, like to arrange for hotel or other accournodation in advance if possible. This is generally done through correspondence, the addresses being obtained from some reliable guide to holiday apartments and hotels. When the " quarters " are not" booked" in this way, it is found advisable to call at various addresses at our destination with a view to making a personal selection of the most suitable; here again it is found convenient to consult a recog- nised official guide generally obtainable at railway stations, thereby obtaining particulars of the addresses available. _Recognising their passengers' needs in this direction, the Groat Western Railway have now in preparation their " Flaunts " 'Guide for next season. The book will be published shortly before Easter and will contain a very complete list of the holiday accommodation at the various places served by , their system. Last season's guide was undoubtedly the best of its kind, and there is no doubt that this enter- prising Company will make the 1909 edition of " Holiday Flaunts "even more useful and attractive. Proprietors of hotels, farm-houses and other accommodation will do well to obtain particulars of the Company's arrange- ments in respect of the publication from the nearest G. W. It. Station Master without delay.</p>
