      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BREAKING AND ENTERING.</head>
        <p>BREAKING AND ENTERING.</p>
        <p>Two Captures at Warrington.</p>
        <p>John Flannagan, of liolt-street, was charged on remand at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday with breaking and entering the lock-up shop of Mr. G. ley,saddler,Winwick-street,on Sunday:January 17th, and stealing therefrom a 3ft. rule and a piece of leather vtiltted at Gs. Gd. the property of the above. The Chief Constable stated that about 10.20 on Sunday ex ening, January 17th, Mr. Danks, residing at 3, Bewsey- street, heard a smash of glass at the rear of his pretnises. Thinking that someone was attempting to break into Mr. BAyley's shop, he' went in search of the police. He returned with Sergeant Dean and P.C. Arm- strong,who found that a window had been broken at the rear of Bayley's prernises.The sergeant had the premises surrounded and he entered by the broken window, lie found prisoner in a corner crouching down. Prisoner said to the officer "You are just in time, I did not intend to break in here. It was Nlounfield's I intended to break into." James Bayley was called and siid he carried on busi- ness as a saddler at 2, Winwick-street. He left ths shop secure at 2 o'clock the preceding day (Saturday). On examining :he premises on the following Monday morn- ing t..e found a window broken at the back, also the Mt. rule and a piece cf leather missing. He valued them at 6s. Gd. The prisoner had no right to the art icles. _</p>
        <p>James Danks, a cycle dealer, residing at 3, Bewspy. 'street, stated that about 10,20 on Janusry 17th he beard a smash of glass at the rear of his premises. Being suspicions he at once went in search of the police. 'He found Sergeant Dean in Horsemarket-street, and they proceeded to the spot. He was with the police and helped in the capture of the prisoner. Sergeant Dean stated that on receiving information from the last witnesF, he went in company with P.C. Armstrong. On looking through the broken window he saw the prisoner striking matches and examining various articles in the shop. Witness caused the build- ing to be surrounded and entered it by the broken window. He found the prisoner in one of the second ti.tor rooms crouching in a corner. Witness asked prisoner what he wanted there and he said " You trn just in time ; you have caught me." Witness then took him into custody. On the way to the Police Station, prisener told witness that he 'did not intend to go into Ba3ley's, he wanted to 04 into Monnlield's. On arrival at the Police Station prisoner said" I did break in and I was caught." The rule was found in his possession, and the leather was found ju.it outside the shop. The prisoner after being formally charged stated that the evidence was correct. Ile was committed to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BURGLIR CAUGHT.</head>
        <p>A BURGLIR CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>.16hn Williams, asvmmart, was charged at the Borough Police Court on iVednesday with breaking and entering the dwelling-house of Ann Boardman, 3, Manchester- road, and stealing /I tablet of soap the previous night. The Chief Constable stated that Miss Sarah Boardman was about to retire to bed (her mother and sister had already done so). She happened to go into the hall when she saw a light in the parlour. She was afraid and went and told her mother. Immediately afterwards a loud knock came to the door and the light went out. She went and admitted P.C. 5t (Taylor) who bad been told of something in Church-street. He had seen prisoner through the window before going in the house. On searching the room he fonnd prisoner under the ofa . inspector Forster arrored on the scene and between them they searched the prisoner on whom they found it watches. 4 silver guards. and other property including the tablet of soap. When charged at the Police Station prisoner replied, " Yes, that's right; that's all I got.' Prisoner had gained admittance by breaking the window and pushing the window Weil 'back. He (the Chief Constable) asked for a remand of .eight, days in order to make inquiries regarding the stolen property. The remand was grantel, and prismter will be brought tip on February 3rd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE LTRERALTSn.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE LTRERALTSn.</p>
        <p>---- --- The LancaNhire and Cheshire Liberal Federation on Wednesday held its first general council meeting at the Reform Club. Liverpool, 11r. B. S. Johnson presiding. —Sir John r. Brunner, Bart., M.P.. was elected presi- Aient of the association, and Mr. Harold Elverston (Manchester) hon. secretary, the following Executive Committee being bppointed :—Messrs. J. BUR; (North T,onsdale), William Brimelow (Westhoughton), Allan Bright (Liverpool), Hartley Ernmott (Burnley), William Healey (Heywood), N. W. Helme, M.P. (Lane:tater), Sir Frank Hollins, Bart., (Blhckpool), J. B. Leach (St. Helens', G. W. Ma c,alpine (Accrington), Colonel Pi!cher (Stretfurd), J. G ri fithm (Chester), Dr. Hodgson (Crewe), T. A. Rigby (Eddisburyl, J. Fergus Smith (Wirral) find W. E. Thompson Altrincham). —After speeches h; Mr. Archibald Williamson. M.P., Mr. A. H. Scott, M.P.' Mr. Allan H. Bright, and Mr. J. L. E. Brunner, a vote -of confidence in the Government. was adopted and .officers %‘ere elected, the new organisation making an tispicious start upon its campaign in the cause of pro- gress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WORKS MANAGER'S TORMENT.</head>
        <p>A WORKS MANAGER'S TORMENT.</p>
        <p>ENDURED PII.F.S FOR 20 YEin,.</p>
        <p>Outspoken Gratitude for Zam-Buk Cure.</p>
        <p>Mr. William IT. Evans, of 20, Beaufort Bond, -Edgbaston, Birmingham, manager of a large transfer printing works, has told a " Birmingham Echo " Teporter how Zam-Buk ended for him years of torture from bleeding piles. " Twenty years ago I began to suffer from piles,The said, " and as I lost a quantity of blood, I consulted a medical man. After treating me for some time with medicine arid ointment the doctor adroeated an opera- lion as my only chine of being cured. I refused to be operated on and instead underwent a special course of treatment for about twelve mouths. My improve- ment, however, was very short-lived, and sron I was in a worse state than ever. I scarcely knew bow to drag myself to work. Besides enduring the terrible smarting and itching, I had pains in the back and was -very languid anA weak through loss of blood. I tried all kinds of remedies hut I could not obtain any errnanent relief. At times I was so dreadfully ill that I had to keep to uny bed for a week at a time. " I went to tie hospital for treatment to enable me to walk about. I spent pounds in m.dical advice and ordinary ointments, and was again told by each •doctor that n:y only hope of cure was I y operation. For nearly twenty years I suffered, and had come to ihe conclusion that there could not possibly be any 'hope for me. " But an accident happened that brought new luck. My daughter had been using Zam-Bilk for some pur- :pose, and one day when I was feeling unusually had 1 was drawn in curiosity to the box of Zam-Buk she bad in the house. I tried the balm and was surprised ot the relief I got. With each dressing of Zam-fluk -my pain decreased. Tie bier ding stopped, and the piles gradually disappealed. Since that time, now nearly twelve months ago, therehas not been the slightest sign of their return, and my general health is all that could be desired ; in fact, better than it has been for twenty years or mote. I would not upon any account now Le without a box of Zarn-Buk in the louse."</p>
      </div>
