      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>INFANTILE MORTALITY. Address to the Women's Liberal</head>
        <p>INFANTILE MORTALITY. Address to the Women's Liberal</p>
        <p>Association</p>
        <p>Another of the series of educltional meetings promoted by the Warrincton Women's Liberal Associa- tion was held at the Liberal Club on Monday, when Miss Sara Andrew.of Manchester, delivered an address on "Infantile Mortality."</p>
        <p>Mrs. PARKER presided over a good attendance and in the course of a short address thanked the members for the honour they had done her in electing her to the presidency of the Association. She must really admit that questions of a purely politic nature did not excite her deepest interest and sy.npithies but as soon as political questions included the human interest and touched those sides of life with which they as ' women were so familiar, her sympathies were aroused to the utmost. The subject they had before them that night was not only of deep hut, she thought she might, sny, of tragic importance. Many of them would prob- ably be present at a sery large and influential meeting held in the Town Hall some three Months ago when they had very fine speaking on the question and when deep feeling appeared to he stirred,so much so that they hoped some steade, organized effort for dealing with the question in their oxn town would be made. She , thought a great, many would be willing to assist if such work could he commenced, and although Miss Andrew had told her that Warrington was by no means the worst of towns as far as infantile mortality was concerned, those who read and knew anything of Warrington lower Me recognized how had it wa.. Tt seemed such a strange thing, con- tinued Mrs. Parker, that one child should he surrounded from birth with all that made life coodifand happy and everything that wisdom and intelligence could do to make that life all that it should be, while there were hundreds and hundreds of little ones who came into j homes ehere they were not even welcome to start with, ' and Wlere it was qnite impossible for them to have any. • thing like a fair start in life. The grentest trouble of it was that where the most ignorance prevailed it was often the most difficult to help. The woman who was sensible and intelligent was generally ready to listen to suggestions and to carry them out to the best of her ability, hut when they approached the real, down-right ignorant person she generally did not need to be told her business ; she knew it all. Tt was a very hard question to solve because, after all, every woman was . supreme in her own home. Whether it was a palace or a slum dwelling, it was the woman's own domain and one had to he careful in offering anything that would ' seem like interference with that home which was prized quite as dearly by the poor as by the rich. Mrs. Parker expressed regret that Mrs. Osborne, their energetic ; secretary, could not be present owing to ill-health and : also announced the receipt of letters of apology for non-attendance from the Rev. 0. Harvey Cook and Mrs. Welker. In the course of his letter the Rev. C. Harvey Cook wrote : "I ant heartily glad that the women Of the Liberal Association are taking up the infant mortality question so earnestly. It, is worthy of their utmost and deepest attention and I hope the counsels and suggestions of Miss Andrew may lead to the women of Warrington inside and outside the Liberal Association taking up the cause of the babes." Miss ANDREW said it was a strange thing that with all the talk of Great Britain's downward trend through the declining birth-rate, the great statesmen and philosophers were only just beginning to realise that there was an enstrmons wastage of human life—snob a wastage that when one came to think of it was simply appalling. She thought it was one of the healthiest signs of modern politics that Parliament was con- descending to take a more active interest in the welfare of the infant population. Not until the subject of the declining birth-rate was brought to the notice of our statesman did they take any interest in the dreadful infantile mortality. Any efforts to stem that were made purely by private Philanthrophy.Thecsre of the children of the nation should he one of the first duties of any Government which wished to keep its country well in the van of progruss. (Applause.) The small children of to-day would he the citizens of to-morrow, and if their lives were being dwarfed in childhood by disease and bad environment, what could they expect the next generation to he like ? (Hear, bear.) It was plain front the evidence vhich had been produced that the dwindling birth-rite was rorfined to the tipper classes of society and did not apply to the working classes. The evil was that so many of the children did not survive. If some of their philosophers would devote a little attention, not to the birth-rite, I tut to the prevention of this dreadful crime of our modern life. they wont I he working in a far better direction than by worrying about things they coldi not alter. (items hear.) Our future citizens were being reared chiefly in homes where poverty failed to provide the necessary food, clothing and healthy environment. Yet the moment legislation was proposed to protect the children, surehody said " You are undermining parental responsibility and making a Socialistic State." As to the doctrine of the " survival of the fittest," the philosophers aho so calmly consigned one-sixth of the English population to an early grave, quite overlooknd the fact that besides the thousands who were killed there were as many thousands left to linger on to manhood and womanhood, dwarfed, stunted, diseased, and ill-fitted mentally, physically and morally to battle with the world. The same conditions which killed off thousands of children also produced the greater number who were not in any sense working for elßciency. Therefore no legislation should be opposed, which had for its ()Neel. the betterment of the children. A statistician, Dr. Newman. said that. the number of children who died in England and Wales every year before they reached the age of 12 months was 120,000. He reckoned that one-sixth of the human race in England died before they reached the age of one year. The death- rate per 1.000 of children under one year was 152, whereas the normal death-rate of men, women, and children, per 1,000 was 10. The death-rate was very heavy in towns which were ieditstrial centres and heaviest of all in towns ahere married women did a great deal of the work. The infant death-rate in Lancashire was 170, whereas in Westmoreland, where the geographical and climatic conditions were practically the same, it was under 100. The reason the industrial centres stood out pre-eminently in the matter of the death-rate over other districts, was that PO many married women laboured in the warehouses, factories, mills and workshops. Miss Andrews dealt at son e length with the evils of the system of married women labour in the factories, pointing out that infants %tete in many cases taken from home in the early hours of the morning and given in charge of some person while the mother worked in the mill all day. Another. c else of the great infantile death-rate was improper feeding. In many fairly well-to-do workmen's homes children were being killed by improper feeding, just as others in poverty-stricken dwellings were being starved to death through want of food. This was due to ignorance. Before we could do away with married women lahaur we must alter the whole social system, because married women did not as a rule 'go to work unless they were forced. The lecturer pointed out bow the child of a mother given to habitual drunkenness came into the world heavily handicapped. Artificial feeding was a great moire of infantile mortality. To bring up a child on nrtifici food the most extreme care and cleanliness must be exercised, hut this was often neglected. With resard to a great, many of the infant fonds which were boomed so largely, there was no guarantee as to what they contained. It would he a good step if all infant foods were included in the working of the Foods and Drugs Act. They should be certified as harmless and nourishing and a full anal3sis should be printed on every package. Some of the foods were good but others ! were not far removed from poisons. Pure milk was another important factor. So long as our milk supply was under private oanership, she was afraid we could not answer for it being all that it should he. The result which had followed where Corporations or private bodies of philanthropists had opened pure milk depots . had hen astounding. If the as-taking of these depots • could be extended and the parents attending them taught some of the principles for the preservation of Want life. there was no doubt we should have tackled one great evil at its sourse. Discussing remedies, Miss Andrew said the first must be the betterment of the cenditions of the working classes to obviate the necessity of married women labour. To quote the words of their late lender Sir Henry Campbell Banner- ninn, "every woman should have good hou,ing,good air, gold food, good water and a certain amount of leisure." Ii by degrees we could raise the working man, so that he would be the breadwinner and the woman the house-keeper, it would he much easier to train the mother to treat her child properly. Miss Andrew emphasised the importance of early notification of births to he followed by the visi!atien of a ladies' ' committee, who would give advice to the mothers. She did not know whether the members of that Association . believed in votes for women or not,liut she thought that if the level-headed women aho had made this question one of deep study could have a direct influence on the Government, they would have a much greater advance in the laws which affected the well-being of the chil- dren and in the next ten 3ears, as woman's influence came more and more to the front, she was hopine there would he a large decrease in the rate of infantile mortality. If they could point to that, the women would have justified their claim not only to the vote bin to anything else they could lay their hands on.(Laughter and applause). Mrs. PARKER said she hoped that the girls would I soon be educated on this question in the schools, as they already were in regard to cooking and hygiene. After the meeting held in the Town Hall a short time ago, she had hoped that something of an organized character would have been done in Warring. to deal with infantile mortality. In Liverpool a creche had been opened, with accommodation for forty babies, but they rarely got more than four or fise. As a rule the working woman hated organisations and it would be necessary to show her that they were working with perfect friendliness towards her. She (Mrs. Parker) would feel very sorry if the care of the children were to he taken over by the State; it seemed to he working ,at the wrong end altogether. What they must do was to help and. educate the mothers. On the motion of Mrs. ,tstasoN seconded by Mrs. LINDLEY, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Miss Andrews for her ndmirable and informing lecture., Mrs. LETHEREN in moving a similar compliment to Mrs. Parker said they admired not oily her fund of infortnation but the delightful way she. had of expressing herself. I</p>
        <p>The rote wits also ec;rdially agreed to and the meeting dosed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Former Warrington Curate's Promotion.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. S. Cameron, vicar of Weston, near Spalding, has been appointed by Lord Lilford to the living of Atherton Parish Church, vacant by the resignation of the Rev. W. Nuttall. Mr. Cameron,who will commence his new duties next month, held curacies at Grassendale and Warrington before gcing to Weston.</p>
        <p>Warrington Philomathic Society. A meeting of the above Society was held in Gregory's Caf6 on Wednesday evening last.—After the ordinary business had been transacted, the debate " Should Home Rule be granted to Ireland" was opened in the affirmative by Mr. It. G. Pierpoint. He was opposed by Mr. E. G. Burgess, and an interesting discussion followed.— The majority of the members supported Mr. Pierpoint. BARG Auci in Silver-plated Teapots, Cruets, Pre- serve Dishes, etc.. during Sale.—.T. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Wscliffe Young People's Society. . _ _ _ - -</p>
        <p>Councillor Hawthorn presided at the weekly meet- ing which was held on Monday evening in the Memorial Hall. The Rev. Harry Wall contributed a paper on " A trip round the Antrim coast." It proved to be very interesting and some splendid views were shown by the aid of the lantern. At the close a hearty rote of thanks to the essayist was proposed by Mr. F. Womersley, seconded by Mr. Millar, and supported by the Rev. J. Yonge and carried. Warrington Women's Circle.</p>
        <p>Sunday was an open meeting at the Circle. Rev. R. E. Letheren presided, and Mr. Herbert Buckby offered prayer. Mr. John Upson gave a forceful address to young men and women which was appre- ciated to the full. The following musical programme was given. Solo, " Into Thy Hands " Miss Hamer : two violin solo' s Mr. Millington ; song, " Gates of the West " Miss Plinston ; duet, " When I survey " Mrs. Pearl and Miss Hewitt ; duet, the Misses Hamer. PUBLIC BF.VEFIT.—UNDER THF. CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRF..—Advt. Warrington Goods Train Wrecked.</p>
        <p>A goods train bound from Buxton to Warrington, was running down a gradient at Cheadle, near ganchester, on the London and North-Western Railway, early on Wednesday morning, when it broke in two. The front portion was pulled up at a point near Cheadle Station, and it was run into by the rear waggons. Two waggons were forced over the embankment, and four others were overturned, con- siderable damage being done to the stock and the permanent way, and both lines being blocked. No one was injured..</p>
        <p>" Y " Branch N.B.W T.A. The usual weekly meeting was held on Wednesday evening when Mi-s Beswick presided over a good attendance of members. After a few introductory remarks Mrs. Owen of Penketh read a pretty aid interesti..g story called "Me an my Blessin Box," and after her closing remarks presented each girl with a prayer card. During the evening Mr. W. ll* Owen sang " The Toilers " and "The Powder Monkey," and Mrs. Owen played a pianoforte solo. Four pledgcs IV( re taken at the close of the meeting. CLEARANCE SALE of Table Lampe, Flanging Lamps, etc., great hargains.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Field Club. The opening meeting of the second half of the win:er session was held on Mondav,Mr. F. Womersley being in the chair. Mr. F. Fuller delivered an inter- esting and instructive lecture on "Plant Propagation." The subject which was illustrated by diagrams and by numerous specimens, was dealt with mainly from the standpoint of the practical gardener and showed how careful observation of the habits of growth and vary- ing rssibil tics of reproduction in different groups of !lams had led to these being made URA of for tl:e service of man. In the discussion which followed, Mr. Fuller was warmly congraturated on his admirable and valuable discourse. Penny Popular Concerts. - - - -</p>
        <p>- A high-class programme, arranged by Mrs. Hui&amp; Newcombe, was submitted at the popular concert held in BAI-street schools on Saturday evening, and afforded great delight to a large audience. Miss Lily Locke played several pianoforte solos with taste and skill, and she was particularly successful in "Scotch airs" and " Trish airs." Songs were given in good ,tyle by tie Misses Gandy, Florence Brown and Niolet Brown, and Messrs. W. 11. Ireland and Wallace Ray. Miss Btown and Miss Gandy were encored for their excellent rendering of the duet "When shall we meet ? " "The tickling ttio" was given by Miss Brown, and Messrs. Ireland and Ray, and the part song "In this hour" was well rendered by Misses Brown and Gandy and Messrs. Ireland and Ray. The humorous part of the programme was in the capable hands of Mr. Alfred Grounds whose sketches created great merriment.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND ' for Coqghs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists only. 91d.,15.1id.,25.9d.-Advt Warrington P.S A. Society. _</p>
        <p>Sunday's meting of the ‘l.7arringten Society at the Friars Green House was the occasion of the quarterly distribution of prizes. There was a large attendance. Mr. Ming, an old friend of the P.S A. movement, pre- sided and spoke upon the subject of temperance. Au address was also delivered by Mr. Waddell, of Liver- pool who took for his text " The glory of man is his strength," dividing his subject into three divisions strength of bAy, mind, and character. While the body required proper food in order to build up physical strength, so the mind must be improved not only by reading good hooks but by conversation that was pure and actions that were beneficial to man- kind generally. Man must also have strength of character and nobleness of purpose. The President, in expressing the thanks of the meeting to the speaker, hoped the time was not far distant when Mr. Waddell would pay the society another visit.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER MR CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Chlid's Sad End. Mr. Coroner Brighourre held an inquiry into the death of Edith Griffiths aged two years, daughter of Albert William Griffith's, ironworker, 90 Fony-lane, at the Police Station on Monday evening.—The Coroner stated that on Thursday last the mother Edith Griffiths, was cleaning the front bedroom and she placed her three children, Doris aged five, Edith aged two and Fred aged seven months in the back room. There was a fire in Lids room with an ashpan in front but no fire guard. The mother had been working for ab nt an hour when she beard screams issuing from the room, and on going to see there found Edith in flatnea. She threw a rug round the child and extinguished the flames. A cab was obtained and the child was taken to the Infirmary. The child Doris had told her mother that Edith was standing in front of the fire, when a spark flew out and set her pinafore on fi e.—The mother stated that she had employed a girl to look after the children until the previ u; Saturday and on that day she left.—Nurse Gerrard of the Warrington Infirmary said the child died early on Saturday morning.— The Coroner remarked that the mother had done all she possibly could in the circumstances.— A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned hy the jury.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL SALE of Tea Services. Dinner Services, etc., great b3rgains —.T. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. Meetings. The weekly meeting of the Wycliffe Church P.S.A. Society was held on Sunday and was very well attended. The S.cretary (Mr B. Farrar) presided and spoke upon " The objects and aims of the brotherhood." The first object was to bring men to Christ and the sec ad to make good men, happy fathers and faithful souls. They had always kept that to the front. When a song was introduced it was to drive home both musk and words. A short time ago a song was rendered in that Arita that in ,ved him to the very depths of his soul. At a conference of P.S A. Societies of Lancashire and neighbour- ing counties yesterday, continued the speaker, a stirring and interesting address was given by Mr. William Ward author of " How can I help England." At the close of his Address he (the secretary) asked him to give the conference what his own experience was in visiting the P.S.A.'s all over the country and abroad, with respect to the semi-concert. He said he was delighted the question had been asked. The P.S.A. men and men did not want the concerts. They could have them any day. What they vant was a good stirring gospel address, and where the concert element had been tried it soon came to grief. When solos were given they were intended to draw out the test in the brotherhood and the address was to lead heavenward. Mr. flUnsworth gave an address upon " Playing the fool." Mr. Joseph Owen took part in the meeting. Mr. R. Wood sang effectively " The soft Southern breeze " and " The sailor's grave."</p>
        <p>LINSEED CO \WOUND' ( %niseed, Senega. Spill a..), for Coughs. Of Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>GAS MADE FROM WASTE CORKS</p>
        <p>Home-made gas from cork refuse is used by Spanish peasants in the cork-oak region. As described by M. Lodian, the process consists in filling several large tea-kettles with the waste bark, and piecing each in turn over the fir-? during the evening, burning the volatile gas as it escapes from the spouts. The car- bonised residue forms the fine black-brown pigment known to commerce as "Spanish brown."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MOTOR CURE FOR AN=A.</head>
        <p>MOTOR CURE FOR AN=A.</p>
        <p>Judiciously used, the automobile may offer a remedy for nervous and mental affections. Dr. Mounteyrat, a French physician, ven- tures the opinion that motoring is a cure for anannia and sleeplessness. He conducted some experiments during several tours of eight days each, and states that he found a decided increase in the number of red cor- puscles. The average increase figures out at 26 per cent. The effect of a week's touring is much the same as that of residence on high altitudes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>DANGERS OF LIVE WIRES.</head>
        <p>DANGERS OF LIVE WIRES.</p>
        <p>The increased use of electricity on railways ha l led to a study of the dangers of hand- ling live wires, and the methods that must be employed in resuscitating those who have been stunned by an electric shock. A special pair of pliers has been designed, which, ac- cording to the Scientific American, enables a man to cut a live wire carrying 23,000 volts without danger to himself. To remove the wire from a body when no other means are at hand, a coat is placed under the wire, and lifted by the sleeves, to raise the wire off the Lody. This was found perfectly safe, even when the garment was damp. Experiments with fire streams shewed that there was no danger of the current flowing down the stream of water even from a high-voltage line when the operator held the nozzle at a distance of between 3ft. and 4ft. from the ire. Experiments with chemical extin- guishers skewed that they were very danger- ous where a solid stream was played on the wire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SNOW AS A FERTILISER.</head>
        <p>SNOW AS A FERTILISER.</p>
        <p>rrom experiments conducted at Ottawa, in Canada, it appears that there are some slight grounds for the widely - accepted opinion among agriculturists that snow is a direct fertiliser, says the Pharmaceutical Journal. It is found to contain total nitrogen equiva- lent in round numbers to about 11b. per acre of land covered by an average winter snow- fall in that district. The amount of nitrogen as free an:monia was high, but fluctuated greatly, from 0.082 to 0589 parts per 1,000.000; the nitrogen as albuminoid am- monia ranged from 0033 to 0-078 parts per 1,000,000, and the nitrogen as nitrates ranged from 0027 to 0390 parts per 1,000,000. The average of twelve determinations from Febru- ary 21st, 1907, to May 4th was, nitrogen as free ammonia 0256, as albuminoid ammonia 0052, and as nitrates and nitrites 0.163 parts per 1,000,000. The value of snow as a direct fertiliser would appear, so far as the nitrogen content is concerned, to be greatly overesti- mated. It is intended to continue the experi- ments both in summer and winter to deter- mine definitely the manurial value of both snow and rain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SEEING UNDER WATER.</head>
        <p>SEEING UNDER WATER.</p>
        <p>The swimmer may open his eyes under water, but he sees very poorly. The reason is that the index of refraction (power to bend rays of light) of the water and of the aqueous humour (the watery fluid which fills part of the eye) of the eye are about the same, the result being that when the cornea (the trans- parent part of the external coat of the eye- ball) is in contact with the water it no longer has any refractive power, and on this the power of vision depends, but the light passes on to the crystalline lens of the eye unaffected at the surface of the cornea. The power of the lens of the eye in situ is such as would make the focus of parallel rays of plight about 14in. behind the retina (the hack of the eyeball), which is the same as though the eye were hypermetropic (far-sighted). In other words, the normal sighted being who opens his eyes under water will see objects beneath the surface of the water with the same indis- tinctness that a hypermetrope (one who has far-sight) would see objects in air. This being SO, it can be readily understood why we can- not see well under the water.</p>
        <p>DRAIN WEIGHT AND INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>-There is something very mournful in ' the labours of those scientists who have devoted their lives to the study of the brain weight of men, women, and races •on the assumption that there is a direct ratio between intelli- gence and the bulk of the brain, says Profes- sor W. I. Thomas, writing in the American Magazine. It would be about as valid to as- sume that a vessel of water and a vessel of lye of the same weight have the same potency, or that timepieces of the same 'weight are necessarily equally good timekeepers. Great men may have great brains or they may not. Turgenieff holds the record at 2,012 grams, while the brain of . Gambetta, who was a greater man in popular estimation and popular achievement, weighed only 1,160, or only 160 grams above the point at which, according to the calculations of French anthropologists, idiocy begins. In .a series of 500 brains the lowest and highest will, in fact, differ .as much as 650 grams in weight, but there will be found</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>NO CONSTANT RELATION</head>
        <p>NO CONSTANT RELATION</p>
        <p>between the weight and the intelligence. It is significant, indeed, that men of small stature, weak health, and even physical afflic- tion, have, if anything, more than an ordi- nary chance of becoming famous. Their attention is limited, and they are, stimulated to in out in spite of their handi- cap. Pasteur is a clear case of a truly great man. He was paralysed on one side from 1868 until his death in 1895, but, as Derthol- let says, it was after he was stricken that his inventive genius perhaps shone most brightly. Herbert Spencer, Darwin, and Von Hartmann hardly had a vell day in their working lives. Pope was so feeble that he could hardly draw on ,his own stockings. Napoleon was of small rtature, and of weak health and physique.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RADIUM IN GRANITE.</head>
        <p>RADIUM IN GRANITE.</p>
        <p>The exietence of radium in the earth may have important effects in modifying its physi- cal constitution. It has been suggested, for e::aniple, that its presence might retard the cooling of the earth, and so lengthen the time hitherto allowed by physical considera- tiOns for ite geological history. Hence special interest attaches to the investigations of the Hon. IL J. Strutt, who hes specially Studied the manifestations of radio-activity in the rocks and minerals as they occur in the carth's crust.</p>
        <p>Pitchblende, the mineral from which radium was first obtained, occurs in veins in the granite rocks. In pitchblende radium is always associated with uranium, and the amount of the former is always proportional to the latter. It is, therefore, believed that radium originates in uranium. , The latter spontaneously changes into radium, and the amount produced annually is proportionate to t:1-2 amount of uranium present. And experi- ment shews that the amount of radium in ritelible.nde is strictly proportional to that of Lranium.</p>
        <p>Whence comes the uranium of the pitch- Llende veins in the Cornish granites? Does it come from the granite? This is one of the questions investigated by Mr. Strutt. In any ease, the amount of uranium present would be too small to be detected by ordinary means. Hence no uranium appears in the results of tlic chemical analysis of granites. But if there is uranium there will be radium also, and radio-activity. And even minute quanti- tim' of the latter can be detected. It a given rock shews traces of radio-activity it insy be inferred that minute quantities of uranium are present. Mr. Strutt has applied this test to Cornish granite and other rocks, and in every case the presence of radium has been proved. The quantity, it is true, is in- finitesimal. only about one-millionth of what occurs in pitchblende. Small as tam anima is. however, it is sufficient to support the view that the uranium of pitchblende hal itil original rourcp in the granite. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON " WARRING TON."-Mr. Alderman Bennett last Monday evening gave an interesting lecture in the Wesleyab schoolroom, Padgate, on Warrington." There was a good attendance. Mr. .Tar-es Gandy of Enfield Hall, presided. Both the lecturer and the chairman gave not only interesting details with regard to the history of Warrington, past and present, but also gave personal reminiscences in connection with the town. A vote of thanks was dulytaccorded to both gentlemen at the close.</p>
        <p>PADGATE LAWN TENNIS CLUB.—The annual social in connection with the above club was held last night week in the Padgate day schools. The proceedings which consisted of a whist drive and a dance were of a highly successful character upwards of SO members and friends of the club being present. The president of the club (Mr. H. S. Page) announced the names of the winners of the club's tennis tournaments, and congratu- lated them on their success which had only been earned after a strenuous struggle. The prizes were gracefully presented to the winners by Mrs. Page, as follows : Tennis tournaments (mixed doubles) : 1, Miss Ella London and Mr. Stanley Adamson ; 2, Miss Marion England and Mr. Hugh Mellor. Singles (president's prizes) ladies : 1, Miss Marion England ; 2, Miss Mabel England. Gentlemen : 1, Mr. Eastwood; 2, Mr. E. England. Whist drive prizes, ladies : 1, Miss Dora Royston ; 2, Mrs. A. 0. Hi Id tch. Special prize: Miss Short. Consolation : Miss Hetherton. Gentle- men : 1, Mr. J. C. Warburton ; 2, Mr. 11. Houghton. Consolation prize: Mrs. J. Bennett (playing as gentle- man). After the presentation of the prizes a vote of thanks was accorded to the President for the manner in which he had carried out his duties during the past season, the audience supplementing with a cheer and musical honours for himself and family. The arrangements were carried out by the president, the hon. treasurer, Mr. E. Lunt, and the hon. secretary, Mr. P. H. Bennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>FIRST STEPS TO CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST STEPS TO CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>Disease's deadly progress arrested by</p>
        <p>New Good Blood</p>
        <p>A remarkable instance of the powers of Dr. Williams Pink Pills,</p>
        <p>Remembering the lamentable sufferings endured by his father, which culminated in a Consumptives' death Mr. E. Garnham, of 24, Wellington-street, Bradford, was able to detect the plesence of this dread malady in himself at an early stage, and so successfully did he fight the disease that now at twenty six years of age he is a strong young man, able to lift heavy weights, and without a trace of Consumption in his system. Mr. Garnharn is well because New, Good Blood was given to b;m by Dr. Williaros'Pink Pill-Rich Blood that promptly arrested the Consumpticn- and eventually banished it com- pletely from his system. " When after an illness," Mr. Gatnham stated to a reporter, 'I developed a constant hacking cough, and felt pair # in my back and chest,with feelings of great weakness, I had reason to fear for myself. Later on, I repeatedly coughed up blood, spent the night-time in weakening sweats, and saw myself getting thinner. " My face was pallid, but about my cheek bones I looked unnaturally flushed, and there was a bright glassy look in my eyes. I could not take -proper food, but just picked at my meals. I became so weak and my lungs were in such a bad state that at last I went to the hospital. There I received skilful treat- ment, but lost ground, and prepared for the worst. I had a knife-like pain with every breath, and the cough often prostrated me. My shoulders drooped so that I could hardy hold my head up. .1 got as thin as a rail and so weak that I had the greatest difficulty in walking about. " At this stage I remembered that my sister who had been very ill was cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. So I decided to try them. " I took two or thee boxes of the pi ls, and then, as I continued them my appetite gradually returned. I felt a desire for food, whereas previously I had destested the sight of it. nen, as my cure pro- gressed, I felt warmer, and I. knew that new blood %%ai coursing throu</p>
        <p>R. EDWARD GARNHAM (from a photograph).</p>
        <p>" Gradually my cough became less irritating, and the pains across my chest were not so intense. I kept taking Dr. Williams' Pink I'ills and my strength came back.. I ate well and slept well. All fear of the Consumption was banished, and I grew strong, hearty, and robust. I regained my lost weight, and wcigh now 10A. 41135., and can carry a load of nine stones on my back nearly a mile without any distress." TIIE DR. WILLIAMS' WAY.</p>
        <p>By giving Good, Rich Blood and building up the system against the ravages of deadly diseases, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have effected many wonderful cures of Anaemia, General Weakness, Decline, Back- aches, Indigestion, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia, and Paralyt-is ; also the ailments from which women alone (-offer. Sold at shops (but look for the full name Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People -on every peonage), or direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46,1101b0rn Viaduct, L.ndon, post free for 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 138,9 d.</p>
