      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>LICENSING BOY ACTOR_ AT WIDNES._</head>
        <p>LICENSING BOY ACTOR_ AT WIDNES._</p>
        <p>_ Stanley Radcliffe made an application at the Widnes Police Court on Monday for a licence to permit a boy, aged 12,1 years, to perform at the Alexandra Theatre in the play called "The Better Laud." Applicant said the boy would only be engaged from eight to ten o'clock. He would attend school so that his education would be looked after.—The Bench, after a lengthy demur, granted the application.—Mr. Thomas, the magistrates' clerk, reii.arking that the Bench discouraged those applications.</p>
        <p>DEAR UNCLE NED,</p>
        <p>-+-</p>
        <p>I hope you spent a happy Christmas. If I am not too late I would I.ke to wish you a Bright and -Prosperous New Year. I am sorry I have not had the opportunity to enter for the Clothing Competition, but I must try to do better another time. Although I could not eto this I would like to make some child happy and warm, so have bought two little vests and am sending them to you to dispose of. Hoping you will be able to find some use for en- closed garments. Your loving niece,</p>
        <p>CLARA DAY.</p>
        <p>Thank you very much, Clara, for the parcel, which has come to hand. It will make a poor little child happy and warm. UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>JUST ONE LIFE. (From McCall's Magazine.) A Ii tie lass, just eight years old, Eyes like the skies and hair like gold, Off to school in a froc of blu With a basket of lunch and a book or two.</p>
        <p>This is Susy A debutante of sweet eighteen In a satin gown, all shimmer and sheen, Flushed and eager at her firs ball, With a score of men at her beck and call. This is Sue!</p>
        <p>Twenty-eight, and is affably bored Vita Engl :nd after a year abroad, Her maid and poodle a c brought from France, And she reads French menu. now at a glane:.</p>
        <p>This is Susanne! Tin y-eight, and a spinster still, The List few years have trea ecl her ill; Fler hair is gre,-, and her face has creases, As she darns the stockings of five little nieces.</p>
        <p>Th:s is Aunt Susan! FLORENCE DARK.</p>
        <p>-+- THAT NAUGHTY BOY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stinger had a particular method of his own in dealing with the various tradesmen who called at his house with the usual request for Christmas-boxes—that is, he had until the butcher's boy called. Then he began to consider whether his plan was as good as he at first thought it to be. Pursuing his usual custom, he opened the door himself in answer to the butcher-boy's knock, and allowed the youngster to begin. This is what followed— Boy: " 'Wish yer merry. Christmas, Sir."</p>
        <p>Mr. Stinger: "Just so." " An' a 'appy New Year." "Just so." "An'—an'—all the rest of it, Sir." "Just so."</p>
        <p>There was an impressive silence, and Mr. Stinger flattered himself that he had crushed the applicant ; but he had not. "I say, Guy'nor„" ventured the boy, "it wouldn't 'tirt you very much ter say, 'Same ter .you,' would it? If yer poor, yer can at least be perlite." By the time Mr. Stinger had recovered his breath, his / visitor had swaggered down the steps.</p>
        <p>C. A. BLISSITT.</p>
        <p>THE SUNDAY JOINT. For Sunday's dialler we can boast, We have a leg of mutton roast; On Monday, if the truth be told, We eat it with some pickles cold! Oa Tuesday we some slices fry; On Wednesday it is mutton pie ; • On Thursday we, to cut a dash, Regale ourselves with mutton hash; And, that our meat may longer last, On Friday we proclaim a fast; On Saturday, all tastes to suit, We crack the bones and make some soup. THE CLEVER CROW.</p>
        <p>There was once a crow who was very thirsty, who, when seeking, found water. The water was in a jug, so it could not be reached by the crow. Anxious to obtain water, the crow set thirO.ing of a way. There was a pebble close by, so the crow threw it in the jug. The wa splashed out, laid his thirst was quench d.</p>
      </div>
