      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>AN EASY GAME FOR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>AN EASY GAME FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Treherbert were the visitors to Runcorn on Saturday, and there were 1,500 spectators. Padbury placed the home team ahead with a penalty goal in the first minute and following this Kennedy scored after running three- parts the length of the field, Jolley adding a goal. Edwards and Thomas then had hard luck on the Runcorn line. The home forwards rushed the ball to the other end, Butterworth scoring at the corner. Padbury failed at goal. The ball was sent to Riley, who failed to get possession, and Hanford scored a grand try, which Galloway failed to convert, Resuming, Richardson and Butterworth each added a try for Runcorn, Padbury improving the last one. Just before the interval Griffiths crossed the Runcorn line after a fine effort, Galloway failing at goal. Half-time score :—Runcorn 3 goals and 4 tries (18 points), Treherbert 2 tries (6 points.) In the second half the visitors were seen to better advantage, Francis dropping a capital goal Francis, Williams, J. Thomas, and Hanford took play well into the Runcorn half, where Jolley was hurt and retired for a few minutes. During his absence Dixon scored another try for Runcorn, which Richardson failed to improve. In the closing stages Kennedy got over after a smart run, Jolley just missing the goal. Result : Ruacorn 3 goals and 6 tries (24 points), Treherbert 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WIDNES BEAT LETGET.</head>
        <p>WIDNES BEAT LETGET.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before 1,000 spectators. In the first few minutes Lavery broke away with a clear field, but stumbled, and lost possession. Leigh had the best of matters, and Isherwood let a glorious chance slip by in consequence of not taking a pass when well placed. Widnes then attacked, and Taylor crossed the line, but the referee ruled that the ball had been thrown forward. Both Neville and Isherwood almost scored for Leigh, but at half-time there as no score. The second half was in favour of Widnes, but the attack was weak. Jones had an unsuccessful shot at goal, and then in the last few minutes, when play was on the home line, Lambert picked up smartly, and gave to Ince, who, after running more than half the length of the field, scored a magnificent try. G. Aspey failed at the plice. Result :—Widnes 1 try (3 points), Leigh nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LANCASHI RE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHI RE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>*Leigh Shamrocks l3 Widnes *lnes l2 Runcorn *Pemberton 9 Wigan Oldham *Warrington *Wigan II igh field * lienotes home club. -I- Also South-East Lancashire League. WARRINGTON "A "v. ST. HELENS " A." These teams tried conclusions before a large crowd at Wllderspool on Saturday. Some interest centred in the re-appearance of T. Hockenhall in the home ranks after a long rebsence. St. Helens won the toss andeh kickinglof ls aLby by ferois; placedgaint eitdnh eaahnli usdolslcsi side ref: owgon Cartwright gnuthenhitde wtboeyr attack.heea easily iVIy judiciousan rdcrhi itvenoicgduktcueohadni and the visiting forwards, ably led by the ez-Chadderton fell-back, Atkinson, made a determined raid upon Mclntyre's charge, but the home custodian saved well. Lees broke away cleverly and gave to Hockenhull, and that player made a smart dash along touch and when confronted by Hillen passed back to Lees, but the transfer was ruled forward and a certain score was lost. Warrington were, however, soon rewarded, the ball being taken over by Cheaters and Gibbs and a try registered. Cartwright was successful with the kick. The defence cf flillen, Toole and Drake was good and the " Wirepullers" were kept at bay until St. Helens were penalized and Mclntyre 'sicked a goal. Half- time :—Warrington " A," seven points ; St. Helens "A," nil. In the second half the home team demon- strated their superiority, and the passing being more accurate they were almost always on the attack. Jordan was working hard to got Bradshaw over and at length succeeded, the winger scoring a grand try. Lees again got the passing machinery in motion and the ball travelling to Bradshaw, he made no mistake about registering his second try. Both goal kicks were unsuccessful. The visitors were now quite out-played, and Bradshaw once more took the ball over and appar- ently grounded it and then lost it. Chesters was handy, however, and Mclntyre converted. Final Warrington " A," 3 goals, 4 tries, 18 points ; St- Helens " A," nil.</p>
        <p>INCE V. RUNCORN "A." Ince opened very vigorously this half, and M'Cann scored, the goal being missed. M'Cann made Brine run, but the visitors kicked dead. The game was more equal in this half, though the hornsters held the upper hand. Brciwn again got going for Ince, but the leather was sent dead. Final: Ince 12 points ; Runcorn 2 points. LETOTI SHAMROCKS V. WIDNES. At Leigh. Widnes started playing the kick and rush game, Ganley landing a goal from a penalty. Lloyd made a sensational run through the visitors' team, scor- ing under the posts, Riley failing at goal. Resuming, Widnes pressed, but finished badly. Lloyd again broke through and gave to Kelly, who scored, Riley failing at goal. Flighana then scored, Devereux gosling. Final:— Leigh Shamrocks, 13 points; Widnes" A," nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>L</p>
        <p>THE THIRD TEST MATCH TEAM.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Australian Sub-committee was held in Manchester on Tuesday evening. The arrangements for a match to be played at Celtic Park, Glasgow, between England and the Australians on Wednesday, February 3, was confirmed. The team to represent England was selected as follows :—H. Giflord (Barrow) full-back ; G. Tyson (Oldham), J. Lomas (Salford), G. Dickenson (Warrington) and W. Batten (Hunslet), three-quarter backs ; E. Brooks (Warrington), and J. Lally (Widnes), half-backs ; A. Mann (Brad- ford), A. Smith (Oldham), %V. Longworth (Old- ham), W. Jukes (Hunslet), R. Higson (Hunslet), and A. Robinson (Halifax), forwards. Reserves : W. B. Little (Halifax), full-back ; E. Ward (Halifax), centre, and W. Miller (Wigan), wing, three-quarter backs ; W. Cleave (Wigan), half-back; F. Boylen (Hull) and R. Padbury (Runcorn), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WARRINGTON " A " LOSE A COMBINATION POINT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " LOSE A COMBINATION POINT.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Northern Rugby League Committee was held in Manchester on Tuesday. The Lancashire Combination match, Runcorn "A " v. Warrington" A," fixed for January 1, was cancelled in consequence of the bad state of the ground. It was decided that each club shall count one point in the table in accordance with Combination rule 6.</p>
      </div>
