        <p>STIFFNESS AFTER EXERCISE.</p>
        <p>Everyone knows that exercise is good for the body; but, if indulged in excessively or without irjuring oneself to it gradually, it produces stiffness or soreness of the muscles, which will be felt for days afterwards unless promptly relieved. • To counteract this effect there is nothing better than Dr. Sloan's Liniment. Lay it on lightly wherever the muscles have bem unduly strained—it requires no rubbing in—and it gives at once a warming and com- forting sensation, which denotes a freer and more elastic condition of the muscular tissues.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. J. Burnett, 30 Lincroft-street, Moss-side, Manchester, writes :—" I have been intending to write you for some time to tell you what good Las a cyclist, find in your liniment. After a long and trying ride, I have proved this liniment a sure preventive of stiffness and pain. It is far more effective in this respect than any of the other preparations I have tried." Dr. Sloan's Liniment is a boon to athletes. Not only is it good for stiffness and aches, but for sprains, bruises, cramp, rheumatism, neuralgia, and many other painful affections it is indispensable. Dr. Earl S. Sloan has issued a book giving many valuable hints on how to keep muscularly fit, as well as some practical advice on what to do in emergences. This Book will be sent free to anyone applying to the Distri- buting Depot, 32 Snow Hill, London, E.C. Dr. Sloan's Liniment may be had of all Chemists is. and 2s. 3d. a bottle.</p>
      </div>
