        <head>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</p>
        <p>and educated in the world, but they have more' comforts, more independence, more of them live in houses that they own, more of them have savings in Savings Institutions, and are better contented, than their rivals anywhere else, and this, according to my view, is the result of Protection—of the protective system that was inaugurated by the Republican Party."—(House of Representatives, April 6th, 1882). One thing that I am unable to understand is why our workmen are flocking to America if trade is as • bad there as Mr. England would have us believe. Mr. McKinley in another speech once said that Englishmen came to the United States for increased wages and they The theory of " Preference" is that if we do got them. not give a material advantage to the Colbnies by I contend that the unemployment in this country is taxing foreign food and letting Colonial food larger than that in Protected countries. If our work- ing men are desirous of obtaining more work I think come in free, the Empire will break up. they will find that Tariff Reform will give them that But South Africa does not export food at all advantage. Mr. England well knows that for years - and the only way to give South Africa a prefer- unemployment has never been so bad as it is to-day. ence worth having is to tax wool and copper. If he will only stroll through the streets of Warrington "Tariff Reformers" should explain how they he will see hundreds of men walking about for the want intend to deal with this point in view of the fact ao bfies )employment.utr,enaiaTe haea me ma jp' ol roity gio of tt hies s ;ear rteb en os ta kuenee ifn Ill) eo sye- that they vow they will never tax raw materials, men, their wives, and their children that I am a disciple The same difficulty occurs with other Colonists of Tariff Reform. I feel that this so-called Free who do not export food. Canadian lumbermen, Trade has had a fair trial and proved itself a for example, can get nothing out of a food pre- hopeless failure. Distress at present existing is terrible. ference. re 0 .C. M. Working men scorn charity. What they want is work ae and it is the duty of every Englishman if he considers that Tariff Reform will give these men work to drop LETTERS TO THE EDITOR. his party politics and to consider only the betterment and uplifting of his fellow men. -- Apologising Mr. Editor for the length of my letter, Old Clothes Wanted. and thanking you for granting me so much space in your valuable paper.—l am, yours truly. Sir,—May I trespass upon yr ur kindness to make an ; G. H. WARBURTON STEEL. appeal on behalf of the children attending our Etona, Marshhouse-lane, Warrington, elementary schools? January 27th, 1903. Many of our children are feeling the effects of the cold weather on account of the lack of clothes. Tho other day the headmaster of one of our schools SAW a THE CHARGES AGAINST A boy with blanched cheeks and fingers blue with cold, and asked him whether he was cold. The boy bravely, DIRECTOR. replied that he was not,but further examination revealed the fact that tall the boy had on was an old overcoat, and that too big for him, no under coat, no waistcoat, I but next to his body was an apology for a shirt in the i ANOTHER COMMITTAL. form of a butter muslin rag. In another school an I assistant mistress out of pity for one of her girls spent Theft or Financial Adjustment ? her dinner hour in drawing together filthy rags, which few of your readers would care to touch, so that they Mr. Louis 'Henshall, Runnymede, Stockton Heath, would not fall from the girl's back. I could give many director of Messrs. It A. Naylor, Ltd., timber more examples. merchants, Howley Mills, again appeared at the War- Our teachers do what they can and whilst many : rington Police Court on Saturday to answer a farther children are warmer to-day through the, thoughtful- nese of the teachers, they could do much more if 1 . ch_arge of stealing on February ts:oth, 1901, the sum of they had the means, but the