        <p>XV.—lf "ties ofiinterest" are neces- sary to bind the Empire, together, how is a " Prefer- ence " to be given to South Africa unless we tax raw materials P</p>
        <p>(c) ,a,_ Arils or - ,rge, an, Jo took your three months' acceptance ?"—lfut his letter says he has disposed of the matter with R.A.N. unemployment relate solely to Trade Unionists Naylor. In receipt of unemployed benefit. They do not 4 Mr. Swift : Ile had undertaken to pay £4,250 and as That is by some arrangement with Mr. R. A. N. he include those out of work through sickness or regards that, £3,750 had actually been paid. £5OO had has taken himself the liability for it and has kept your superannuatio bill of exchange which won't be due for three months ? n. not been paid and that we are charged with stealing. This last consideration makes it clear that not only is it Whether we stole it or not is a question your worships —Yes, so that he may pay R. A. N. were at the time ?--Certainly not. impossible to compare these figures with the figures for are investigating. You don't know what the terms with R. A. Naylor es ple,of the American Trade Union returns which do Wiiness continuing, spoke to further correspondence include these out of work thrqugh sickness, superannu- completing the transaction as far as Messrs. Myth and There is a letter in which he says something about ation and other causes—but also that they cannot be taken Platt were concerned. In the company's hooks the the financial position, Did you hear at that time that as in any sense complete or satisfactory evidence of the Payment of £4,250 had always been treated as a payment he was not only finding money for Mr. Rowe, finding actual number out of work even amongst the Trade to R. A. Naylor on account of work and labour and no money to settle with Hayes and to guarantee Nnylor's Unions reported to our Board of Trade. After this Part of that sum had been returned to the company. account, but he was also finding money for R. A. Naylor ?—I don't know anything about that, beyond ments." , ' I am making new arrange- explanation I trust Mr. England will not quote me any No claim had ever been made against the company for figures to prove that his contention is right. , £5OO in respect of that. It had never been suggested the letter in which he says Returning to Mr. England's quotation, for his benefit, ' by prisoner or anybody that a smaller sum than £4,520 I might tell him that that phrase was culled by me from had been paid. It first came to witness's knowledge a I don't know whether you heard at the time he drew a speech by the late Presidect.McKinley on this very few days ago that only £3,750 was credited in Messrs. his money from Blyth and Plats, that that was because question. With your s permission, Mr. Editor, I will Naylor's hooks. Prisoner became a director of Messrs. he was going into R. A. Naylor' company which was then being talked about ?—Quite likely. As far as I ; ' quote a little more from that same speech. 1 Blyth and Platt, Ltd. and ultimately chairman of President McKinley says : 1 directors, on the death of Mr. Rowe. In July 1906 remember, I believe Mr. Henshall told me as much. "The fundamental argument for Protection is its prisoner resigned his directorship and on 15th Septem- I suppose you would have no objection to an benefits to labour. . . . There is not a branch ber 1906 the last of his shares, except one, were trans- accountant looking at any account you have or Rowe of labour in the United States that does not ferred by him. On October 2nd 1903 witness paid to receive higher rewards than in any other country. Messrs. 'Naylor £5OO for which a receipt was given and For Children's tackingeough at night, Wood's Dur labourers arc not only tho best paid, clothed, £250 on October 24th. On March 18th 1901, prisoner Great Peppermint Cure, Is. 1. 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
        <p>gave a receipt for £1,012 in settlement of account for work done at Watford. A total payment of £6,012 was therefore made. Witness produced an voice from Messrs. Naylor for the work in connection with the new establishment amounting to £6,012. That was the amount they understood to be due but there was no complete contract. The firm had a further transaction with Messrs. Naylor who acted as their agents in the purchase of an engine, boiler and dynamo from Messrs. Scott and Mountain before November 1904. At that time one payment had been made on account to Messrs.; Naylor. In November 1904 a sum of more than £lOO ; was due by way of balance. Witness produced a letter from prisoner dated 7th November 1901 written from his private house, stating that he had received an urgent letter from Messrs. Scott and Mountain demand- ing some money. He continued : "Do you accept the account rendered by us to which was attached Scott a, d Mountain's account, subject, of course, to the work being satisfactorily completed. If so, if you will send me your three months acceptance for the amount, pins 5 per cent interest, I will personally discharge the account, remitting at once £4OO, holding the balance over for a week or so until you are satisfied. Return accounts and any corres- pondence relating to money under cover to me." At that time prisoner was still a director of Messrs. Blyth and Platt and it was not at that moment con- venient for the company to find £lOO in cash. They fell in with prisoner's suggestion and became acceptors of the bill for £lOO which was endorsed " Louis Henshall, Parr's Bank, Ltd., Warrington, to Manchester Branch." On November 12th, 1901, prisoner wrote again from Runnymede acknowledging receipt of the bill and stating : " I will send you on Monday R. A. N.'s receipt and I expect £lOO was sent to Scott and Mountain. If not I shall do so on Monday, sub- ject to everything being in order. I hope you will find it convenient to remit the small balance early. I want to keep the account from our stocktaking balances, if I can. I never expected such a prolonged transaction." On November 19th, 1901, prisoner wrote again from his private house enclosing formal receipt for £lOO and asking them "to address all communications respecting this machinery account to me, to obviate the necessity of letting the account appear in the stocktaking balances. I have dealt with the account, in so far as it affects R. A. N." The formal receipt was signed "per pro. R. A. Naylor, L. H." The £4OO was treated by Messrs. Blyth and Platt as a payment on account of their indebtedness to Mr. Naylor in respect of the machinery. On December 18th prisoner wrote enclosing a further small account from Messrs Scott and Mountain and asking the firm to remit or tack to the balance in R. A. Naylor's statement £5l ss. " Although I have dealt with the account in so far as it affects R. A. Naylor," the letter continued, "Scott and Mountain's frequent letters for settlement come to the office and our people don't like these applications. Remit under cover to me. I want it out of hand." In a letter dated January 2nd 1905 prisoner stated : "If you have not already posted the promised cheque, I hope you will send under cover to me. As I explained,l relieved R. A. Naylor of the account, so that it should not appear in outstanding balances at stocktaking." The bill for £lOO was passed through the bank by the prisoner and honoured. Witness produced an account of February 1905, in prisoner's handwriting, for "three months' interest on the £4OO bill due February 12th £5, and discount 155." Tne firm paid the £5 15s. and they understood that by means of the bill they had paid Mr. R. A. Naylor for the machinery supplied by Messrs. Scott and Mountain. By Mr. Swift : Witness took up his duties as director of the firm of Messrs. Blyth and Platt on October Ist, 1901. He was, however, in sufficient touch with the business before that date to know all the facts he had related. He had intimate knowledge of the formation of the company. He did not know how much was owing from Mr. Rowe to ft. A. Naylor on February 20th, 1901, or how much he was owing to Alr. Hayes, who was interested in the business at Altrincham. Roughly, the amount owing to Hayes, before he was settled with, was £3,000. Mr. Swift: Can you tell me how much Rowe was owing to R. A. Nalyor on February 20th, 1901? Witness : It was a peculiar transaction. There was no contract, so far as I know, and Rowe was in the hands of Henshall as far as the amount owing was con- cerned. So far as I know, no statement was made, except by Henshall. Did that statement show that Rowe was owing some- thing over £4,250 ?—Witness stated that the letters were all ha knew about the amount owing to Naylor's. The gentleman at Altrincharn (Mr. Hayes) was press- ing for his money and Mr. Naylor was pressing forhis, so that altogether Mr. Rowe was being pressed for something like £7,000 or £B,OOO. Da you know he could not find the money ?—Yes, I know that. Do you know that unless somebody had come to his rescue, Hayes would have lost his £3,000 and R. A. Naylor would have lost whatever was owing to him ? No, I would not put it that way. What would have happened ?—The business was a valuable one. It would not have been wrecked. Some- one would have taken the business over. Would it have paid 55. in the £ ?—I should say so, decidedly. The building was not even completed or paid for ? No, but the buildings were there and the business was there. I should say it was worth more than that. They would not have been paid in full, I don't suppose for a moment. Unless someone had come to finance Rowe, neither Hayes nor Naylor would have been paid in full ?—I don't suppose they would. The person who did come and finance Mr. Rowe was Mr. Hensbaill ?—Largely. Not alone. Do you know the extent to which Mr. Henshall did it P—From memory, Mr. Henshall found, so far as I know, about .24,000 to £5,000, the most substantial amount of anyone. I will put it at £s,ooo.—Yes, roughly. Do you know that to raise the money Mr. flenshall had to mortgage his house ?—f don't know that. He told me he had to raise money on securities. It was not suggested on the 19th February that the whole of ale liability to Naylor's would be wiped out? —No, I don't remember that that was so. Do you know, or is there anything in the books which shows whether before February 19th Mablenshall bad made any payment to Naylor's on account of Rowe P—No. The only payments we know of are the two receipts 1 have put in. You don't know whether before that Mr. Henshall had made a payment of £2,000 to R. A. Naylor on account of Rowe P—l don't. In this transaction when the prisoner gave a receipt for £4,250 no money passed ?—So far as I know, it did not. I don't remember any money passing. That receipt was given in order to make a financial adjustment between three parties—Rowe, Henshall and R. A. Naylor—wasn't it ?—No. I don't quite follow. The position was : Henshall lending money to Rowe in order that Naylor's should no longer press ? Yes. In fact no money passed. R. A. Naylor gives Rowe credit for something and Rowe gives Henshall credit.— Mr. Henshall at the meeting promised to advance £1,250 and to pay it to Naylor and to take a charge for it. Whore was the money to come from to settle with the Altrincham gentleman, Mr. Hayes ?—The charge was a mutual one. It was not given to Henshall alone. Mr. Latham was finding £1,500 and another gentleman £5OO. Mr. Hayes, you know, was to have £3,000 ?—Yes. Didn't be have something of the £1,250 ?—I cannot tell you. I don't know how the moneys were made up to pay him. Before February 19th had not Mr. Hayes been paid up ?—I don't remember the exact date. First of all Mr. Rowe, and then Blyth and Platt have always been credited by R. A. Naylor for £1,250 ?—We got the receipt. And you have never been asked to pay it again ? —No. In the final settlement it was regarded by you as a payment of £4,250 ?—As far as I know. You didn't know anything at all about the arrange- ments between Mr. Honshall and R. A. Naylor at that time ?--No. You never saw Mr. R. A. Naylor ?—No. You knew he had some information about what was being done, didn't you ? I see in one or two letters his name is mentioned as corning to see you in London ? I never saw Mr. Naylor. Do you know that Mr. R. A. Naylor signed the trust deeds for the debenture holders ?-lle was a trustee for the debenture holders, but I don't think he ever saw his colleagues. That is the amount of interest he took in it. His signature was witnessed by Mr. John Naylor ? That, I cannot remember. Have you a separate invoice for the boiler, from the £6,012 ?—Yes, I believe there is. Scott and Mountain would debit R. A. Naylor ?—Yes, Henshall told you he would personally pay it ?-11e didn't say he would personally pay it. He would pay it through Naylor's. He would pay Naylor's and they would pay the account. Mr. Henshall wrote: "If you will send acceptance I will personally discharge the account"—With Naylors'. From that time onwards be said : "Don't send the matters about these occounts for the machinery to R. A. Naylor. It has not to appear in the stocktaking balances. In order to prevent that I will discharge it," ad be took a -</p>
        <p>undertaken to' pay an equivalent sum to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>had with R. A. Naylor ?—I cannot bind my co-directors but, as fares I am concerned, I see no objection. Alderman Tinnion : The receipt signed by Mr. Henshall states : "formal receipt to follow." Did you ever got a formal receipt ?—Not that I know of. That is the only receipt you have ?—I believe so. I never saw another.</p>
        <p>And you were satisfied with that ?-0h yes. You see we ultitnately got the invoice which is put in. We asked for that repeatedly. The invoice for £8,900 covered that £4,251 Wa3 the receipt written out at the meeting on the 19th February ?-31 far as I know, it was. Mr. Sivift : Did you ever ask for a formal receipt ? —No.</p>
        <p>Further questioned, witness said it would be wrong to say they knew they had not plid the £4,250 but that Henshall was paying the money for them. Mr. Swift: Taey knew they were credited with £0,012 by Naylor's. Mr. Hewart said he agreed that so far as the £4,250 was concerned, it did not purport to be a payment direct to Henshall. It was a payment to the prisoner upon the undertaking of the prisoner to pay it to Naylor's. Mr. Swift: It was not a payment to the prisoner at all.</p>
        <p>Frederick Hargraves, accountant, Manchester, stated that in 1903 and at the beginning of 1901 ho was acting for Mr. Rowe in connection with the conversion of his business into a limited company. At the meeting on February 19th Mr. Heashall proposed to pay to Mr. R. A. Naylor£4,2so on account of the contract with Mr. Rowe and then to find an additional £l,OOO towards payments due to Mr. Hayes. On the following day there was another meeting, when the solicitor had pre- pared the necessary documents, and the receipts were handed by the prisoner to Mr. Rowe. Charles Albert Littler, clerk at Parr's Bank, produced the prisoner's banking account for 1903-4-5. The bill for £lOO (produced) was presented for payment and passed through the bank on January 25th, 1905. Walter Mather, chartered accountant, Manchester, stated that he had examined the journal, cash books and ledgers of Mr.R.A. Naylor relating to the transactions in questions. He had compared them with a copy of the ledger account of Messrs. Blyth and Platt. The cash book of the latter firm contained an entry under date February 20th, 1001, of a payment of £4,250" to R. A. Naylor, G. W. R. account." There was another entry of a payment on the same account of £1,012 on March 18th, 1901. On October Ist, 1904, there appeared the entry "by R. A. Naylor, £500," and on October 23rd, 1903, by R. A. Naylor, £250." The total of all the items was £6,012. In Messrs. Naylor's ledger there were corresponding credit items except as to the £4,250. In respect of that sum only £3,750 was credited. The debit items were on October 31st, 1903, "proportionate amount of goods on account contract £2,500," on November 30th "to goods £3 4s. 10d.' " January 28th, 1904, "to goods, £2,501 10e.," (which in the journal was split up into two items, namely 12 trays £1 103., and proportionate amount of goods on account of contract £2,500), 30th March, 1904, "to goods £3l us. 91," April, 1901 (in the journal) "amount balance for material and labour supplied, all to schedule, nett, L. H., £lOl 51. 2d." Excluding the last item, the total debit was £5,039 Cs. 7d. Three items were for goods and came to £39 6. 7d., and the total of the two proportionate amounts of "goods on account contract" was £5,000. It followed that if £5,512 was paid in respect of it, the sum of £512 was over-paid. It appeared that Messrs. Blyth and Platt received credit for two payments of £lOO and £7 Hs. 10d. Deducting £lO7 14s. 103. from the £512 left £404 ss. 2d., the amount of the entry initialled by the prisoner. The effect was to make the contract appear as for £5,512. If the amount brought in on 20th February, 1901, had been £4,250 instead of £3,750, the total would have been £6,012as shown on tbe invoice received by Messrs. Blyth and Platt. After searching Mr. Naylor's books he was unable to find any trace of the £5OO paid in, neither was there any entry relating to the £4OO note alleged to have been paid to Mr. Henshall. The account was concluded with an item of £37 and in the journal it was described as "commission on the pnrchase of engine and boiler, in settlement." There was a further entry in the ledger of £lO described in the journal as "travelling and incidental expenses in re engine." He did not anywhere in Messrs. Naylor's books find any- thing to justify the debit entry of £37. The £lO was all right. It did not appear that the previous sum of £5OO was accounted for to Messrs. Naylor. Mr. Swift: Did you come across any account between Mr. Henshall and Mr. R. A. Naylor in the books ?—I have come across an account with Mr. Henshall, just an ordinary account for goods sold, but I have not seen any other account with him.</p>
        <p>- You know, I suppose, that a great many monetary transactions took place between them ?—I only know that from what I have heard in Court.</p>
        <p>Is there any account anywhere to show that Mr. Henshall adyanced some time in 1901, £2,500 to Mr. Naylor ?—I have not seen such an account, if there is one.</p>
        <p>Is there anything in the books to show that later on in 1904 he advanced £4,000 ?—I have not seen the account if there is one.</p>
        <p>Your attention has not been called to any such account ?—No.</p>
        <p>Was there anything to show that Mr. Naylor was paying Mr. Henshall interest for some time ?—I have not seen anything of that kind. Is there any account which shows what Mr. Naylor when he died was owing to the business of Naylor, Ltd. ?—I have not seen such an account.</p>
        <p>Yon don't know whether it was a large amount or not ?—No. I don't know.</p>
        <p>Take this £1,250 which Blyth and Platt's cash book gives as a payment. Is it not obvious, looking at both sides of the page, that it is a book-keeping entry and no money pissed ?—There is an entry on the debit eide of the account as well to show that a certain sum has been received from flenshall of £4,250. Is it not quite obvious to you as an accountant, that no money passed ?—No. It is not conclusive to me, because in some instances cheques would be drawn on either side.</p>
        <p>Have you found any trace of such cheques ?—I have not seen Blyth and Platt's books. The suggestion that no money passed is quite con- sistent with what you find in the books ?—Yes. Your ignorance as to the relations between R. A. Naylor and Mr. Henshall with regard to the .24,250 applied equally with regard to the .2400. You don't know anything about that do you ?—Well, I have searched the books for the £4OO to see whether it has been received and it is not entered in the books.</p>
        <p>You have no idea why ?—No. With regard to the £37 you say it is entered in the journal as commission paid or payable ?—Yes. Where would you expect to find the commission if there was one paid, except in the journal? Am I right in sayng that if it was a commission, there is all in that you would expect to find about it ?—No. I think it ought to be entered somewhere else. Will you suggest where you would like to find it entered, as an accountant ? You are the first person I know of who has ever suggested there was anything wrong about the £37, and I want to know what you say is wrong about it.—l say what is wrong about it is this : That on the 10th January, 1905, a sum of £37 was received from Blyth and Platt. Well, it required an entry to be made to balance that.</p>
        <p>And the entry had been made in the journal that it was for commission on December 31st,—Yes. Then what should have been charged to Blyth and Platt should have been for the engine and the boiler. You mean instead of being entered by the clerk as to goods he ought to have entered it as commission re engine and boiler ?—No. The engine and boiler should have been charged to Blyth and Platt at the amount they were to pay for them. Further questioned, witness said he could not see it was obvious that the entry "proportionate amount" meant that part had to be charged to Naylor's and part to somebody else. Mr. Swift: All this depends on the relations between R. A. Naylor and Henshall in the financing of Blyth and Platt, doesn't it ?—I don't know that it all depends upon that. If there was to be a division between R. A. Naylor and Henshall on the financing of Blyth and Platt, would not that account for the figures re" proportion for engine, boiler, Stc. "?—No, I don't think so. Would it not be an explanation of it ?—Not a satis- factory explanatioq. Not a satisfactory explanation to an accountant, pro- bably a satisfactory explanation to whoever was making up these books. It is quite clear from the bank book, is it net, that there was some financial relation- ship between them ?—I have not seen the bank book. PROSECUTION SUGGEST ADJOURNNIENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BENCA SAY CASE MUST GO ON.</head>
        <p>BENCA SAY CASE MUST GO ON.</p>
        <p>- i -Arr. Hewart : That is as far as the prosecution s pre- pared to carry the case to-day. There will be some little remaining evidence ppm this charge, and there will probably be one other charge—not more,l think. Mr. Swift asked that the case in reference to this matter should be completed that day. It put him in a very awkward position to have a charge half-investi- gated. Mr. Henshall had been in custody for a week and he (Mr. Swift) knew perfectly well that until the whole of the charges had been investigated and the Bench saw what finally the prisoner was going for trial upon, he could not hope that the justices would change their determination of keeping him in custody, but as soon as they had heard all the charges he hoped to be able to convince the Bench that the prisoner ought to be again liberated on bail. It was very unfair and very hard on Mr. Henshall that a charge— Mr. Hewed : I don't think my friend is entitled to use the word " unfair."</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: I don't impute any intention to my friend to be harsh. It suits the convenience of the prosecution, for some reason best known to themselves, not to go further to-day, but I ask the Bench not to allow them to adopt that course unless some better reason is given. I don't suppose they want to injure Mr. Henshall or that they recognize the difficulty they are putting me in. Mr. Hewart said he had never, in such experience as he had in practice of criminal law, heard it suggested that the prosecution should be dictated to as to how many witnessses it was to call on a particular day. Judging from the length of his friend's cross-examina- tion on previous occasions, he had not anticipated that they would be able to call so many witnesses rs they had called that day. He had called the witnesses with whom he was prepared. His friend had cross- examined very little, but they could not foresee that. If remarks were to ha made on the way in which the prosecution was conducted, he could only</p>
        <p>say tlytt those who did not know the facts had no con- oeption of the mass of material that had to be investi- gated and the number of years which that material referred to. They were conducting the prosecution with the utmost dispatch which the circumstances and nature of the case permitted. Mr. Swift: I never in my experience knew a Bench of magistrates dictated to as to the time at which they should rise. Mr. Hewed: lam not dictating. . . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: One of the 'witnesses whom I have cross-examined all through has not been called to-day. The magistrates retired to consider the matter and on their return the Chairman said they were unanimous that that case should be finished that day. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hewart : I am not sure that I understand exactly what that means. The Clerk : The magistrates have decided that they will not grant any further adjournment upon this case. . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman : On these two cases. You have had plenty of time to prepare. Mr. Hewart : Of course, the Bench has made its decision and announced it. On behalf of the prosecu- tion, I respectfully submit that if that decision means that if we are not able to complete our case to-day we are not to be able to tender additional evidence con- cerning it, it places us in a position of unprecedented difficulty. The Chairman : That rests with yourselves, as to whether you will tender additional evidence. It is not a question for the Bench. Mr. "lowan : If the decision of the Bench means that we are not to be permitted after to-day to offer upon these charges any evidence in ad dition to the evidence which we are prepared to offer to-day, then I say the Bench is really saying that the case is to be cut short.-</p>
        <p>leir. Swift: What I understand the 136nch to say is that so far as this particular case is concerned, they intend to deal with it tg-day either by sending it for trial or refusing to do so. The Chairman : That is so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : If it is sent for trial, my friend may give as much additional evidence as he likes by goin g through the proper formalities. Mr. Ele,,vart : I agree, if he is sent for trial, but if it were to be said that the evidence has not been com- pleted and that upon the evidence as submitted we do not think a prima facie case is set up, then I submit we ought to have an opportunity of calling further evidence which we shall be prepared to call.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We are here to listen to this case and if yon bring evidence we are prepared to listen to it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : This is about the eighth time Mr. Flonshall has been up, having charges made against him. I don't know how many more days we are going to have here. He has aim Idy been committed on seven charges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CASE RESUMED.</head>
        <p>CASE RESUMED.</p>
        <p>Edward Jones, clerk in the employ of R. A. Naylor, Ltd., stated that there was an entry in the journal "to amount of balance for rn Aerial and labour supplied all to scheinle II.) £4Ol ss. 2-1." dated April. The entry was in the prisoner's handwriting. There was an entry for October, of "to amount not included in schedule as proportion of engine, boiler ecc., outside contract, £293 193. 101." That entry was also in the prisoner's handwriting. Under date December 31st, 1904, there was an entry "commission relative to purchase of boiler, in settlement £37," also in prisoner's handwriting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SECRB rARk CROSS-EX XMINE D.</head>
        <p>SECRB rARk CROSS-EX XMINE D.</p>
        <p>John Edwards, director and secretary of R. A. Naylor, Ltd., stated that before the limited company was forrred he was cashier to Mr. R. A. Naylor. He remembered the firm being engaged in the erection of the works at Watford. At that time prisoner was a traveller and he took entire &amp;large of the work at Watford. Witness had examined the books relatin,g to the matter. There were no special instructions with reference to the entries in the books with regard to the Watford undertaking. He agreed that the entries mentioned by the last witness were in the prisoner's handwriting. The bulk of the entries in the book were writted by a clerk and in the ordinary course the entries in the journal would be made by He had not been able to find any particulars in the books as to how the entries in question were made up. As far as he could ascertain no part of the sums of £5OO and £4OO had been paid to Mr. R. A. Naylor. The counterfoil receipt book produced was the usual form of receipt in use at R. A. Naylor's at the time. The receipt for ,2400 (produced) had not been carbonised by the usual method but was written direct on the paper In ink. He had not been able to find the book from which the receipt was taken. He had, however, the book immediately following that one. Apparently the £4OO receipt was the last one in the missing book. There were 100 forms in a book. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: Upon whose instructions were entries made in the books of R. A. Naylor with reference to the work at Watford?</p>
        <p>Witness: They were made in the ordinary way. Upon whose instructions, anybody's ?—No. No special instructions wore issued with regard to that. Are we to understand from the evidence you have just given that no receipts were given between the 17th November, 1904, and January 10th, 1905 ?—Oh, no. This receipt is dated 17th November, 1904, and is marked" received November 21st, 1991." That book which you say is the next to it does not begin till January 10th. How many receipts had been given in the meantime ?—Another book probably. Where is that ?—I don't know where the book is. Have you looked for it ?—Yes. How many receipt books have you got ?—That (the one produced) is the oldest I could find. What has become of the receipt books before that? They have been destroyed, apparently. Who by ?—I don't know. In the ordinary course of business ?—I suppose so. I cannot find any earlier books. You said they must have been destroyed in the ordinary course of business. Do you mean it or not ? I mean I cannot find them.</p>
        <p>Am I right in saying that up to January 1905, there must have been dozens of such receipt books ?—Yes. Hundreds ?—I would not say hundreds. You are cashier and, I suppose, responsible for the receipts. Do you know whether it was usual to destroy them ?—After the lapse of a few years. Have you any doubt that the book from which that receipt has been taken has been destroyed in the ordinary course ?—lt may be that the books for a year or so before that have not been destroyed, but I cannot drop across them.</p>
        <p>Was it you who alighted upon this discovery about the receipt books and pointed it out to the advisers of the prosecution ?—No. That is not part of your ingenuity ?—No, You were the cashier and I suppose you knew exactly what was being done from a financial point of view by Mr. flenshall and !qr. R. A. Naylor with regard to Blyth and Platt?—There was no special arrangement regarding Blyth and Platt.</p>
        <p>What is the use of telling the magistrates that, when they have been listening to Mr. Brown and Mr. Hargraves telling them of spacial arrangements? Did you know Mr. Henshall was finding £4,250?—N0. When did you learn that ?—f havo not learnt it yet. When did you first hear it ?—I first heard it this morning.</p>
        <p>- Did you ever know Mr. llenshall had got Rowe's deeds as security ?—No. Did you know that Mr. Henshall or Hr. Naylor had any interest in Rowe's business ?—I have heard that Mr. Henshall had. Ido not know. Do you know whether Hr. Henshall found any money for Mr. R. A. Naylor ?—No. You never heard of it?—Mr. Henshall told me once that ha had done. That is all I know. Well, don't you know it?—Flo said so. How were you to know, if you were not to get to know from the one who did know, and he told you? —To get it from the books of R. A. Naylor. It was not in the books P—No. Therefore I do not know it officially. Did you know, officially or otherwise, that R. A. Naylor was paying interest to Mr. Henshall ?—No. Did you ever draw cheques for interest ?—Not that I know of.</p>
        <p>Did you got some payments from Mr. Honshall to R. A. Naylor ?—No. I may take it you knew nothing about the transaction i of £2,700, which s in the bank book ?—Mr. Naylor did not take me into his confidence.</p>
        <p>That is what I thought. 141 r. Naylor did net take you into his confidence. He did take Mr. Henshall, didn't he ?—I don't knos%.</p>
        <p>Don't you knew perfectly well that Mr. Henshall and Mr. Naylor were very confidential about these matters? —I don't know.</p>
        <p>Did you know Mr. Naylor had signed the trust deeds of Blyth and Platt ?—I don't know. He never told you anything about it ?—No. It is funny that when you and I meet we always get down to the stage at which you don't know anything. Have you noticed that? (Laughter.) Do you remember the company of R. A. Naylor Ltd. being formed ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>Do you remernber Mr. R. A. Naylor wanting Mr. Henshall to take £4,000 in shares of the company in lieu of cash which Mr. R. A. Naylor was owing him ? Mr. Henshall told me that he did. Do you doubt that it was true ?—I don't know. You wore told at the time that R. A. Naylor was owing Mr. Henshall £4,000 and wanted him to take it in shares ?—Mr. Henshall told me. Were you told that Mr. Henshall would not do that and wanted his cash ?—Mr. Henshall told me so. Did he get his cash ?—He said so.</p>
        <p>_ -- —. Did he i:ell you that before he could get it Mr. R. A. Naylor tried to make it a debt of the company ?—No. You have not heard that ?—No. The question as to whether or not it sh be treated as a debt owing by the company r. Henshall was discussed at a Board meeting was ? —I have no recollection of it.</p>
        <p>Did B. A. Naylor ow; the company money ?—When? At any time—if it comes to that, always, as long as there was a company.—There have been accounts between B. A. Naylor and the company. Had B. A. Naylor been owir.g the company money ever since the day the company came into exietenoe ? That is a matter of dispute. Dispute between whom ?—The executors of B. A. Naylor and the company. What do you say ?—I say the matter has not yet been settled.</p>
        <p>For Influenza and Bronchial Coughs,Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. I. lid., 28. 9d.—Advt,</p>
        <p>Do you say or not that R. A. Naylor was owing the company money ?—The amounts have not been settled. Are you going to answer the question or not? D 3 you say that R. A. Naylor was owing the company money ?—I cannot say until it is settled, whether his claim and our claim—</p>
        <p>Are you as a director of the company claiming on R.A. Naylor's executors ?—Yes. For how much ?—We don't know how much it is. I How much have you asked them to pgLy you ?—They are claiming against us. How much have you asked them to pay you ?-oh, several thousand pounds: _ _ _</p>
        <p>How many thousatxls is several ?—I cannot say. Let me see if I can help you. Had you asked them for £ll,OOO ?—I don't think so.</p>
        <p>Have you asked them for £1.0,000 ?—I cannot say whether it has got that far or not. Are you quite sure whether it has got as far as 29,000 ?—I cannot say positively.</p>
        <p>Have you instructed our solicitor about it ?—No. Have you consulted them at all about it ?—We hays talked to them about it, but not what is generally understood by instructing solicitors.. Your view is that they are owing you something over £B,OOO ?—Well, I don't say the amount. Several thousand pounds ?—Yes. Doesn't it come to this, that R. A. Naylor owed the company money. Did John Naylor owe the company money ? I think you told me two or three days ago that he did but there was a settlement by credit at the bank 2—Yes.</p>
        <p>John Naylor owed the company money. Then Henshall made a disturbance about it at a Board meeting and people began to look into lienshall's accounts. Is that so ?—I don't think Mr. Henshall made a disturbance about it at the Board meeting. Shall we say he mentioned it ?—Yes. Henshall mentioned it and then began this investiga- of the books ?—The investigation certainly began. I don't want to suggest that the investigation came out i of it.</p>
        <p>About the £5OO. Who discovered this ?—The account- ant, Mr. Mather.</p>
        <p>Further questioned on this matter, witness said he knew nothing about it until Mr. Mather called his attention to the item. The £3,730 was paid in Parr's Bank to the firm's credit but witness did not make any inquiries as to how it came there as he presumed it was on account of the contract. He spoke to Mr. Henshall about it and he said it was all right. Mr. Henshall had supreme control of the contract and immediately he said it was right no further notice was taken of it. Has there ever been any book in which R. A. Naylor's borrowings from Henshall and his repayments were kept ?—I don't know of any transactions of the kind. But do you know there was £4,000. Yon have admitted that.—But I haven't seen it in any book. I After the company was formed, didn't the company I pay interest on that .£4,000 ?—No. Didn't the company make payments to Henshall ? In the way of salary Yes, and in other ways ?—I have no recollection of it.</p>
        <p>I suggest that regularly you drew cheques to pay Ilenshall's interest and charged them to R. A. Naylor's account with the company ?—I cannot recall any such arrangement. _</p>
        <p>The'Chairrnan : Under 11,.A.Naylor and Co.,Was there any commission paid to Llenshall in addition to hii salary ?—No. Mr. Hewart : Is it not the case that in the time of , the limited company, as distinguished from the private firm, the prisoner was receiving a commission in ' addition to his salary ?—I think not. _ _</p>
        <p>j Do you know at all whether he received a commission at any timo ?—I always understood he received a com- mission before the formation of the company. j That was hearsay wasn't it ?—lt was hearsay. ! There is nothing in the books to show that ?—No. How do you explain it ?—I thought he would do. I was never told so. Mr. R. A. Naylor never mentioned the matter of Air. Honshall's salary or commission to me.</p>
        <p>' It was simply an opinion formed in your own mind? —Yes. ! The Chairman : If commission were paid,who would pay it ?—I should write the cheque out but I would not know what it was for.</p>
        <p>j Police Sergeant Stuart said he charged the prisoner with the two offences and be made no reply.</p>
        <p>SPEECH FOR THE DEFENCE. Mr. Swift said he could not help thinking that the time had come to suggest that the prosecution in this case was going further than was really reasonable. Already Mr. Henshall had been committed to take his trial upon seven charges. Then the prosecution, in addition to those seven, continued to bring np charges, I apparently in every case in which they could find some discrepancy in the books which in the eyes of their accountant was not good book keeping. IMr. Hewart protested that there was no truth in that suggestion,</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift, continuing, said there was absolutely no foundation for the charge which had been made against Mr. Henshall with reard to these two items. The prosecution had actually launched against him the charge of stealing £5OO with no stronger evidence in this world than that in the books there was an entry of £3,750, while a receipt had been given by Mr. Henshall for £1,250. They shut their eyes to the fact that the £5OO never existed. Nobody had ever seen those 500 sovereigns. The money had not been there either in gold or in notes. It, was absolutely nothing but a book- keeping entry. So far as the actual transaction was concerned the arrangement was this : Rowe could not keep on any longer. Naylor's and other people were pressing him for payment. " Very well " said Mr. Henshall" You hand over to me some securities and I will pay £4,250 to Naylor's." " Done " said Rowe, and the securities were handed over. The entry male in the books clearly showed that no money passed. R. A. Naylor's claim, however, was wiped out as far as that £4,250 was concerned. Mr. llenshall had got to square up with R. A. Naylor. He had placed himself under an obligation to Rowe and unless he carried out that obligation he had broken his contract with Rowe. Unless he stopped R. A. Naylor from coming down on Rowe, the latter would have an action for damages against him. How was that to be done? Mr. Ilenshall could do it either by paving £1,250 in cash or. by going to Mr. R. A. Naylor and saying " You owe me this, and I owe you that. I will give you £3,750 and that has to be taken for this X.:1,250 that is due from Rowe. Rowe's account is to be continued and he is not to be pressed." Mr. Hewart : I protest. My friend is stating things to have happened as facts, as to which there is not a syllable of evidence before your worships. It is all the result of ray friend's imagination. Mr. Swift said he had not stated either of these things as facts hut was only pointing out that Mr. llenshall had to discharge his obligation by paying the £4,250 in cash or making some arrangement with Mr. Neylor. There was absolutely no evidence that he did not do either of those things, except that if a payment in full had been made in the office it would probably have been put down in the book as £1,250 instead of £3,750. There was absolutely no evidence that Mr. R. A. Naylor did not know perfectly well what the transaction between Rowe and !Tensile]] was. On the other hand there was strong evidence to show that Mr. R. A. Naylor knew what was going on, knew that Henshall was financing not only Rowe but R. A. Naylor himself, and R. A. Naylor was signatory of the trust deed for the debenture holders. If that was the true position, where was there any criminal offence at all ? What in the world had this man done that any- body could allege was a criminal offence? He had broken his contract, perhaps, with Rowe, unless he could show that he bed satisfied R. A. Naylor about it, that he had squared him in some way. The prosecu- tion charged him with stealing £5OO. That £5OO was never in existence. Stealing implied "to take and carry away" and there could not be any stealing if there was nothing to take away. He (Nlr.Swift) never yet knew a charge of stealing in which somebody could not say the form in which the money was paid over. If Mr. Henshall had had the most criminal mind and the most wicked intentions, he could not have stolen the £5OO, because it was not there for him to steal. He asked the Bench to say it was not good enough, after being committed on seven charges, that prisoner should be sent for trial on more still, on flimsy grounds like that. The same applied to the £lOO. There never was that £4OO. •Mr Hewart • There was a bill for £4OO. __ _ •__ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: Please do not expect me-t.o say every- thing at once. I cannot talk in parallel columns. (Laughter.) He contended that with regard to the £lOO the position was exactly the same in law as it was with regard to the £5OO. Blyth and Platt owed 11. A. Naylor .£4OO for a boiler in respect of which R. A. Naylor owed something to Scott and Mountain on account. Henshall said to Myth and Platt : " Don't send in your acconnt to Naylor's. I will square it with Scott and Mountain. Send me the bill." Mr. Hewart : Where is the evidence of this conversation ? I really must protest against this imaginary. evidence.</p>
        <p>M. Sift : [protest against these constant inter- ruptions. Inever knew them before coming from the prosecution. But then I should not complain ; I never knew a prosecution quite like this before. Continuing, he said the prisoner wrote :—"lf you-will send me sour acceptance, I will personally discharge the account." Surely that meant " If you will give me £4OO I will discharge the account." They sent their acceptance and he cashed it. Where in the world was the evidence that that £4OO was ever sent to him for Naylor's, or that it was ever Naylor's money ? No-one suggested the boilers had not been paid for. No one suggested R. A. Naylor's paid the whole of it, or that Blyth and Platt paid the whole of it. The entry in the books showed there had been a division of the cost and a proportionate part had been charged in R. A. Naylor's books against Blyth and Platt. So far from stealing the £4OO, it was sent to Mr. Henshall on the express understanding that it should be his money. It was made payable to him. No one else in the world could have cashed it. Mr. S mitt asked the Court to put aside the position the prisoner was in with regard to the other charges, and approach the case as if they had never beard of Louis Fienshall or R. A. Naylor before they came into Court. Could anyone in the world then believe that there would be the slightest hesitation about saying "This charge is dismissed?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>COMMITTED FOR TRIAL</head>
        <p>COMMITTED FOR TRIAL</p>
        <p>After deliberating in private the ma3istrates decided that prisoner must go for trial at the Assizes on both counts.</p>
        <p>Having been formally cautioned, Mr. Halashall replied "Not guilty." Mr. Hewart : I gathered on the question of proceed- ng with further charges the justices had formed an Opinion, ard,on the part of the prosecution,if the justices think it right to express an opinion,the prosecution will abide by it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We express the opinion that the case has gone sufficiently far here. Mr. Hewart : In that case, we shall not launch any further charges. The Chairman: You can prefer what charges yoll like at Liverpool. Mr. Rowan : Theoretically that is so, but it is most unusual.</p>
        <p>BAIL REFUSED. Mr. Swift said that now the justices had investigated all the charges, he asked them to allow the prisoner out on bail until his trial took place. The Clerk : They cannot do it, after last week. Bail was refused until the Assizes, in the other cases. Even if they granted bail on this charge, they could not on last week's.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : So far as I know, there is no reason why bail should not be granted at any time. The Clerk : He is already committed without bail on last week's charges. Mr. Swift: Having been committed without bail, there is no reason in the world why later on he should not be admitted to bail. It is the commonest thing. The Clerk: This is not the same Bench as lest week. Mr. Swift: You mean I must go to the same Bench who dealt with him last week or to a higher Court. Then I would ask the B3nch with regard to this case to fix bail in case the other Bench decides to liberate him or the High Court judges orders it. The Chairman : I think the Bench have decided already that they cannot fix bail. Prisoner was accordingly committed in custody.</p>
        <p>Mancl V I</p>
