      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-30_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SH AK f?, SATI</head>
        <p>SH AK f?, SATI</p>
        <p>In the current number of " The World "are notes from Cannes, which' chronicle some of the doings there of the Member for Warrington and his wife. We reprint the following "The Grand Duke Michael and Countess TorbY, with their family, have arrived, and are installed, as usual, at Kazbeck, a charming villa on the Californie, The Grand Duke Michael lost no time in visiting the links at Napoule, there to find Mr. John Shelton, the hon. secretary, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, the captain, and, among other players, the Grand Duke Cyril, the Grand Duchess Anastasia of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, Lady Decies, and Sir Edward Sas.soon. The annual dinner given at the Hotel Beau Site to celebrate Accession Day was a very successful function. Sir Patteson Nickel's presided, and gave the toast ; and a congratulatory telegram was sent from the corn- *pany, which included officers of the American Navy's ships at present in Riviera waters. The menu, a triumph of skill, was as follows :</p>
        <p>'Nitres Marennes vertes. Consomm6 Roi Edouard VII. Turbot a la Britannia. Baron d'Agneau h la Windsor. Volaile h la Derby. Asperges d'Argenteuil—Sauce Esp‘rance. Faisan d'Ecosse—alade des Iles. Timbale d'Ananas—Sauce Fruits. Mousse de Glace Islandaise. Gateau Union. Paillettes an Parmesan. Fruits—Dessert. The Grand Duke Michael presided at the first Golf Club dinner, which was given at the Union Club on Saturday, when there was a score or more covers; and among these who welcomed the Grand Duke Michael were the Grand Duke Cyril, Sir Edward Sassoon, Sir John Dickson-Poynder, Mr. A. a Crosfield, and Mr. John Shelton. Nice vanquished Cannes in the inter-club lawn.. tencis match played last week at Cannes. Mrs. A. Crosfield and Major Puckle were winners in the Singles,thereby beating the two strongest Nice players in their respective sets. The Grand Duchess Anastasiti of Meckleeburg-Schwerin (hon. president of the club) was an interested spectator, and so were Count- and Countess Szechenyi. The Grand Duke Cyril is a constant player, and is taking lessons again from Burke, the club's professional. A charming luncheon was served at the Hotel de la Plage, when the bon. secretary of the Cannes Club, in the absence of Mr. C. J. Letts (the president ; gave the toast of "The Visitors," and called upon Mr. A. IT. Crosfied, captain of the Cannes Golf Club, who spoke of the importance of inter-club matches, whether in golf, tennis, or croquet, in extending friendly rivalry in the Riviera world of sport. Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Tennent's guests at dinner at Les Batnly,us were Sir Edward Sasson, Sir Jobn Dickson-Poynder, Mr. and Mrs. Singer, Mr. de Glos, Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, and M. and Mme- Marchetti. Lord and Lady Cheylesmore's luncheon guests on Sunday at the Casino Municipal were Mrs. E. French Vanderbilt, Mr. Stillman, Mr. and Mrs. W- Rockfeller, and Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield. Another luncheon party at the Casino was given by Mr. and Mrs. William Gibson, whose guests were Mr. and Mrs: McKenna and Mrs. and Miss Wilson. Sir Edward Sassoon was also entertaining. Edith Lady' Winchilsea and Dowager Lady Acton have arrived.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be. held on Tuesday evening next at six o'clock. There was a meeting of the General Purposes Conr mitten on Monday, and on the minutes for confirmation by the Council is the following reference to the nen' Warring-ton Bridge : The following proceedings of the Bridges Sub-Com- mittee were read and approved "A representative from Messrs. L. G. Mouchel and Partners attended and -explained the system of re-enforced concrete, patented by the firm, and furnished the Committee with valuable information with refer- ence to the model of construction and the cost of a new ferro-concrete bridge. Resolved, that Mr. J. Webster be informed that if he desires to iutervievt the Committee with regard to the proposed new bridge,- the Committee would give him an opportunity of expressing his views." The General Purposes Committee also considered a letter from the County Bridgemaster with reference t.cr Sankey Bridge requesting representatives of the Council to attend a meeting to be held in Manchester on the 2nd February.—Resolved, that Mr. Alderman Tinnion and Dir. Councillor Wilkinson with the Borough Surveyor be appointed to represent the Corporation.</p>
        <p>LESS CRIME IN LANCASHIRE. A marked diminution in crime in the administrative. county is recorded in the report of Colonel H. AI Moorsozn, Chief Constable of Lancashire, for the quarter ended December 31st, 100 g. The apprehensions numbered 2,614, or 86 fewer than in the preceding quarter, and four fewer than in the corresponding quarter of 1907. The decrease was in drunkenness and vagrancy. The number of summonses issued by the police was 3,116, or 414 below that of tho preceding quarter, but 118 more than in the corresponding period of last year. The decrease was in vagrancy and high- way offences, and the increase in the latterand offenceS in relation to dogs. The summonses by parish authorities and others numbered 3.136, a decrease of 323 on the preceding quarter, and 550 on the corres- ponding period of 1907. The decline was in rateS, summonses, malicious damage, poor law offences and assaults.</p>
        <p>An interesting debate took place in the Essex Hall. London, on the taxation of land values, in the course of which Mr. E. G. Ilemmerde, M.P.,said a capital tax of a penny in the pound would produce about £31,000,000,</p>
        <p>Births, Marriages, and Deaths. All announcements of Births, Marriages, and Des 09 are charged at the rate oflls. 6d. for not more than three lines, and Gd. for each additional line.</p>
        <p>" W.G.P.C." My word those initials stand for P potentiality ; you know what they represent? Yes, i&amp; is a query that all can answer, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure is known the world over as the finest cough medicine ever compounded. Have a try for oqe of those 82 prizes given for three verses, £250 In all.—Advt.</p>
