natiou. If you will look at last candies and other small gifts, encouraged her to week's paper you will notice that I offered come; and at this time, although the office was . 2s. Gd. for the best letter received written on crowded with clients, he had called her to his ~.. the subject of "I-low to Make People Happy." side and given her a seat upon his knee, where I hope to receive a 'lumber of letters, as I am she remained unusually quiet, until a pause in . sure that all of you if asked the way that your the conversation had given her an opportunity ' friends could make YOU happy would not have for the question which seemed uppermost ln much hesital ion in answering. If this is the her mind. s ' case it would not be much effort for you to "Say do you?" she persisted, as he hesitated, , put it into a letter and send me. I shall visibly embarrassed. Although he was now an . publish the best received, but my readers must open follower of infidel doctrine, he had in his . not imagine that all the senders of letters boyhood a Christian home, and, somehow, he printed Will receive prizes. Do you know I could scarcely have explained why,, he was . . thin; many children arc not doing as much as ashamed to meet the honest blue eyes of that •, !he .. could ia the matter of being kind to th ir five-year-old child, and frankly acknowledge . , ~, friends. Why not try each day to do some that he did not pray. -tittle action to help one another? If you have "Do you?" he inquired at last, desperately, i:.. to put yourself out just a little bit, what does it hoping thus to change the, to him, very ' matter, providing you are doing something for obnoxious subject, for there was an unmistak, . your brothers or sisters, or perhaps parents? able smile showing upon the faces of his Think this over, my dear children. visitors at his evident unwillingness to answer. With love, "Oh, yes, of course! " she answered, promptly, UNCLE NED. not in the least disturbed by the question; . .: "once every night and morning, besides lots .. of other times. But say, do you? You know— you did not tell. me." .. "Didn't I ? "—the colour actually crimsoning his forehead. " Well, I think I might say, 'God bless my little child.' Do you think that . would do?" ' , "Yes, sir," naively, "but I think you would ~ much better pray for yourself." " I could have stood up and faced and an- . swered the most scathing rebuke ever levelled . at infidelity in public," he said, in repeating the story afterwards to a company of friends. " I could have laughed in the face of the most, eminent divine who dared to urge upon me the . - duty of prayer; but that child's question - - completely nonplussed me. I believe, in all „ the defeats I ever experienced, I never felt so unspeakably little as I did that day." ._ —4— SMALL AND EARLY. BY TUDOR JENKS. When Dorothy and I took tea, we sat upon the floor, No matter how much tea I drank, she always . gave me more; . Our table was the scarlet box in which her tea-set came, :T Our guests, an armless, one-eyed doll, a wooden horse gone lame. She poured out nothing, very fast,—the teapot tipped on high,— And in the bowl found sugar lumps unseen by my dull eye. She added rich (pretended) cream—it seemed a wilful waste, For though she overflowed the cup, it did not change the taste. She asked, "Take milk?" or "Sugar?" and though I answered, "No," She put them in, and told me that I "musi take it so!" •- She'd say, "Another cup, Papa?" and I, "No, , thank you, Ma'am," But then I had to take it—her courtesy was sham. Still, being neither green, nor black, nor . ,c English-breakfast tea, It did not give her guests the "nerves"— whatever those may be. , • Though often I upset my cup, she only minded when I would mistake the empty cups for those she'd • filled again. , • She tasted my cup gingerly, for fear I'd burn ~ my tongue; .. Indeed, she really hurt my pride—she made ... me feel so young. _. I must have drank some two-score . cups, and . Dorothy sixteen, . Allowing only needful time to pour them in ~ between. .. We stirred with massive pewter spoons, and ' sipped with courtly ease, . ' With all the ceremony of the stately Japanese. At length she put the cups away. 'Good night, Papa," she said; • And I went to a real tea, and Dorothy to bed. .</p>
