        <p>RU GBY FOOTBA LL. INTERNATIONAL TEST MATCH. G. T. P.* G. T. P. *NORTHERN UXION 3 3 15 AUSTRALIANS 1 1 5 Played at Newcastle-on-Tyne. DIE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. ITunslet *Bradford 1 6 20 York *Wigan *Widnes *Keighley *Runcorn *Dewsbury 6 8 36 Barrow *Huddersfield *Oldham *Hull Kingston H. 5 9 37 Swinton *Halifax *Warrington 3 3 15 St. Ilelens *Broughton R 0 1 3 Hull Mid Rhondda Warrington's Fine Victory, Warrington played their second match at St. Helens this season on Saturday, and again recorded a handsome victory. The first match between the two clubs was a ! Lancashire Cup tie, Saturchy's engagement being a ! League match. Both sides were well represented, ' though Warrington had Brooks and O'Neill away. Fine weather prevailed, and a very fast game was ; witnessed notwithstanding a heavy ground. There were 5.000 spectators. Teams : St. Helens—T. Barton, back • A. F. Kelly, J. Green- wood, H. Greenwood. and 1. Harris' three-quarter ; backs ; Hill and Tren Nitb, half backs ; Briers, Bradley, • Frescott, Bed worth, Welsby, and Lee, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, back; Fish, Dickenson, Ike Taylor, I and Trehearne, three-quarter backs; Jenkins and Lloyd, i half backs; Thomas A. Boardman, P. Boardman,l Dowell, Naylor, and Belton, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. Mainwaring, Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. 11. Ball, Wigan, and J. Hayes, Leigh. tvarrington started in very aggressive style, and were soon in the neighbourhood of the St. Helens line, Lloyd broke away smartly and was only pushed in touch at the corner. Smart combination between Jenkins, Dickenson and Trehearne caused St. Helens trouble, and it required all the home backs' resources to save. An attempt to relieve by Trenwith was frustrated and then Dickenson cleverly eluded several players and kicked across the line, but I. ish unfortun- ately overran the ball. Warrington continued to attack cleverly and after good handling Taylor made an open- ing for Treherne, who put on the try ia the corner. Fish failed at the place kick. The home forwards next had a look in, and for a time the Warring- ton goal was jeopardized, and Kelly eventu- ally got over, but there had been a palpable infringements, and the try was promptly negatived. The Warrington backs were next seen at their best, for after Jenkins and Lloyd had exchanged, Taylor and Dickenson broke through and served Fish who raced round the posts. He also placed the goal from an easy position. Warrington were now playing fine foot- ball and a great run by Taylor came near to accom- plishing its desired object. The attack was maintained and Se. Helens kicking weakly when hard pressed Naylor marked in a good position, for Thomas to place the " Wire-pullers ' ten points ahead. St. Helens effected another capital rally and playing the kick-and- rush game Tilley failed to take the ball and Lee was placed in the position to score an easy try. J. Green- wood failed at the place-kick. St. Helens again attacked and Kelly cross-kicking, J. Greenwood scored, hut the point was not improved upon. Half-time : Warrington, two goals two tries (ten points) ; St. Helens, two tries (six points). On resuming, the home side made great efforts to equalise, but Warrington came nearest to scoring through Dickinson and Treharne. By vigorous forward work St. Helens reached the Warrington line and Tilley was hard put to keep his lines intact. Passing between the two Greenwoods and Prescot, ended in a try to the forward, so that with the failure at the goal kick St. Helens were within a point of Warrington's score. A risky cross-kick by Hill towards Kelly was intercepted by Fish, who fielded splendidly, and outpassing the opposition succeeded in scoring again near the goal, amidst loud cheers, these being renewed when he kicked the goal and made Warrington's victory all the more pronounced. Result : Warrington, three goals and three tries (l 5 points) ; St. Helens, three tries (nine points).</p>
