        <p>LESS CRIME IN LANCASHIRE. A marked diminution in crime in the administrative county is recorded in the report of Colonel H. M Moorsoar, Chief Constable of Lancashire, for the quarter ended December 31st, 1908. The apprehensions numbered 2,614, or 86 fewer than in the preceding quarter, and four fewer than in the corresponding quarter of 1907. The decrease was in drunkenness and vagrancy. The number of summonses issued by the police was 3,116, or 414 below that of the preceding quarter, but 118 more than in the corresponling period of last year. The decrease was in vagrancy and high- way offences, and the increase in the latter and offences in relation to dogs. The summonses by parish authorities and others numbered 3,136, a decrease of 323 on the preceding quarter, and 550 on the corres- ponding period of 1907. The decline was in rates, summonses, malicious damage, pgor law offences and assaults. An interesting debate took place in the Essex Hall. London, on the taxation of land values, in the course of which Mr. E. G. Hemmerde, 11. P., said a capital tax of a penny in the pound would produce about £31,000,000,</p>
